window.PREVIEW_MOCKS = window.PREVIEW_MOCKS || {};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["cip-2023"] = {"id": "cip-2023", "title": "CIP Ranchi 2023: recall set", "fullTitle": "CIP Ranchi 2023, built from the topics students recalled after the exam (not the real paper)", "sub": "79 Qs, one per recalled CIP 2023 topic · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "How many items does the MMPI-2-RF (Restructured Form, 2008) contain?", "options": {"a": "567", "b": "338", "c": "478", "d": "335"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the MMPI-2-RF contains 338 items. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory began with Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), who built its scales by empirical keying: an item was kept if patients in a diagnostic group answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The revision by James Butcher and colleagues (1989), the MMPI-2, has 567 true-false items. Its old clinical scales overlap heavily because they share a general distress factor, which Auke Tellegen and colleagues (2003) called demoralisation. They removed it and built the Restructured Clinical (RC) scales, such as RC2 (low positive emotions) and RC4 (antisocial behaviour). Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen (2008) then chose the 338 MMPI-2 items needed to score the new scales and published them as the MMPI-2-RF (Restructured Form). It is shorter and faster, yet keeps validity scales that check how the person answered: random or fixed responding, over-reporting and under-reporting. Example: a client who marks \"true\" to almost every item raises TRIN-r, warning that the clinical profile cannot be trusted. Option b is right because 338 is the published item count of the MMPI-2-RF. Exam trap: all four numbers belong to the same family; 567 is the MMPI-2, 478 the MMPI-A for adolescents (1992) and 335 the MMPI-3 (2020). Key note: the recall list remembered this item as \"338 items and 4 validity scales\". The 338 is right, but the MMPI-2-RF has nine validity scales: eight in 2008 (VRIN-r, TRIN-r, F-r, Fp-r, Fs, FBS-r, L-r, K-r) plus RBS, added in 2011.", "topic": "MMPI-2-RF item count"}, {"q": "In a one-way ANOVA, the null hypothesis that all group means are equal is rejected only when the F ratio is:", "options": {"a": "far below 1, in the left tail of the F distribution", "b": "negative", "c": "large, in the right (upper) tail of the F distribution", "d": "either very small or very large, in both tails"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: in a one-way ANOVA the null hypothesis is rejected only when F is large, in the right tail of the F distribution. Analysis of variance was developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, and George Snedecor named the F ratio after him in 1934. ANOVA asks whether three or more group means differ by splitting the total variability into two parts. The between-groups mean square measures how far the group means spread around the grand mean; the within-groups mean square measures ordinary error inside each group. F is their ratio: MS between divided by MS within. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance, so F hovers around 1. If the means really differ, the numerator grows and F rises well above 1. Because both mean squares are variances, F can never be negative. Example: three teaching methods give mean scores of 50, 60 and 70 with little spread inside each class of ten. MS between is large, F might be 9.0, and with 2 and 27 degrees of freedom that far exceeds the 0.05 critical value of about 3.35, so H0 is rejected. Option c is right because only a big F shows that group means differ more than chance allows. Exam trap: an F far below 1 is not evidence of a difference; it only means the group means are unusually close together. Key note: the recall list remembers this item as \"the F test is essentially a two tailed test\". ANOVA's alternative hypothesis is non-directional, which is why some books loosely call it two-tailed in spirit, but any difference among means makes F bigger, so the rejection region lies only in the right tail. See also the next F question in this set.", "topic": "F ratio and the right-tail rejection region in ANOVA"}, {"q": "After both medial temporal lobes were removed in 1953, patient H.M. could no longer form new long-term declarative memories. The structure mainly responsible for this loss was the:", "options": {"a": "hippocampus", "b": "amygdala", "c": "cerebellum", "d": "basal ganglia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the hippocampus was mainly responsible for H.M.'s loss of new long-term declarative memory. Henry Molaison (1926 to 2008), known in the literature as H.M., had severe epilepsy. In 1953 the neurosurgeon William Scoville removed the medial temporal lobes on both sides, including most of the hippocampus, the amygdala and nearby cortex. The seizures came under control, but H.M. was left with profound anterograde amnesia: he could not form new conscious memories of facts or events. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) described the case, and Milner went on to study him for years. His short-term memory was normal, so he could hold a number in mind by rehearsing it, but once distracted the information was gone. Many older memories survived, showing that the hippocampus is needed to consolidate new declarative memories rather than to store all old ones. Example: H.M. could meet the same researcher many times and still treat each visit as a first meeting. Yet Milner taught him mirror drawing, tracing a star seen only in a mirror, and he improved day after day while saying he had never done the task before. That spared procedural learning depends on other circuits. Option a is right because the hippocampus consolidates new facts and events into long-term memory, and its loss caused his amnesia. Exam trap: the amygdala was removed too, but it handles emotional memory and fear learning, not the general laying down of new facts and events.", "topic": "H.M. and the hippocampus in declarative memory"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a subtest of the Performance Scale of the classic Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS and WAIS-R)?", "options": {"a": "Digit Span", "b": "Picture Arrangement", "c": "Block Design", "d": "Object Assembly"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: Digit Span is not a Performance subtest of the classic WAIS and WAIS-R; it belongs to the Verbal Scale. David Wechsler (1896 to 1981), chief psychologist at Bellevue Hospital in New York, published the Wechsler-Bellevue scale in 1939, the WAIS in 1955 and the WAIS-R in 1981. Unlike the early Stanford-Binet, his scales gave separate Verbal and Performance IQs as well as a Full Scale IQ, and used the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15). The Verbal Scale had six subtests: Information, Comprehension, Arithmetic, Similarities, Digit Span and Vocabulary. The Performance Scale had five: Digit Symbol, Picture Completion, Block Design, Picture Arrangement and Object Assembly. Verbal subtests are given and answered in words; Performance subtests need the person to arrange, assemble or copy materials, often against the clock. Example: in Digit Span the examiner reads 7-2-9-4 and the person repeats it forwards or backwards. Nothing is handled, so it is a verbal task of attention and working memory. In Block Design, by contrast, the person copies a red and white pattern with cubes. Option a is right because Digit Span sat on the Verbal side, while the other three were Performance subtests. Exam trap: later editions changed the structure. The WAIS-III (1997) kept the Verbal and Performance IQs but added four index scores, and the WAIS-IV (2008) dropped the Verbal and Performance IQs altogether. Digit Span now counts towards the Working Memory Index.", "topic": "Classic WAIS Verbal and Performance subtests"}, {"q": "Which of these theories of intelligence was built on factor analysis?", "options": {"a": "Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences", "b": "Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory", "c": "Jean Piaget's theory of cognitive development", "d": "Louis Thurstone's primary mental abilities"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Louis Thurstone's primary mental abilities theory was built on factor analysis. Factor analysis is a statistical method that examines how many test scores correlate with one another and finds a smaller number of underlying factors that explain those correlations. Charles Spearman (1904) used an early form of it to argue for a general factor, g, plus specific factors, s. Louis Thurstone (1938) developed multiple factor analysis, gave a large battery of tests to college students, and found about seven separate abilities rather than one dominant g. His primary mental abilities are verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning. Later factor-analytic models followed the same path: J. P. Guilford's structure of intellect, Raymond Cattell and John Horn's fluid and crystallised intelligence, and John Carroll's three-stratum theory (1993). Example: if vocabulary and reading tests correlate highly with each other but only weakly with mental rotation tests, factor analysis separates a verbal factor from a spatial factor. Option d is right because Thurstone's seven abilities came directly out of factor analysing test scores. Exam trap: Gardner and Sternberg also describe several kinds of intelligence, so they sound similar, but neither derived their list from factor analysis. Gardner used criteria such as the effects of brain damage, savants and cultural evidence, and Sternberg used an information-processing view.", "topic": "Thurstone's factor-analytic primary mental abilities"}, {"q": "Solomon Asch's line-judgement studies and Stanley Milgram's 'shock' studies are the classic demonstrations of, respectively:", "options": {"a": "obedience and conformity", "b": "compliance and conformity", "c": "conformity and obedience", "d": "group polarisation and obedience"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Asch's line studies demonstrate conformity and Milgram's shock studies demonstrate obedience. Social influence comes in three classic kinds. Conformity means changing behaviour or belief to match a group, even though no one asks you to. Compliance means agreeing to a direct request from someone without special authority. Obedience means following an order from an authority figure. Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) showed groups a standard line and three comparison lines and asked which one matched. Only one member was a real participant; the rest were confederates who all gave the same wrong answer on critical trials. Real participants went along with the wrong majority on about 37% of critical trials, and about 75% conformed at least once, even though the right answer was obvious. Stanley Milgram (1963) at Yale told participants, acting as \"teachers\", to give a \"learner\" shocks rising in 15-volt steps for each mistake. The shocks were fake, but the participants did not know that. When they hesitated, the experimenter said things like \"Please continue\". 65% went all the way to 450 volts. Example: copying classmates who all stand up for a song is conformity; standing because the principal orders it is obedience. Option c is right because Asch's pressure came from peers and Milgram's from an authority, in that order. Exam trap: the question says \"respectively\", so the order matters; option a names the right two ideas in reverse order.", "topic": "Conformity (Asch) versus obedience (Milgram)"}, {"q": "Indian textbooks credit the 'unifactor' (monarchic) theory, which treats intelligence as one single, undivided capacity, to:", "options": {"a": "Alfred Binet", "b": "Charles Spearman", "c": "Edward Thorndike", "d": "Louis Thurstone"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Indian textbooks credit the unifactor (monarchic) theory to Alfred Binet. Many Indian psychology and education books sort the early theories of intelligence by how many factors they allow. The unifactor or monarchic theory treats intelligence as one single, undivided power that rules all mental activity, as a monarch rules a kingdom. It is credited to Alfred Binet, who with Théodore Simon built the first practical intelligence test (1905), and is often linked with Lewis Terman and William Stern. On this view each person has a fixed total amount of this one capacity, used equally in every task. The two-factor theory belongs to Charles Spearman (1904): a general factor g plus a specific factor s for each test. The multifactor or anarchic theory belongs to Edward Thorndike: intelligence is many separate abilities with no single ruler. The group factor or oligarchic theory belongs to Louis Thurstone: a few broad primary abilities share power, like a small ruling group. Example: on the monarchic view, a child who does well in arithmetic should do equally well in language and drawing, because one power runs all three. Option a is right because this scheme gives the monarchic label to Binet's view of intelligence as a single, unified capacity. Exam trap: Spearman's g sounds like a \"single factor\", but his theory is the two-factor theory, g plus s, so in this classification it is not the unifactor theory.", "topic": "Unifactor (monarchic) theory of intelligence"}, {"q": "The first woman to be officially awarded a PhD in psychology (Cornell University, 1894) was:", "options": {"a": "Mary Whiton Calkins", "b": "Margaret Floy Washburn", "c": "Christine Ladd-Franklin", "d": "Leta Stetter Hollingworth"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Margaret Floy Washburn was the first woman officially awarded a PhD in psychology, by Cornell University in 1894. In the late nineteenth century many universities let women attend classes but would not grant them doctorates, so several women did doctoral-level work without receiving the degree. Washburn (1871 to 1939) first hoped to study with James McKeen Cattell at Columbia, which would admit her only as an unofficial \"hearer\". She moved to Cornell, where Edward Bradford Titchener, the leading structuralist in America, supervised her. Her doctoral thesis was on tactual perception, and Wilhelm Wundt published it in his journal Philosophische Studien. She then taught at Vassar College for decades, wrote The Animal Mind (1908), an influential textbook of comparative psychology, and in 1921 became the second woman president of the American Psychological Association (APA). Example of the contrast: Mary Whiton Calkins finished every requirement for a Harvard PhD in 1895, yet Harvard refused to award the degree to a woman, so the first official psychology doctorate for a woman remains Washburn's. Option b is right because Washburn's 1894 Cornell degree was the first doctorate in psychology actually awarded to a woman. Exam trap: Calkins is famous as the first woman APA president (1905), so students often pick her here; earning a degree in practice and being officially awarded it are different things, and the question asks about the official award.", "topic": "First woman PhD in psychology: Washburn"}, {"q": "The term 'meme', for a unit of cultural information (an idea, tune or habit) that spreads from person to person by imitation, was coined by:", "options": {"a": "Richard Dawkins", "b": "Edward O. Wilson", "c": "Lev Vygotsky", "d": "Albert Bandura"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term \"meme\" was coined by Richard Dawkins. Dawkins, a British evolutionary biologist, introduced the word in The Selfish Gene (1976). The book argued that the gene is the real unit of natural selection, and in a late chapter he suggested that culture has a replicator of its own. He took the Greek mimeme, \"something imitated\", and shortened it to rhyme with gene. A meme is any unit of cultural information that is copied from mind to mind: a tune, a catchphrase, a fashion, a recipe or a ritual. Like genes, memes vary, are copied (mostly by imitation) and are selected: the catchier or more useful ones spread, and the rest die out. This view treats culture as an evolutionary process in its own right, running far faster than genetic change. Example: a dance step from a film song is copied by thousands of people at weddings within months, often changing slightly as it spreads; on Dawkins's view it is a meme competing with other dance steps for attention and imitation. Internet memes borrowed the word much later. Option a is right because Dawkins coined the word in 1976 by analogy with gene. Exam trap: Bandura's observational learning explains how a habit is copied, and Vygotsky stressed cultural tools, so both sound related, but neither coined the term.", "topic": "Dawkins and the meme"}, {"q": "A test gives nearly the same scores when the same people take it again two weeks later. This shows that the test has high:", "options": {"a": "predictive validity", "b": "content validity", "c": "construct validity", "d": "test-retest reliability"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: nearly identical scores two weeks apart show high test-retest reliability. Reliability is the consistency of a measurement. In classical test theory, which grew from the work of Charles Spearman (1904), every observed score is a true score plus random error, and reliability is the proportion of score variance that is true variance. Test-retest reliability is estimated by giving the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores. The correlation is called the coefficient of stability, because it shows how stable scores are over time. The time gap matters. If it is too short, people remember their answers (carry-over) or improve with practice, which inflates the coefficient. If it is too long, the trait itself may truly change, which lowers it. So a test manual should always report the interval. Other forms answer different questions: parallel (alternate) forms reliability correlates two equivalent versions; internal consistency (split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach's alpha) checks whether the items in one sitting hang together; inter-rater reliability checks agreement between scorers. Example: 50 students take a numerical reasoning test in June and again two weeks later, and the two sets of scores correlate 0.88, so the test is stable. Option d is right because consistency of scores across two occasions is exactly what test-retest reliability measures. Exam trap: consistency is reliability, not validity; high stability says nothing about whether the test measures what it claims to.", "topic": "Test-retest reliability"}, {"q": "Which statement about reliability and validity is correct?", "options": {"a": "A test can be valid without being reliable", "b": "A reliable test is always valid", "c": "Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity", "d": "Reliability tells us whether a test measures what it claims to measure"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same result each time? Validity is accuracy of meaning: does the test measure what it claims to measure? The two are linked in one direction only. A test full of random error cannot correlate well with anything, so an unreliable test cannot be valid. But a test can be perfectly consistent and still measure the wrong thing. Classical test theory makes this exact, building on the correction for attenuation of Charles Spearman (1904). The highest validity coefficient a test can reach is the square root of its reliability coefficient, called the index of reliability. If reliability is 0.64, validity cannot exceed 0.80; if reliability is 0.25, validity cannot exceed 0.50. Raising reliability raises the ceiling on validity, but it does not guarantee that validity reaches that ceiling. Example: a bathroom scale that always shows you 5 kg heavier than you are is reliable, because it gives the same reading every time, but it is not valid, because the reading is wrong. A scale whose reading jumps around by several kilos is neither reliable nor valid. Option c is right because reliability sets the upper limit on validity without ensuring it. Exam trap: option b reverses the logic; \"a valid test must be reliable\" is true, but \"a reliable test must be valid\" is false.", "topic": "Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity"}, {"q": "A new anxiety scale correlates highly with an established anxiety scale and only weakly with a vocabulary test. This pattern is evidence of its:", "options": {"a": "face validity", "b": "predictive validity", "c": "split-half reliability", "d": "convergent and discriminant (construct) validity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: high correlation with another anxiety scale and low correlation with a vocabulary test is evidence of convergent and discriminant (construct) validity. Construct validity is the degree to which a test measures the theoretical trait, or construct, it claims to measure. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) set out the idea: because a construct such as anxiety cannot be seen directly, we gather evidence that test scores behave as the theory predicts. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) added two key kinds of evidence in their multitrait-multimethod matrix. Convergent validity means the test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct. Discriminant (or divergent) validity means it correlates weakly with measures of different constructs. Both are needed: a new anxiety scale that correlated highly with everything, including vocabulary, might really be measuring reading ability or general test-taking skill. In the matrix, several traits are each measured by several methods, such as self-report, peer rating and interview, and same-trait correlations should be higher than different-trait ones. Example: a new depression inventory should correlate strongly with the Beck Depression Inventory but only weakly with a spelling test. Option d is right because high correlation with a similar measure plus low correlation with an unrelated one is exactly convergent and discriminant evidence. Exam trap: students notice only the high correlation, but the low correlation with vocabulary is the discriminant half and matters just as much.", "topic": "Convergent and discriminant validity"}, {"q": "A 60-year-old man insists that his intestines have rotted and that he is already dead. He wakes at 3 a.m. every day and cannot fall asleep again, and he has lost a lot of weight. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": {"a": "schizophrenia", "b": "delusional disorder, somatic type", "c": "severe major depressive disorder with psychotic features", "d": "generalised anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: severe major depressive disorder with psychotic features is the most likely diagnosis. Major depression is marked by low mood or loss of interest for at least two weeks, plus symptoms such as sleep change, appetite or weight change, guilt and suicidal thoughts. In severe cases delusions appear. When their content matches the depressed mood, such as guilt, poverty, disease, punishment or death, they are called mood-congruent. The most extreme is the nihilistic delusion: the belief that one's organs have rotted, the world has ended, or one is already dead. Jules Cotard (1880) described this picture, now called Cotard's syndrome, and it is seen mostly in older patients with severe depression. Waking around 3 a.m. and being unable to sleep again is early morning awakening (terminal insomnia). Together with marked weight loss it points to melancholic features, the biological pattern of depression that is often worse in the morning. Older textbooks called this kind of late-life depression involutional melancholia. Applying it: this man has a depressive syndrome (insomnia, weight loss) and a delusion that fits it (decay and death), so the delusion is part of the mood disorder. Option c is right because nihilistic, mood-congruent delusions plus melancholic biological symptoms define psychotic depression. Exam trap: any delusion tempts students towards schizophrenia, but when the delusions match a dominant depressive syndrome and arise within it, the diagnosis is a mood disorder with psychotic features.", "topic": "Psychotic depression with nihilistic (Cotard) delusions"}, {"q": "Which of these is a first-line, evidence-based psychological treatment for post-traumatic stress disorder?", "options": {"a": "Free association", "b": "Prolonged exposure therapy", "c": "Token economy", "d": "Aversion therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: prolonged exposure therapy is a first-line, evidence-based psychological treatment for PTSD. Post-traumatic stress disorder follows a terrifying event and brings intrusive memories and nightmares, avoidance of reminders, negative changes in mood and beliefs, and hyperarousal. Avoidance keeps the disorder going: the person never stays with the memory long enough to learn that it is a memory, not a present danger. Prolonged exposure (PE) was developed by Edna Foa, based on the emotional processing theory of Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986). It has two parts. In imaginal exposure the client repeatedly retells the trauma aloud in the present tense, and the session is recorded so they can listen again at home. In in vivo exposure the client gradually approaches safe situations they have been avoiding. Fear rises, then falls with repetition, and the fear memory is corrected. Other first-line treatments include cognitive processing therapy (Patricia Resick), trauma-focused CBT, and eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR, Francine Shapiro, 1987). NICE and American Psychological Association guidelines recommend these trauma-focused therapies. Example: a woman assaulted in a car park recounts the attack in every session and then practises parking in busy, safe car parks until her fear drops. Option b is right because PE has strong support from randomised trials in PTSD. Exam trap: aversion therapy also makes the client face something unpleasant, but it pairs an unwanted behaviour with discomfort to create a new aversion, while PE removes an existing fear.", "topic": "Prolonged exposure therapy for PTSD"}, {"q": "In a moderately skewed distribution the mean is 62 and the median is 65. Using Karl Pearson's empirical formula, the mode is approximately:", "options": {"a": "63.5", "b": "68", "c": "59", "d": "71"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the mode is approximately 71. Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936), the British statistician who gave us the product-moment correlation, noticed that in a moderately skewed, single-peaked distribution the three averages keep a steady spacing. The median lies about two-thirds of the way from the mode to the mean, so the distance from mean to mode is about three times the distance from mean to median. As a formula: Mean minus Mode = 3 (Mean minus Median). Rearranged: Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean. This empirical relation is only approximate, but it lets you estimate the mode when the raw data are not given. Working: 3 x 65 = 195; 2 x 62 = 124; 195 minus 124 = 71. Check the order: mean 62, median 65, mode 71. The mean is lowest because it is pulled towards a long tail of low scores, so the distribution is negatively (left) skewed, like marks on a very easy test where most students score high and a few score low. Pearson's coefficient of skewness, 3 (Mean minus Median) divided by SD, is also negative here. Option d is right because 3 x 65 minus 2 x 62 gives 71. Exam trap: students often swap the multipliers and compute 3 Mean minus 2 Median, which gives 56, or forget that in a negative skew the mode must lie above the median, not between the mean and median.", "topic": "Pearson's empirical formula for the mode"}, {"q": "A between-subjects 3 x 3 x 2 factorial experiment has 10 participants in each group. How many participants are needed in total?", "options": {"a": "80", "b": "180", "c": "18", "d": "90"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: 180 participants are needed in total. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at the same time, crossing every level of each factor with every level of the others. Ronald Fisher made the method popular in agricultural research and described it in The Design of Experiments (1935). The notation tells you the structure: each number is one factor, and its value is that factor's number of levels. So 3 x 3 x 2 means three factors, with 3, 3 and 2 levels. The number of conditions, or cells, is found by multiplying the levels: 3 x 3 x 2 = 18 cells. In a between-subjects design each participant serves in only one cell, so the total sample is cells times participants per cell: 18 x 10 = 180. Example: a study crosses therapy type (CBT, interpersonal therapy, waiting list), session length (30, 45 or 60 minutes) and homework (given or not given). One cell is \"CBT in 45-minute sessions with homework\", and it needs its own ten people. Factorial designs let you test each main effect and also interactions, for instance whether homework helps only when sessions are short. Option b is right because 18 cells with 10 participants each gives 180. Exam trap: 18 is the number of cells, not people. In a fully within-subjects design, where the same people pass through all 18 conditions, only 10 would be needed.", "topic": "Total sample size in a between-subjects factorial design"}, {"q": "The rhombencephalon (hindbrain) develops into:", "options": {"a": "the thalamus and hypothalamus", "b": "the tectum and tegmentum", "c": "the cerebral hemispheres and basal ganglia", "d": "the medulla, pons and cerebellum"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the rhombencephalon (hindbrain) develops into the medulla, pons and cerebellum. Early in embryonic life the front end of the neural tube swells into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), the mesencephalon (midbrain) and the rhombencephalon (hindbrain). By about the fifth week two of these divide, giving five secondary vesicles. The prosencephalon splits into the telencephalon, which becomes the cerebral cortex, basal ganglia and limbic structures, and the diencephalon, which becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays single and becomes the midbrain, with its tectum (roof, holding the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum (floor). The rhombencephalon splits into the metencephalon, which becomes the pons and cerebellum, and the myelencephalon, which becomes the medulla oblongata. The name comes from the diamond (rhomboid) shape of the floor of the fourth ventricle in this region. The medulla and pons, together with the midbrain, form the brainstem, which controls breathing, heart rate and arousal. Example: a stroke in the medulla can stop breathing, and damage to the cerebellum, also a hindbrain structure, makes walking unsteady and movements clumsy. Option d is right because the metencephalon and myelencephalon, the two parts of the rhombencephalon, form exactly these three structures. Exam trap: \"mes-\" means middle and \"met-\" means after; the mesencephalon is the midbrain, while the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) is part of the hindbrain.", "topic": "Rhombencephalon and its derivatives"}, {"q": "The classic dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia holds that the positive symptoms result from:", "options": {"a": "too little dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway", "b": "too much dopamine activity, especially at D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway", "c": "too much serotonin activity in the raphe nuclei", "d": "loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the classic dopamine hypothesis holds that positive symptoms come from too much dopamine activity, especially at D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway. Positive symptoms are experiences added to normal life: delusions, hallucinations and disorganised thinking. The hypothesis grew from drug evidence. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) argued that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, blocks dopamine receptors. In 1976 Philip Seeman and colleagues, and separately Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, showed that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks how strongly they bind D2 receptors: the tighter the binding, the smaller the dose needed. Drugs that raise dopamine point the same way: high doses of amphetamine can cause a paranoid psychosis, and L-dopa given for Parkinson's disease can cause hallucinations. The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and limbic areas, and its overactivity is blamed for positive symptoms. The revised view (Kenneth Davis and colleagues, 1991; Oliver Howes and Shitij Kapur, 2009) adds that dopamine is too low in the mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex, which helps explain negative and cognitive symptoms. Example: haloperidol, a strong D2 blocker, often reduces hallucinations but does little for apathy and social withdrawal. Option b is right because positive symptoms are tied to dopamine excess, not deficit. Exam trap: low dopamine in the mesocortical pathway belongs to negative symptoms, and loss of dopamine cells in the substantia nigra belongs to Parkinson's disease.", "topic": "Dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia"}, {"q": "A mother tells her son that he can have dessert only after he finishes his vegetables. She is using:", "options": {"a": "decay conditioning", "b": "the Crespi effect", "c": "higher-order conditioning", "d": "the Premack principle"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the mother is using the Premack principle. David Premack (1959) proposed that reinforcers are best thought of as behaviours, not things. Any behaviour a person does often, when free to choose, can reinforce a behaviour they do less often. The rule is: a more probable behaviour, allowed only after a less probable one, will strengthen the less probable one. To find what will work, you watch what the person does when free to choose and rank the activities by how much time they get. Premack showed this with children: those who preferred eating sweets to playing a pinball machine played more pinball to earn sweets, while those who preferred pinball ate more sweets to earn a turn at the machine. Because parents use it naturally, it is called Grandma's rule: \"first you work, then you play\". It is widely used in behaviour therapy, classrooms and parent training. Example: a student who loves cricket but avoids homework must study for an hour before going out to play; the cricket, a high-probability activity, reinforces studying. Here the son eats dessert gladly but avoids vegetables, so making dessert depend on vegetables strengthens vegetable eating. Option d is right because a preferred activity is being used to reinforce a less preferred one. Exam trap: the Crespi effect also involves rewards, but it is about a sudden change in the size of a reward, not about using one behaviour to reinforce another.", "topic": "Premack principle"}, {"q": "A trainer teaches a pigeon to turn in a full circle by reinforcing first a slight head turn, then a quarter turn, then a half turn, and so on. This procedure is called:", "options": {"a": "chaining", "b": "shaping", "c": "fading", "d": "stimulus generalisation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: reinforcing a slight head turn, then a quarter turn, then a half turn, is shaping. Shaping is the operant technique of reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour. B. F. Skinner (1904 to 1990) developed it in the 1940s while training rats and pigeons. It solves a basic problem: you cannot reinforce a behaviour the animal never performs, and a pigeon will not spontaneously turn a full circle. So the trainer starts with something the bird already does, such as any slight turn of the head, and reinforces it with food. Then the criterion is raised: only bigger turns earn food, while smaller ones are no longer reinforced. This is differential reinforcement: closer approximations are reinforced and earlier ones extinguished. Step by step, the behaviour moves towards the goal. A click or a light paired with food (a conditioned reinforcer) is often used so that reinforcement arrives instantly, at the exact moment of the right movement. Example: a speech therapist working with a child who does not speak first reinforces any sound, then a \"b\" sound, then \"ba\", and finally the word \"ball\". Option b is right because the trainer reinforced closer and closer approximations of one response until the full circle appeared. Exam trap: chaining also builds complex behaviour step by step, but it links different responses into a sequence, while shaping gradually refines a single response.", "topic": "Shaping by successive approximations"}, {"q": "People choose a surgery described as having a '90% survival rate' more often than the same surgery described as having a '10% death rate'. This is the:", "options": {"a": "availability heuristic", "b": "anchoring effect", "c": "framing effect", "d": "sunk cost effect"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: choosing the surgery more often when it is described by a 90% survival rate than by a 10% death rate is the framing effect. The framing effect is a shift in choice when the same facts are worded differently, usually as gains or as losses. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1981) showed it with the Asian disease problem. Told that a disease will kill 600 people, most people preferred a programme that saves 200 for sure to a gamble with a one-third chance of saving all 600. When the same options were worded as 400 dying for sure versus a one-third chance that nobody dies, most chose the gamble. Their prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky, 1979) explains why: people judge outcomes as gains or losses from a reference point, and losses loom larger than equal gains (loss aversion). So people avoid risk when options are framed as gains and seek risk when they are framed as losses. Barbara McNeil and colleagues (1982) found the same in medicine: patients, students and doctors chose surgery over radiation therapy more often when results were given as survival rather than death rates. Example: shoppers rate minced beef labelled \"75% lean\" more highly than the same beef labelled \"25% fat\". Option c is right because 90% surviving and 10% dying are the same fact, and only the positive versus negative wording moved the choice. Exam trap: the percentages look like an anchor, but anchoring needs a first number that biases a later estimate, and nobody estimates anything here.", "topic": "Framing effect"}, {"q": "The MMPI-2 (1989) has how many items and how many basic clinical scales?", "options": {"a": "478 items and 10 clinical scales", "b": "338 items and 9 clinical scales", "c": "550 items and 10 clinical scales", "d": "567 items and 10 clinical scales"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the MMPI-2 has 567 items and 10 basic clinical scales. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) was built by psychologist Starke Hathaway and psychiatrist J. C. McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota. They used empirical criterion keying: an item was kept for a scale only if patients with a particular diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The original test had 550 different true-false statements (566 counting repeated items). In 1989 James Butcher and colleagues published the MMPI-2, which rewrote dated items, removed objectionable ones and collected new norms from about 2,600 American adults; it has 567 items. The 10 clinical scales kept their old numbers and names: 1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania and 0 Social Introversion. Validity scales such as L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness) check how the person approached the test, and the MMPI-2 added VRIN and TRIN to detect inconsistent or fixed responding. Scores are T-scores, and 65 or above counts as clinically raised. Example: a person whose two highest scales are 2 and 7 (a 2-7 code type) usually presents as anxious, tense and depressed. Option d is right because 567 items and ten clinical scales are the defining numbers of the MMPI-2. Exam trap: 550 belongs to the 1943 MMPI, 478 to the adolescent MMPI-A and 338 to the MMPI-2-RF, so learn each number with its version.", "topic": "MMPI-2 items and clinical scales"}, {"q": "The WAIS-IV yields a Full Scale IQ and four index scores. Which of the following is NOT one of the WAIS-IV indexes?", "options": {"a": "Fluid Reasoning Index", "b": "Perceptual Reasoning Index", "c": "Verbal Comprehension Index", "d": "Processing Speed Index"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Fluid Reasoning Index is not one of the WAIS-IV indexes. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale was created by David Wechsler (1955), building on his Wechsler-Bellevue scale of 1939, and its fourth edition, the WAIS-IV, appeared in 2008 for ages 16 to 90. The WAIS-IV dropped the old Verbal IQ and Performance IQ and reports a Full Scale IQ plus four index scores. The Verbal Comprehension Index (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information) measures word knowledge and verbal reasoning. The Perceptual Reasoning Index (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles) measures non-verbal reasoning and spatial problem solving. The Working Memory Index (Digit Span, Arithmetic) measures holding and working on information in mind. The Processing Speed Index (Symbol Search, Coding) measures quick, accurate visual scanning. These ten core subtests give the Full Scale IQ. A General Ability Index, built from the verbal and perceptual subtests only, gives an estimate less affected by memory and speed. Fluid Reasoning became a separate index in the children's WISC-V (2014), which split perceptual reasoning into Visual Spatial and Fluid Reasoning to give five primary indexes; the WAIS-5 (2024) later did the same. Example: an adult with a Verbal Comprehension Index of 115 and a Processing Speed Index of 85 has a 30-point gap that the Full Scale IQ hides. Option a is right because Fluid Reasoning is a WISC-V index, not a WAIS-IV one. Exam trap: Matrix Reasoning measures fluid ability, yet in the WAIS-IV it sits under the Perceptual Reasoning Index.", "topic": "WAIS-IV index scores"}, {"q": "A 72-year-old man is asked the date and where he is, to repeat three words, to count back from 100 in sevens, to recall the three words, to name a pencil and a watch, to follow a three-step command and to copy two intersecting pentagons. The test being given is the:", "options": {"a": "Wechsler Memory Scale", "b": "Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test", "c": "Mini-Mental State Examination", "d": "Rorschach inkblot test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the test being given is the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE). The MMSE was published by Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh (1975) as a quick bedside screen for cognitive impairment that takes about ten minutes. It is scored out of 30 across five areas. Orientation gives 10 points: five for time, such as the date, and five for place. Registration gives 3 points for repeating three words. Attention and calculation give 5 points, for counting back from 100 in sevens or spelling WORLD backwards. Recall gives 3 points for the same three words a few minutes later. Language and praxis give 9 points: naming two objects, repeating a phrase, following a three-step command, reading and obeying \"Close your eyes\", writing a sentence and copying two intersecting pentagons. The total is 10 + 3 + 5 + 3 + 9 = 30. A common cut-off is 23/24: 24 or above is usually taken as normal, and 23 or below calls for full assessment. Scores are affected by education, and the MMSE misses much mild cognitive impairment, which the Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) detects better. Example: a retired professor with early dementia may still score 27, while a man with little schooling may score 22 with no disease. Option c is right because every task in the vignette is a standard MMSE item. Exam trap: the pentagon copy may suggest the Bender test, but one copying item inside a broad screen does not make it the Bender.", "topic": "Mini-Mental State Examination"}, {"q": "A woman has had six major depressive episodes and one hypomanic episode lasting five days, but never a full manic episode. The diagnosis is:", "options": {"a": "bipolar II disorder", "b": "bipolar I disorder", "c": "cyclothymic disorder", "d": "recurrent major depressive disorder"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: six major depressive episodes plus one hypomanic episode, with no history of mania, is bipolar II disorder. The current rules come from the DSM-5-TR of the American Psychiatric Association (2022); bipolar II first entered the manual in DSM-IV (1994). A major depressive episode needs at least five of nine symptoms, including depressed mood or loss of interest, for at least two weeks. A manic episode needs abnormally elevated or irritable mood with increased energy for at least seven days (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causing marked impairment or with psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same kind of symptoms for at least four consecutive days, clear enough for others to notice, but not severe enough to cause marked impairment or hospitalisation, and without psychosis. Bipolar I needs only one manic episode, with or without depression. Bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, and never a manic one. Example, applying the rules step by step to her: her high lasted 5 days; 5 is at least 4, so it meets hypomania; 5 is less than 7 and she was not hospitalised, so it is not mania. One hypomanic episode plus six major depressive episodes, with no mania, gives bipolar II. Option a is right because her history matches the bipolar II rule exactly. Exam trap: bipolar II is not simply a milder bipolar I; its depressions are often long and disabling.", "topic": "Bipolar II disorder"}, {"q": "A factory pays its tailors 50 rupees for every 10 shirts they finish. This is an example of which schedule of reinforcement?", "options": {"a": "Fixed ratio", "b": "Fixed interval", "c": "Variable ratio", "d": "Variable interval"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: paying tailors 50 rupees for every 10 shirts they finish is a fixed ratio schedule. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses earn a reward. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) mapped these schedules in their book Schedules of Reinforcement, using pigeons and rats. Continuous reinforcement rewards every response; partial (intermittent) schedules reward only some, and they differ on two questions. First, does the reward depend on the number of responses (ratio) or on time passing (interval)? Second, is the requirement fixed or variable? Working out this case: the pay depends on output, so it is a ratio schedule; the number never changes, so it is fixed. One payment per 10 shirts is FR-10, worth 50 / 10 = 5 rupees a shirt. Fixed ratio schedules produce a high, steady rate of work with a short post-reinforcement pause just after each reward. Fixed interval schedules give a scalloped pattern: slow after a reward, fast just before the next is due. Variable ratio schedules give the highest rates and the most resistance to extinction, and variable interval schedules give slow, steady responding. Example: a tailor works quickly through ten shirts, collects the 50 rupees, takes a tea break, then speeds up again. Option a is right because payment is tied to a set count of finished shirts, not to the clock. Exam trap: the word \"every\" can suggest a regular time interval, but here it means every tenth piece of work, so it is a ratio schedule.", "topic": "Fixed ratio schedule of reinforcement"}, {"q": "The F ratio can be used to test hypotheses that are:", "options": {"a": "one-tailed only", "b": "both one-tailed and two-tailed, depending on how the hypothesis is stated", "c": "two-tailed only", "d": "neither, because the F ratio is only a descriptive statistic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the F ratio can test both one-tailed and two-tailed hypotheses, depending on how the hypothesis is stated. The F ratio is one variance divided by another. Ronald A. Fisher developed analysis of variance in the 1920s (Statistical Methods for Research Workers, 1925), and George Snedecor named the statistic F in his honour. F can never be negative, and its distribution is skewed to the right. In one-way ANOVA, F = mean square between groups / mean square within groups. If the null hypothesis of equal population means is true, F is close to 1, and only a large F counts against the null, so the ANOVA test uses the right tail alone. F is also used to compare two variances directly. With a non-directional hypothesis (the two population variances differ), the test is two-tailed: put the larger variance on top and read the critical value at alpha / 2. With a directional hypothesis (group 1 is more variable), it is one-tailed at alpha. Example: 21 patients have an anxiety variance of 36 and 21 controls a variance of 16, so F = 36 / 16 = 2.25 with 20 and 20 degrees of freedom. One-tailed at .05, the critical F is 2.12, so 2.25 is significant; two-tailed at .05 (.025 in each tail), the critical F is 2.46, so it is not. Option b is right because the tails follow the hypothesis, not the statistic. Exam trap: ANOVA's right-tail-only rejection region does not make every F test one-tailed. Key note: the recall list (the topics students remembered from the CIP 2023 exam) does not say which option CIP keyed, and one earlier recall line remembers the answer as 'essentially two tailed'; statistically, 'both' is the defensible answer.", "topic": "F ratio: one-tailed and two-tailed tests"}, {"q": "A researcher reports the average anxiety score of her 50 participants, then uses a t-test to conclude that anxiety differs between men and women in the whole college. These two steps use, in order:", "options": {"a": "inferential statistics, then descriptive statistics", "b": "descriptive statistics, then inferential statistics", "c": "descriptive statistics both times", "d": "inferential statistics both times"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: reporting the average anxiety score of the 50 participants is descriptive statistics, and using a t-test to conclude something about the whole college is inferential statistics. Descriptive statistics summarise the data actually collected. They include measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode), measures of variability (range, standard deviation), tables and graphs, and they make no claim beyond the people measured. Inferential statistics use probability to reason from a sample to the population it came from. They include significance tests such as the t-test, F test and chi-square, and confidence intervals. The t-test itself was published by William Sealy Gosset (1908) under the pen name \"Student\". A number that describes a sample is a statistic, written with Roman letters such as M or s; the matching value for the whole population is a parameter, written with Greek letters such as μ (mu) or σ (sigma). Example: suppose the 25 men average 45 and the 25 women average 39. Saying the gap is 45 - 39 = 6 points is description. Asking whether a 6-point gap in these 50 people means that men and women in the whole college truly differ, and getting a p value from the t-test, is inference. Option b is right because the first step summarises the sample and the second generalises to the population. Exam trap: a step that contains a mean is not automatically descriptive; ask whether the conclusion goes beyond the people actually measured.", "topic": "Descriptive vs inferential statistics"}, {"q": "'Children are born with a clean slate, and everything they become is written on it by experience.' Which perspective does this describe?", "options": {"a": "Nativism", "b": "Empiricism (tabula rasa)", "c": "The psychoanalytic perspective", "d": "The humanistic perspective"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the view that children are born with a clean slate on which experience writes everything is empiricism, the tabula rasa position. Empiricism holds that all knowledge comes from experience through the senses, not from ideas present at birth. Its classic statement is by the English philosopher John Locke (1690) in An Essay Concerning Human Understanding. Locke compared the newborn mind to \"white paper, void of all characters\"; the Latin label tabula rasa means a scraped or blank writing tablet. Simple ideas enter through sensation and reflection, and complex ideas are built by combining them. Later British empiricists included George Berkeley, David Hume, James Mill and John Stuart Mill, with Thomas Hobbes as an earlier forerunner. The opposing position, nativism, says some knowledge or capacities are inborn; Plato, René Descartes with his innate ideas, and Immanuel Kant are its classic voices. This contrast is the philosophical root of the nature versus nurture debate. In psychology, John B. Watson's behaviourism is the modern heir of empiricism: his boast \"Give me a dozen healthy infants\" claimed that training alone could turn any child into any type of adult. Example: on Locke's view, a child who grows up fearing dogs and speaking Bengali acquired both entirely from what he saw, heard and was taught. Option b is right because a blank slate filled only by experience is the defining claim of empiricism. Exam trap: if an exam offers 'behavioural perspective' and not empiricism, choose behavioural, since it carries the same blank-slate assumption.", "topic": "Empiricism and the tabula rasa"}, {"q": "A study uses a significance level of .05, and the statistical test gives p = .01. The researcher should:", "options": {"a": "reject the null hypothesis", "b": "accept the null hypothesis", "c": "reject the alternative hypothesis", "d": "conclude that the result is due to chance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: with a significance level of .05 and p = .01, the researcher should reject the null hypothesis. A significance test starts with the null hypothesis (H0), the claim that there is no effect or no difference in the population. The p-value is the probability of getting data at least as extreme as those observed if H0 were true. Ronald A. Fisher (1925), in Statistical Methods for Research Workers, set out this logic and suggested .05 as a convenient cut-off. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) added the alternative hypothesis (H1), a significance level (alpha) fixed before the study, and two kinds of error. The decision rule is simple: if p is less than or equal to alpha, reject H0 and call the result statistically significant; if p is greater than alpha, fail to reject H0. Here, step by step: alpha = .05 and p = .01; .01 is smaller than .05; so H0 is rejected. Rejecting H0 risks a Type I error (rejecting a true null), and alpha is the rate of that error accepted in advance. Example: a relaxation programme is compared with a control group on exam anxiety; the t-test gives p = .01, so the researcher rejects the null of no difference and concludes that the programme group differs. Option a is right because p falls below alpha. Exam trap: p is not the probability that H0 is true, and a large p never lets you \"accept\" H0, only fail to reject it.", "topic": "p-value and rejecting the null hypothesis"}, {"q": "After a stroke, a man understands speech and speaks fluently, but he cannot find the names of everyday objects such as a comb or a key. The lobe most likely damaged is the:", "options": {"a": "occipital lobe", "b": "right frontal lobe", "c": "right parietal lobe", "d": "left temporal lobe"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: a man who understands speech and speaks fluently but cannot name everyday objects most likely has damage to the left temporal lobe. His problem is anomia, and when it is the main deficit it is called anomic aphasia: comprehension, fluency and repetition are good, but word finding fails, especially for nouns. Patients fill the gaps with circumlocution (talking around the word) and vague words such as \"thing\". Language is usually based in the left hemisphere. Paul Broca (1861) linked non-fluent, effortful speech to the left frontal lobe, and Carl Wernicke (1874) linked fluent speech with poor comprehension to the left posterior temporal lobe. Naming itself depends heavily on left temporal cortex. Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1996) showed that damage to the left temporal pole and inferior temporal regions impaired retrieval of names for people, animals and tools, with different parts linked to different categories. Example: shown a comb, the man says \"it's the thing you, you know, for your hair\" and can show how to use it, which proves he recognises it and lacks only the word. Option d is right because fluent speech, intact comprehension and an isolated naming failure point to the left temporal lobe. Exam trap: \"cannot name\" is not \"cannot recognise\"; failing to recognise a seen object would be visual agnosia, a problem of the occipital and temporal visual areas. Key note: anomia is the least localising aphasia and can also follow damage to the left angular gyrus in the parietal lobe, but the left temporal lobe is the textbook answer.", "topic": "Anomic aphasia and the left temporal lobe"}, {"q": "In Joseph LeDoux's 'low road' for fear, a sensory signal about a threat travels from the thalamus directly to the:", "options": {"a": "prefrontal cortex", "b": "hippocampus", "c": "amygdala", "d": "cerebellum"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Joseph LeDoux's low road for fear, a threat signal travels from the thalamus directly to the amygdala. Joseph LeDoux (1996) set out this two-route model in The Emotional Brain, based on fear conditioning studies in rats pairing a tone with shock. Sensory signals pass first through the thalamus, the brain's relay station, and from there take two routes. The low road runs straight from the thalamus to the amygdala. It is fast but crude, carrying only a rough sketch of the stimulus, enough to start a defensive reaction before you know what you are reacting to. The high road runs from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and then to the amygdala. It is slower but detailed, so it can identify what the stimulus really is. Both roads end in the amygdala, an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei in the medial temporal lobe. Its central nucleus then triggers the fear response through the hypothalamus (racing heart, stress hormones) and the brainstem (freezing, startle). Example: in a garden you jump back from a curved shape on the path; a moment later the visual cortex tells you it is a hose, and you calm down. The jump came from the low road, the correction from the high road. Option c is right because the amygdala is the direct target of the thalamic low road. Exam trap: the prefrontal cortex regulates and extinguishes fear afterwards; it does not receive the first signal. Key note: the recall list (topics students remembered from the CIP 2023 exam) records only 'emotional info goes to which area at the end', with options including prefrontal cortex, thalamus and amygdala; both roads end in the amygdala, so it is the answer either way.", "topic": "LeDoux's low road and the amygdala"}, {"q": "According to the actor-observer bias, when we explain another person's behaviour we tend to attribute it to:", "options": {"a": "situational factors", "b": "luck", "c": "situational and dispositional factors equally", "d": "dispositional (internal) factors"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: according to the actor-observer bias, we tend to attribute another person's behaviour to dispositional (internal) factors. Attribution is the process of explaining why people act as they do. Fritz Heider (1958) first separated internal (dispositional) causes, such as personality, ability or effort, from external (situational) causes, such as luck, pressure or task difficulty. Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971) then described the actor-observer bias: as actors we explain our own behaviour by the situation, but as observers we explain other people's identical behaviour by their dispositions. Two reasons are usually given. The first is perceptual: the actor looks out at the situation, while the observer looks at the actor, so whatever is most visible seems to be the cause. Michael Storms (1973) reversed the bias by showing people videotapes of a conversation from the other person's viewpoint. The second is information: actors know how they behave across many situations, while observers usually see a single moment. The observer's half of this bias overlaps with the fundamental attribution error, named by Lee Ross (1977): the general tendency to overrate dispositional and underrate situational causes of others' behaviour. Example: when you trip on the stairs, you blame the wet step; when a classmate trips on the same stairs, you decide she is clumsy. Option d is right because observers explaining someone else's behaviour lean towards disposition. Exam trap: do not confuse this with the self-serving bias, which depends on the outcome: we credit our successes to ourselves and blame our failures on the situation.", "topic": "Actor-observer bias"}, {"q": "Ravi learned Spanish in college and is now learning French. When he tries to recall his Spanish vocabulary, French words keep coming to mind instead. This is:", "options": {"a": "retroactive interference", "b": "proactive interference", "c": "decay", "d": "repression"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: when newly learned French words block Ravi's recall of his older Spanish vocabulary, that is retroactive interference. Interference theory says we forget mainly because memories compete with one another, not because they simply fade. John McGeoch (1932) argued this in his attack on the old law of disuse, and interference became the leading account of forgetting from long-term memory. It works in two directions. In retroactive interference, new learning disrupts recall of something learned earlier; retro means backwards, so the new memory reaches back and spoils the old. In proactive interference, earlier learning disrupts recall of something learned later; pro means forwards, so the old memory pushes forward into the new. Benton Underwood (1957) showed how strong proactive interference is: the more word lists people had learned before, the faster they forgot a new one. Interference is greatest when the two sets of material are similar, which is why two closely related languages clash so badly. John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924) supported interference over decay: people who slept after learning nonsense syllables recalled more than those who stayed awake. Example: after changing your phone number, you struggle to remember the old one (retroactive); when asked for the new one, the old one keeps coming out (proactive). Option a is right because the memory being tested is the old one (Spanish) and the intruder is the new one (French). Exam trap: first ask which memory is being recalled, then remember that PRO means the old works forward and RETRO means the new works backward.", "topic": "Retroactive interference"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT common to the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ) and the NEO Personality Inventory?", "options": {"a": "Neuroticism", "b": "Extraversion", "c": "Psychoticism", "d": "None of these; all three appear in both"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychoticism is not common to the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ) and the NEO Personality Inventory. The EPQ was published by Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975). It measures Hans Eysenck's three superfactors, extraversion (E), neuroticism (N) and psychoticism (P), plus a Lie scale that checks for faking good. It added P to the earlier Eysenck Personality Inventory (1964), which measured only E and N. Eysenck saw psychoticism as tough-mindedness: aggressive, cold, impulsive, egocentric and low in empathy. The NEO Personality Inventory was developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985), and its revised form, the NEO-PI-R (1992), measures the Big Five: neuroticism, extraversion, openness to experience, agreeableness and conscientiousness, each with six facets. Setting the lists side by side: the EPQ has E, N and P; the NEO has N, E, O, A and C. The overlap is only N and E. Psychoticism appears only in the EPQ, while openness, agreeableness and conscientiousness appear only in the NEO. Research links high P with low agreeableness and low conscientiousness, which is partly why Eysenck argued that three factors were enough. Example: a person who is impulsive, careless of rules and unconcerned about others would score high on EPQ psychoticism and low on NEO agreeableness and conscientiousness. Option c is right because the NEO has no psychoticism scale. Exam trap: if a version of this question lists agreeableness instead, it too is not shared, because the EPQ has no agreeableness scale; only neuroticism and extraversion appear in both.", "topic": "EPQ vs NEO: shared dimensions"}, {"q": "Order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation are facets of which NEO-PI-R domain?", "options": {"a": "Agreeableness", "b": "Openness to experience", "c": "Extraversion", "d": "Conscientiousness"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation are facets of the conscientiousness domain of the NEO-PI-R. The NEO-PI-R was published by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992) to measure the Big Five, the five broad trait dimensions that factor analyses of personality keep finding. Its structure is simple arithmetic: 5 domains × 6 facets = 30 facets, and 30 facets × 8 items = 240 items, each rated on a five-point scale. Conscientiousness is the tendency to be organised, reliable, hard-working and self-controlled. Its six facets are competence (feeling capable), order (being neat and organised), dutifulness (keeping rules and promises), achievement striving (working hard towards goals), self-discipline (finishing tasks despite boredom) and deliberation (thinking before acting). Only competence is missing from the question's list. Each other domain has its own six. Neuroticism: anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness, vulnerability. Extraversion: warmth, gregariousness, assertiveness, activity, excitement seeking, positive emotions. Openness: fantasy, aesthetics, feelings, actions, ideas, values. Agreeableness: trust, straightforwardness, altruism, compliance, modesty, tender-mindedness. Murray Barrick and Michael Mount (1991) found conscientiousness to be the most consistent Big Five predictor of job performance across occupations. Example: a student who keeps a planner, submits work before deadlines and checks answers before handing in a paper is high in conscientiousness. Option d is right because all five listed facets belong to conscientiousness. Exam trap: impulsiveness sounds like the opposite of deliberation, but in the NEO-PI-R it is a neuroticism facet, not a conscientiousness one.", "topic": "NEO-PI-R conscientiousness facets"}, {"q": "The Bonferroni correction is used to:", "options": {"a": "increase the power of a single t-test", "b": "control the familywise Type I error rate when several comparisons are made", "c": "correct for unequal variances between two groups", "d": "normalise a skewed distribution"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Bonferroni correction is used to control the familywise Type I error rate when several comparisons are made. A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis; alpha (often .05) is its chance in one test. When a researcher runs many tests, these chances build up. The familywise error rate is the probability of at least one Type I error anywhere in the family of tests. With five independent tests at .05, the chance of no error in all five is 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 = 0.774, so the chance of at least one false positive is 1 - 0.774 = 0.226, about 23%. The correction is named after the Italian mathematician Carlo Emilio Bonferroni, whose probability inequality underlies it; Olive Jean Dunn (1961) applied it to multiple comparisons of means. The rule is to divide alpha by the number of comparisons. For five tests, .05 / 5 = .01, so each test must reach p ≤ .01, and the familywise rate stays at or below 5 × .01 = .05. Example: comparing four therapy groups in every pair gives 4 × 3 / 2 = 6 t-tests, so the per-test alpha becomes .05 / 6 = .0083. The price is that Bonferroni is conservative: stricter thresholds lower power and raise Type II errors. Option b is right because the correction keeps the overall false-positive rate at the chosen alpha. Exam trap: Bonferroni never increases power; it trades power for protection against false positives.", "topic": "Bonferroni correction"}, {"q": "The most effective psychological treatment for a specific phobia, such as an intense fear of dogs, is:", "options": {"a": "client-centred therapy", "b": "free association", "c": "exposure-based therapy, such as systematic desensitisation or in vivo exposure", "d": "electroconvulsive therapy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the most effective psychological treatment for a specific phobia such as an intense fear of dogs is exposure-based therapy, such as systematic desensitisation or in vivo exposure. A specific phobia is a marked, lasting fear of a particular object or situation that the person avoids. Avoidance brings instant relief, which negatively reinforces it, so the fear never fades. Exposure breaks this cycle through repeated, prolonged contact with the feared thing until fear declines and new safety learning takes hold. Mary Cover Jones (1924) removed a toddler called Peter's fear of a rabbit by bringing it closer bit by bit while he ate. Joseph Wolpe (1958) formalised it as systematic desensitisation, based on reciprocal inhibition: relaxation and anxiety cannot exist together. The client learns deep muscle relaxation, builds a fear hierarchy rated in Subjective Units of Distress (SUDS, 0 to 100), then imagines each step while relaxed, starting at the bottom. In vivo exposure uses real situations, and flooding starts with the most feared situation at once. Lars-Göran Öst (1989) showed that a single guided exposure session of up to three hours can treat many specific phobias. Example: a dog-phobic client climbs a hierarchy: a photo of a dog (SUDS 20), a video (35), a leashed dog across the road (50), standing two metres away (70), and patting a calm dog (90). Option c is right because exposure has the strongest evidence for specific phobia. Exam trap: flooding is also exposure; it differs only in being intense rather than graded.", "topic": "Exposure therapy for specific phobia"}, {"q": "Which therapy was developed specifically for, and has the strongest evidence in, borderline personality disorder?", "options": {"a": "Exposure and response prevention", "b": "Aversion therapy", "c": "Systematic desensitisation", "d": "Dialectical behaviour therapy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed specifically for borderline personality disorder and has the strongest evidence in it. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a lasting pattern of unstable emotions, relationships and self-image, with impulsivity, fear of abandonment, and often self-harm and suicidal behaviour. DBT was developed by the American psychologist Marsha Linehan (1993), originally for chronically suicidal women with BPD. Her biosocial theory says BPD grows from a biological vulnerability to intense emotion combined with an invalidating environment, one that dismisses or punishes the child's feelings. Dialectical refers to balancing two opposites: acceptance (validation and mindfulness, drawn from Zen practice) and change (behavioural problem solving). Standard DBT combines weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills group, phone coaching in a crisis and a consultation team for therapists. The skills group teaches four modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Randomised trials have shown less self-harm and fewer suicide attempts and hospital days than usual treatment. Other therapies with evidence in BPD include mentalisation-based treatment (Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy), schema therapy (Jeffrey Young) and transference-focused psychotherapy (Otto Kernberg). Example: a young woman who cuts herself after arguments learns to ride out the urge with distress tolerance skills, such as holding ice, then uses interpersonal effectiveness skills to ask her partner for what she needs. Option d is right because DBT is the only option designed for BPD. Exam trap: DBT is a third-wave cognitive-behavioural therapy, so do not rule it out just because the other options are behavioural too.", "topic": "Dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD"}, {"q": "Charles Spearman proposed a general intelligence factor, 'g', after noticing that:", "options": {"a": "scores on very different mental tests all correlate positively with one another", "b": "people have eight separate and independent intelligences", "c": "intelligence is entirely inherited", "d": "average IQ scores rise from one generation to the next"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Charles Spearman proposed a general intelligence factor, g, after noticing that scores on very different mental tests all correlate positively with one another. Charles Spearman (1904), a British psychologist, set this out in his paper \"'General intelligence', objectively determined and measured\". He studied schoolchildren's marks in subjects such as classics, French, English and mathematics, along with tests of sensory discrimination, and found that a child who did well on one tended to do well on the others. This pattern of all-positive correlations is now called the positive manifold. To explain it, Spearman developed an early form of factor analysis, a method that finds the hidden factors behind a set of correlations. He concluded that every test draws on one general factor, g, shared by all intellectual tasks, plus a specific factor, s, unique to that test: his two-factor theory. Tests differ in how heavily they load on g; abstract reasoning tasks load highly, simple sensory tasks less. Spearman linked g to a kind of general mental energy. Example: a student who scores high on vocabulary will usually score above average on arithmetic and block design too, although each test also needs its own specific skill. Louis Thurstone (1938) later challenged a single g with seven primary mental abilities, but even his abilities correlated with one another, pointing back to g. Option a is right because the positive correlations among diverse tests were the evidence g was built on. Exam trap: \"two-factor theory\" does not mean two kinds of intelligence; it means g plus s.", "topic": "Spearman's g and the positive manifold"}, {"q": "In the method of paired comparisons, each stimulus is compared with every other stimulus. With 8 stimuli, how many pairs must a judge rate?", "options": {"a": "16", "b": "64", "c": "56", "d": "28"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: with 8 stimuli a judge must rate 28 pairs. In the method of paired comparisons, every stimulus is presented with every other stimulus, one pair at a time, and the judge simply says which member of the pair is preferred, heavier, brighter or more favourable. Louis Thurstone (1927) gave the method its theory in his law of comparative judgement. He assumed that each stimulus produces a spread of reactions on a hidden psychological scale, so the proportion of times A is chosen over B shows how far apart A and B lie. Turning those proportions into z scores yields an interval scale, which Thurstone used in psychophysics and in attitude scaling. The number of pairs follows a simple rule: each stimulus is paired with the n minus 1 others, and each pair is counted once, so pairs = n(n - 1) / 2. Step by step for this item: n = 8; n - 1 = 7; 8 x 7 = 56; 56 / 2 = 28. Example: a researcher ranking 8 brands of tea by taste would serve 28 pairs of cups, and a brand that wins most of its 7 contests ends up at the top of the scale. The count grows fast (20 stimuli need 190 pairs), so the method suits small sets. Option d is right because 8 x 7 / 2 = 28. Exam trap: forgetting to divide by 2 gives 56, which counts A versus B and B versus A as two different pairs.", "topic": "Method of paired comparisons: number of pairs"}, {"q": "In Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, which stages come immediately before and immediately after 'autonomy vs shame and doubt'?", "options": {"a": "Initiative vs guilt before; industry vs inferiority after", "b": "Trust vs mistrust before; initiative vs guilt after", "c": "Trust vs mistrust before; industry vs inferiority after", "d": "Intimacy vs isolation before; identity vs role confusion after"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: trust vs mistrust comes before autonomy vs shame and doubt, and initiative vs guilt comes after it. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, proposed eight psychosocial stages that span the whole life. Each stage is a crisis, a turning point between a healthy and an unhealthy pole, and resolving it well leaves a lasting virtue or basic strength. The order is fixed by what Erikson called the epigenetic principle: each stage has its own time of special importance and builds on those before it. The stages are: trust vs mistrust (birth to about 1 year, hope); autonomy vs shame and doubt (about 1 to 3, will); initiative vs guilt (about 3 to 6, purpose); industry vs inferiority (about 6 to 12, competence); identity vs role confusion (adolescence, fidelity); intimacy vs isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity vs stagnation (middle adulthood, care); integrity vs despair (old age, wisdom). Example: a two-year-old who insists on feeding herself and choosing her own clothes is working on autonomy; if her parents mock every spill, she may learn shame and doubt. A year earlier the issue was whether her caregivers were reliable (trust); a few years later it will be whether she can plan and start activities without guilt (initiative). Option b is right because it gives the true neighbours of stage 2: stage 1 before and stage 3 after. Exam trap: industry vs inferiority is stage 4, the school years, so it comes two steps after autonomy, not next.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial stages: sequence"}, {"q": "During psychoanalysis, a client keeps 'forgetting' appointments and changes the subject whenever painful memories come close. Sigmund Freud called this:", "options": {"a": "transference", "b": "countertransference", "c": "catharsis", "d": "resistance"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: Freud called this resistance. Resistance is the client's unconscious opposition to the work of therapy, especially to bringing repressed, painful material into awareness. Sigmund Freud described it in his chapter of Studies on Hysteria (1895), written with Josef Breuer, when he noticed that patients pushed back against the very memories whose recall would relieve their symptoms. The same forces that repressed a memory in the first place keep it out of consciousness during treatment, so the closer the analysis comes to a sensitive area, the stronger the resistance becomes. It takes many forms: missing or forgetting sessions, arriving late, long silences, changing the subject, joking, intellectualising, or suddenly feeling cured. Freud came to see resistance not as a nuisance but as a signpost. The analyst notices it, interprets it and helps the client understand what it protects, and this slow process of working through is central to psychoanalysis. Example: a woman in analysis talks freely about her job, but each time her father's drinking comes up she says she has nothing to say, and then she forgets her next appointment. The analyst might gently point out the pattern and ask what makes that topic so hard to approach. Option d is right because forgetting appointments and dodging painful memories are classic signs of opposing the uncovering process. Exam trap: transference can also slow therapy, but it means directing old feelings onto the analyst; behaviour that steers away from the painful material itself is resistance.", "topic": "Resistance in psychoanalysis"}, {"q": "A 10-year-old helps around the house mainly so that his parents will see him as a 'good boy'. In Lawrence Kohlberg's theory he is reasoning at:", "options": {"a": "Stage 1, punishment and obedience orientation", "b": "Stage 3, good boy-good girl (interpersonal conformity) orientation", "c": "Stage 2, instrumental exchange (naive hedonism)", "d": "Stage 4, law and order orientation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the boy is reasoning at Stage 3, the good boy-good girl (interpersonal conformity) orientation. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), in his doctoral dissertation at the University of Chicago, studied moral reasoning by giving boys dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasons behind a choice, not the choice itself. He proposed three levels with two stages each. Preconventional: Stage 1, punishment and obedience (right is whatever avoids punishment); Stage 2, instrumental exchange (right is what serves my interests, 'you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours'). Conventional: Stage 3, good boy-good girl (right is what pleases others and earns their approval); Stage 4, law and order (right is doing one's duty and keeping the social system going). Postconventional: Stage 5, social contract (laws are agreements that can be changed for the common good); Stage 6, universal ethical principles (justice and human dignity). Example: asked why Heinz should steal, a Stage 3 child says, 'His family would think he is a bad husband if he let her die.' Carol Gilligan (1982) later argued that this justice-based scheme undervalued an ethic of care. Option b is right because the boy acts mainly to be seen as a 'good boy' by his parents, which is approval-seeking. Exam trap: Stage 2 also involves doing things for others, but for a concrete return; Stage 3 helps to win approval and be thought of as nice.", "topic": "Kohlberg's Stage 3: good boy-good girl"}, {"q": "Match each theme with its theorist. (i) Attachment theory (ii) Parenting styles (iii) Ecological systems theory (iv) Zone of proximal development. A: Diana Baumrind, B: John Bowlby, C: Lev Vygotsky, D: Urie Bronfenbrenner", "options": {"a": "i-B, ii-A, iii-D, iv-C", "b": "i-A, ii-B, iii-D, iv-C", "c": "i-B, ii-A, iii-C, iv-D", "d": "i-D, ii-A, iii-B, iv-C"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: attachment theory goes with John Bowlby, parenting styles with Diana Baumrind, ecological systems theory with Urie Bronfenbrenner and the zone of proximal development with Lev Vygotsky. Bowlby, a British psychiatrist, set out attachment theory in his trilogy Attachment (1969), Separation (1973) and Loss (1980). He argued that infants are biologically prepared to bond with a caregiver, that one main figure matters most (monotropy) and that early bonds become an internal working model for later relationships. Baumrind (1966, 1971), by crossing warmth with control, described authoritative (warm and firm), authoritarian (strict and cold) and permissive (warm but lax) styles; Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) added the neglectful style. Bronfenbrenner (1979) placed the child inside nested systems: the microsystem (family, school), the mesosystem (links between them), the exosystem (settings that affect the child indirectly, such as a parent's job) and the macrosystem (culture); he later added the chronosystem (change over time). Vygotsky, the Soviet psychologist whose ideas reached English readers in Mind in Society (1978), defined the zone of proximal development as the gap between what a child can do alone and what she can do with skilled help. Example: a child whose father loses his job (exosystem) may meet harsher, authoritarian parenting at home, which can strain a once secure attachment. Option a is right because it is the only option with all four pairs correct. Exam trap: Bowlby and Baumrind both start with B, and Bronfenbrenner and Vygotsky both stress social context, so check every pair.", "topic": "Matching developmental theorists to their theories"}, {"q": "A girl with a ratio IQ of 125 has a chronological age of 8 years. What is her mental age?", "options": {"a": "9 years", "b": "12.5 years", "c": "10 years", "d": "6.4 years"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: her mental age is 10 years. Mental age (MA) is the age level at which a child performs on an intelligence test; the idea grew out of the age-graded scales of Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905, 1908). William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age (CA) to get a mental quotient, and Lewis Terman multiplied it by 100 in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, giving the ratio IQ: IQ = MA / CA x 100. To find MA, rearrange the formula: MA = IQ x CA / 100. Step by step: IQ x CA = 125 x 8 = 1000; 1000 / 100 = 10 years. Check: 10 / 8 = 1.25, and 1.25 x 100 = 125. To find CA instead, use CA = MA x 100 / IQ; for example, a boy with MA 9 and IQ 75 has CA = 9 x 100 / 75 = 900 / 75 = 12 years. Ratio IQ fails for adults, because mental age stops rising in the late teens while chronological age keeps growing. David Wechsler (1939) therefore introduced the deviation IQ, which compares a person with others of the same age on a scale with mean 100 and standard deviation 15. Option c is right because 125 x 8 / 100 = 10. Exam trap: dividing the wrong way (8 x 100 / 125 = 6.4) gives an MA below the CA, which is impossible when the IQ is above 100.", "topic": "Ratio IQ: calculating mental age"}, {"q": "Conformity and obedience differ in the kind of social pressure involved. Which pairing is correct?", "options": {"a": "Conformity: implicit pressure from peers or the group; obedience: an explicit, direct command from an authority", "b": "Conformity: an explicit command from an authority; obedience: implicit pressure from peers", "c": "Both involve explicit, direct commands from an authority", "d": "Both involve only implicit pressure from the group"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: in conformity the pressure is implicit and comes from peers or the group, whereas in obedience it is an explicit, direct command from an authority. Social influence can be arranged as a ladder of increasing directness. Conformity means changing behaviour or belief to match a group when nobody has asked you to; the pressure comes from the group's unspoken norms. Solomon Asch (1951) showed it in his line-judgement studies, where students gave clearly wrong answers to match a unanimous group of confederates. Compliance is going along with an explicit request, usually from an equal, as in the foot-in-the-door and door-in-the-face tactics. Obedience is following a direct order from someone seen as a legitimate authority; Stanley Milgram (1963) found that 65% of his participants obeyed an experimenter's commands up to what they believed was a 450-volt shock. So conformity is horizontal (between equals) and implicit, while obedience is vertical (from above) and explicit. Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955) explained why we conform: normative influence (the wish to be liked and accepted) and informational influence (the belief that the group knows better). Example: a new employee starts wearing formal clothes because everyone else does (conformity); when the manager says 'Wear a tie tomorrow' and he does, that is obedience. Option a is right because it matches each process with its true source of pressure. Exam trap: compliance also involves an explicit request, but from a peer rather than an authority, so 'explicit' alone does not mean obedience.", "topic": "Conformity versus obedience"}, {"q": "Which of the following does NOT describe Raymond Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test (CFIT)?", "options": {"a": "It is a performance test in which objects are handled and arranged", "b": "It is a paper-and-pencil test that can be given to groups", "c": "It is non-verbal, using figures and patterns", "d": "It aims to reduce the influence of language and cultural background"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the CFIT is not a performance test in which objects are handled and arranged. Raymond Cattell (1940) published an early 'culture-free' intelligence test, later revised as the Culture Fair Intelligence Test and issued by his Institute for Personality and Ability Testing (IPAT, founded 1949). It aims to measure fluid intelligence (Gf), the ability to reason with new problems, while keeping the effects of language, schooling and culture as low as possible. It comes in three scales for different ages. Scales 2 and 3 are paper-and-pencil booklets that can be given to groups, each with four subtests: series (choose the figure that completes a sequence), classification (find the odd figure out), matrices (complete a pattern) and conditions or topology (find the figure where a dot can be placed in the same relation to the shapes). All items use figures, so no reading is needed, and every subtest has a strict time limit, so the test is speeded. A performance test, by contrast, requires the person to manipulate concrete material, as in Kohs Block Design, Alexander's Pass-Along Test or the Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests. Example: a child who cannot read English can still take the CFIT by marking which drawing completes a row of shapes. Option a is right because the CFIT is a figural paper-and-pencil test, not a test of handling objects. Exam trap: 'non-verbal' does not mean 'performance'; a test can avoid words and still be done with pencil and paper. Key note: the recall list says the CIP 2023 options were speed test, power test, non-verbal test and performance test, and that the first three were true but 'performance test' was not. That matches this item: the CFIT is timed, non-verbal and measures reasoning power, but it is not a performance test.", "topic": "Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a feature of a major depressive episode 'with atypical features' in DSM-5-TR?", "options": {"a": "Early morning awakening", "b": "Mood brightens in response to positive events (mood reactivity)", "c": "Marked loss of appetite and weight", "d": "Depression regularly worse in the morning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: mood reactivity, in which mood brightens in response to positive events, is a feature of depression with atypical features. In DSM-5-TR (2022), 'with atypical features' is a specifier added to a major depressive episode. Mood reactivity is required, plus at least two of four other features: significant weight gain or increased appetite; hypersomnia (sleeping too much); leaden paralysis (heavy, leaden feelings in the arms or legs); and a long-standing pattern of sensitivity to interpersonal rejection that causes real social or work problems. The specifier cannot be given if the same episode meets criteria for melancholic or catatonic features. It entered the manual in DSM-IV (1994), building on work by Frederic Quitkin and colleagues at Columbia University in the 1980s, who found that these patients responded better to monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) than to tricyclic antidepressants. Its features are almost the mirror image of melancholia, the classic 'typical' depression, whose signs include loss of pleasure, lack of mood reactivity, early morning awakening, depression regularly worse in the morning, marked weight loss, psychomotor change and excessive guilt. Example: a 25-year-old woman has felt low for weeks, yet she cheers up when friends visit; she sleeps eleven hours a night, craves sweets, has gained weight, says her limbs feel like lead and is crushed by any hint of criticism. Option b is right because mood reactivity is the one required atypical feature. Exam trap: the other three options are classic melancholic features, so think 'reverse of melancholia' when you see atypical.", "topic": "Depression with atypical features"}, {"q": "A chemical that a neuron releases into the synaptic cleft and that acts on a nearby target cell across that small gap is a:", "options": {"a": "hormone", "b": "neurohormone", "c": "neurotransmitter", "d": "pheromone"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: a chemical released into the synaptic cleft that acts on a nearby target cell is a neurotransmitter. Neurons communicate across synapses, tiny gaps between the end of one neuron and the next cell. Otto Loewi (1921) proved that this communication is chemical. He stimulated the vagus nerve of a frog's heart, which slowed it, then transferred the fluid bathing that heart to a second heart, which also slowed. He called the substance Vagusstoff; it was later identified as acetylcholine, and Loewi shared the 1936 Nobel Prize with Henry Dale. A substance counts as a neurotransmitter when it is made in the neuron, stored in synaptic vesicles in the terminal button, released when an action potential arrives (calcium enters and the vesicles fuse with the membrane), binds to receptors on the postsynaptic cell, and is then quickly removed by reuptake or broken down by an enzyme. Neurohormones, hormones and pheromones also carry chemical messages, but they travel farther: through the blood, or even outside the body. Example: at the neuromuscular junction, a motor neuron releases acetylcholine, which binds to receptors on the muscle fibre and makes it contract; the enzyme acetylcholinesterase then breaks it down so the muscle can relax. Option c is right because release into the synaptic cleft onto a nearby cell is the defining feature of a neurotransmitter. Exam trap: a neurohormone is also released by a neuron, so the deciding clue is where it goes: into the cleft (neurotransmitter) or into the blood (neurohormone).", "topic": "Neurotransmitter versus hormone and neurohormone"}, {"q": "The smallest unit of language that carries meaning is the:", "options": {"a": "morpheme", "b": "phoneme", "c": "syntax", "d": "pragmatics"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the morpheme is the smallest unit of language that carries meaning. Language is built in layers: sounds combine into meaningful units, these form words, words form phrases and sentences, and rules govern each step. The term 'morpheme' came from the Polish linguist Jan Baudouin de Courtenay in the late nineteenth century, and Leonard Bloomfield's book Language (1933) made it a basic idea of linguistics. A morpheme cannot be split into smaller parts that still mean something. Free morphemes can stand alone as words (dog, happy, run); bound morphemes must attach to another morpheme (the plural -s, the past tense -ed, prefixes such as un- and suffixes such as -ness). So 'dog' is one morpheme, 'dogs' is two, and 'unhappiness' is three: un + happy + ness. A morpheme differs from a phoneme, the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning without having any meaning itself. The /b/ and /p/ in 'bat' and 'pat' are phonemes; English has about 40 to 44 of them. Above these units sit syntax (rules for ordering words into sentences), semantics (meaning) and pragmatics (how language is used in social settings). Example: a child who says 'two foots' has learned that the bound morpheme -s marks the plural, even though she applies it to the wrong word. Option a is right because the morpheme is defined as the minimal unit of meaning. Exam trap: if a question asks for the smallest unit of sound, or the basic unit of speech, the answer switches to phoneme; the word 'meaning' points to morpheme.", "topic": "Morpheme: smallest unit of meaning"}, {"q": "A tutor gives a child a lot of help with a new puzzle and then gradually withdraws that help as the child becomes able to solve it alone. This is called:", "options": {"a": "conservation", "b": "assimilation", "c": "scaffolding", "d": "shaping"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: giving a lot of help at first and then gradually withdrawing it as the child becomes able to manage alone is called scaffolding. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) coined the term in a study in which a tutor helped 3- to 5-year-olds build a pyramid from interlocking wooden blocks. Like the temporary scaffolding around a building, the support holds the learner up while a skill is being built and is taken away once the structure can stand on its own. Wood and his colleagues listed what a good tutor does: recruits the child's interest, simplifies the task, keeps the child on track, marks the important features, controls frustration and demonstrates solutions. The key rule is contingency: help increases when the child struggles and decreases when the child succeeds. Scaffolding is closely tied to the zone of proximal development of Lev Vygotsky, the gap between what a child can do alone and what she can do with a more skilled partner. Scaffolding is the practical way an adult works inside that zone, although Vygotsky himself never used the word. Example: when teaching a child to ride a bicycle, a parent first holds the seat firmly, then holds it loosely, then just runs alongside, and finally lets go completely. Option c is right because the gradual withdrawal of support as competence grows is the defining feature of scaffolding. Exam trap: shaping also builds skills in small steps, but by reinforcing closer and closer approximations to a target, not by fading an adult's help.", "topic": "Scaffolding (Wood, Bruner and Ross)"}, {"q": "In the prisoner's dilemma, each player does better individually by ______, yet both end up worse off than if both had ______.", "options": {"a": "cooperating (staying silent); defected (confessed)", "b": "defecting (confessing); cooperated (stayed silent)", "c": "withdrawing from the game; competed", "d": "trusting the other; distrusted"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: each player does better individually by defecting (confessing), yet both end up worse off than if both had cooperated (stayed silent). The game was devised by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher (1950) at the RAND Corporation, and the mathematician Albert Tucker gave it the prisoners' story and its name. Two suspects are questioned separately. In a typical version, if both stay silent each gets 1 year; if one confesses and the other stays silent, the confessor goes free and the silent one gets 10 years; if both confess, each gets 5 years. From one prisoner's view: if my partner stays silent, confessing gives me 0 years instead of 1; if my partner confesses, confessing gives me 5 years instead of 10. Either way confessing is better for me, so it is the dominant strategy, and both confessing is the Nash equilibrium. Yet 5 years each is worse than the 1 year each they would get by both staying silent. This makes it a mixed-motive game and a model of social dilemmas, where individual rationality leads to collective loss. When the game is repeated, cooperation can win: in Robert Axelrod's computer tournaments around 1980, the simple tit-for-tat strategy (cooperate first, then copy the partner's last move) scored best. Example: two rival shops each cut prices to steal customers and both earn less than if neither had. Option b is right because defection is individually best but mutually harmful. Exam trap: option a reverses the logic; cooperation pays only when both players cooperate.", "topic": "Prisoner's dilemma"}, {"q": "In research practice, the questionable practice called HARKing stands for:", "options": {"a": "Hypothesizing After the Results are Known", "b": "Hypothesis And Research Keying", "c": "Holding Analyses Rigid until Known", "d": "Hierarchical Analysis of Research Knowledge"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: HARKing stands for Hypothesizing After the Results are Known. Norbert Kerr (1998) coined the term for a common questionable research practice: looking at the data, finding a pattern, and then writing the report as if that pattern had been predicted from the start. The problem is not exploring data; exploration is valuable. The problem is disguising an after-the-fact (post hoc) idea as a before-the-fact (a priori) hypothesis. When many patterns are possible, some will appear by chance alone, so a hypothesis built to fit one of them will look strongly confirmed even if it is just noise. HARKing therefore inflates false positives, hides the hypotheses that failed and gives readers a false picture of how strong a theory is. It became a central concern in the replication crisis of the 2010s, alongside p-hacking (trying many analyses until one gives p below .05). The main remedies are pre-registration, in which hypotheses and analyses are filed publicly before data collection, and Registered Reports, in which a journal reviews and accepts the study plan before the results exist. Example: a researcher predicts that caffeine will improve memory, finds no overall effect, notices that it helped only students tested in the afternoon, and publishes a paper whose introduction 'predicts' an afternoon effect. Option a is right because Kerr's acronym spells out exactly this practice. Exam trap: HARKing is not simply forming ideas after seeing data; it is presenting such an idea as if it had been made in advance.", "topic": "HARKing (Hypothesizing After the Results are Known)"}, {"q": "In the Atkinson-Shiffrin multi-store model (1968), information passes through the memory stores in which order?", "options": {"a": "Short-term store, sensory register, long-term store", "b": "Long-term store, short-term store, sensory register", "c": "Sensory register, long-term store, short-term store", "d": "Sensory register, short-term store, long-term store"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: information passes from the sensory register to the short-term store and then to the long-term store. Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) proposed this multi-store model, often called the modal model, which treats memory as a flow of information through three stores linked by control processes. First, the sensory register holds a brief, raw copy of what the senses take in. George Sperling (1960) showed that the visual (iconic) store lasts only about a quarter to half a second; the auditory (echoic) store lasts about 2 to 4 seconds. Only what we attend to moves on. Second, the short-term store holds a small amount, about 7 plus or minus 2 items according to George Miller (1956), for roughly 18 to 30 seconds unless it is rehearsed; Lloyd and Margaret Peterson (1959) showed rapid forgetting when rehearsal was blocked. Third, rehearsal transfers information to the long-term store, which seems unlimited in capacity and can last a lifetime. Example: you glance at a phone number (sensory register), repeat it while dialling (short-term store), and after using it many times you know it by heart (long-term store). Later, Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) replaced the single short-term store with a multi-part working memory, and Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) argued that depth of processing matters more than rehearsal. Option d is right because it gives the sensory, short-term, long-term order. Exam trap: the retrieval route from long-term back to short-term does not change the order in which new information enters.", "topic": "Atkinson-Shiffrin multi-store model"}, {"q": "Using interviews, observation and questionnaires to study the same research question, so that each set of findings can cross-check the others, is called:", "options": {"a": "randomisation", "b": "counterbalancing", "c": "triangulation", "d": "matching"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: using interviews, observation and questionnaires on the same question so that each set of findings cross-checks the others is called triangulation. The word comes from navigation and surveying, where the position of a point is fixed by taking bearings from two or more known points. In research it means approaching the same question from several angles, so that the weaknesses of one approach are balanced by the strengths of another. Norman Denzin (1970), in The Research Act, described four types: data triangulation (different times, places or people), investigator triangulation (several researchers), theory triangulation (several theoretical lenses) and methodological triangulation (several methods), which he further split into within-method and between-method forms. When the different sources agree, confidence in the conclusion grows; when they disagree, the gap itself becomes something to explain. Triangulation is a key way to build credibility, one of the trustworthiness criteria of Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985), and it is central to mixed-methods research. Example: a researcher studying stress among nurses interviews nurses about their experiences, observes ward routines during night shifts and gives a standard stress questionnaire. If all three show that understaffing drives stress, the finding is far more convincing than any one source alone. Option c is right because combining methods so that each set of results checks the others is exactly what triangulation means. Exam trap: despite the 'tri', triangulation does not require exactly three methods; two or more sources or methods are enough.", "topic": "Triangulation in research"}, {"q": "Which is the correct order of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, from the base of the pyramid to the top?", "options": {"a": "Physiological, love and belongingness, safety, esteem, self-actualisation", "b": "Safety, physiological, esteem, love and belongingness, self-actualisation", "c": "Physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, self-actualisation", "d": "Physiological, safety, esteem, love and belongingness, self-actualisation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: from base to top, Maslow's hierarchy runs physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, self-actualisation. Abraham Maslow (1943) set out the hierarchy in 'A Theory of Human Motivation' and developed it in Motivation and Personality (1954). His central idea is prepotency: lower needs are more urgent, and higher needs become strong motivators only when the ones below are reasonably, though not completely, satisfied. Physiological needs (food, water, sleep, warmth) come first. Next are safety needs (security, order, freedom from fear), then love and belongingness needs (friends, family, acceptance in a group), then esteem needs (self-respect, competence and recognition from others). At the top is self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming. Maslow called the lower four deficiency needs (D-needs), because their absence creates a tension we try to reduce, and called the growth motives at the top being needs (B-needs). Later he also wrote of cognitive and aesthetic needs and, near the end of his life, of self-transcendence. Example: a man who has just lost his home in a flood thinks first about food and water, then about a safe place to stay; only once these are settled does he miss his friends, then care about his standing at work, and later about painting again. Option c is right because it is the only option with the five needs in the correct order. Exam trap: students often swap esteem and belongingness; remember 'Please Stop Licking Every Sweet': physiological, safety, love, esteem, self-actualisation.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs: order"}, {"q": "A toddler carries one particular soft blanket everywhere and cannot fall asleep without it. Donald Winnicott called such a thing a:", "options": {"a": "part object", "b": "transitional object", "c": "secure base", "d": "selfobject"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Winnicott called such a thing a transitional object. Donald Winnicott (1953), a British paediatrician and psychoanalyst, described it in his paper 'Transitional Objects and Transitional Phenomena'. A transitional object is the infant's first 'not-me' possession: a blanket, a soft toy or the corner of a sheet, chosen by the child and treated as uniquely precious. It belongs to an intermediate area of experience between inner reality (the baby's sense that the mother is part of himself) and outer reality (the discovery that she is a separate person who comes and goes). The object stands in for the comfort of the mother, so it helps the child tolerate separation, cope with anxiety, especially at bedtime, and move gradually towards independence. The child insists that it must not be changed, may both cuddle and maul it, and in time it simply loses its meaning rather than being mourned. Winnicott saw this as a normal, healthy step, made possible by a 'good-enough mother' who provides a reliable holding environment and then lets the infant face small, bearable frustrations. He traced the same intermediate space into later play, creativity and culture. Example: Linus in the Peanuts comic strip, who drags his blanket everywhere, is the best-known picture of a transitional object. Option b is right because a special soft item that soothes the toddler in the mother's absence is exactly Winnicott's concept. Exam trap: a secure base also gives comfort, but in attachment theory it is the caregiver, a person, not an object.", "topic": "Winnicott's transitional object"}, {"q": "Research carried out by practitioners to solve a problem in their own setting, in repeated cycles of planning, acting, observing and reflecting, is called ______ and was introduced by ______.", "options": {"a": "action research; Kurt Lewin", "b": "basic research; Wilhelm Wundt", "c": "ex post facto research; Fred Kerlinger", "d": "evaluation research; Donald Campbell"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: this is action research, introduced by Kurt Lewin. Kurt Lewin (1946), the German-American founder of modern social psychology, set out the idea in 'Action Research and Minority Problems'. He wanted research that would not just describe social problems, such as prejudice or poor working conditions, but help solve them, and he described it as a spiral of steps: plan, take action, find out what happened, then plan again. Later writers in education, notably Stephen Kemmis, turned this into the familiar cycle of planning, acting, observing and reflecting. Its defining features are that the practitioners themselves (teachers, nurses, managers, community members) do the research in their own setting, the aim is practical change rather than general laws, and the process repeats until the problem improves. When the people affected are full partners at every stage, it is called participatory action research. The trade-off is that findings are tied to one local setting and are hard to generalise, and the researcher is not a neutral outsider. Example: a school counsellor notices that many students skip the first class of the day, plans a peer-mentoring scheme, runs it for a month, tracks attendance, reflects with the teachers on what worked, and then revises the scheme for the next cycle. Option a is right because practitioner-led cycles of planning, acting, observing and reflecting are what Lewin named action research. Exam trap: evaluation research also judges whether a programme works, but it is usually done by outside evaluators to deliver a verdict, not by practitioners in repeated improvement cycles.", "topic": "Action research (Kurt Lewin)"}, {"q": "In Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant is extremely upset when the mother leaves; when she returns, the infant clings to her but also pushes her away angrily and cannot be comforted. This attachment pattern is:", "options": {"a": "secure", "b": "insecure-avoidant", "c": "disorganised", "d": "insecure-resistant (ambivalent)"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: this is the insecure-resistant (ambivalent) attachment pattern. Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978) reported in Patterns of Attachment the results of the Strange Situation, a laboratory procedure for infants of about 12 to 18 months. It has eight short episodes in which the mother and a stranger come and go, and observers note exploration, distress at separation and, above all, behaviour at reunion. In the original Baltimore samples, about 65 to 70% of infants were secure (Type B): they used the mother as a secure base, were upset when she left and were quickly comforted when she returned. About 20% were insecure-avoidant (Type A): little distress, and they ignored or turned away from the mother at reunion. About 10 to 15% were insecure-resistant or ambivalent (Type C): clingy and reluctant to explore even with the mother present, extremely distressed at separation, and at reunion they sought contact but also resisted it, arching away, hitting or pushing, and could not be settled. Ainsworth linked this pattern to inconsistent caregiving, warm at some times and unavailable at others. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986, 1990) later added a disorganised pattern (Type D). Example: an infant cries hard when the mother leaves, reaches for her on return, then pushes her away angrily and keeps crying. Option d is right because clinging combined with angry resistance and failure to be soothed defines resistant attachment. Exam trap: disorganised infants also behave oddly, but they lack any coherent strategy, freezing or approaching with the face turned away.", "topic": "Insecure-resistant (ambivalent) attachment"}, {"q": "Which psychoanalyst rejected Sigmund Freud's concept of penis envy, arguing that women envy men's social power and that men may feel 'womb envy'?", "options": {"a": "Karen Horney", "b": "Melanie Klein", "c": "Anna Freud", "d": "Carl Jung"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Karen Horney rejected Sigmund Freud's concept of penis envy. Freud held that a girl who discovers she lacks a penis feels inferior and envious, and that this envy shapes female personality. Karen Horney (1885 to 1952), a German-born psychoanalyst who later worked in the United States, challenged this in essays written between 1922 and 1937, such as \"The Flight from Womanhood\" (1926); they were collected after her death as Feminine Psychology (1967). She argued that what women envy is not the organ but the social power, freedom and status that men enjoy in a male-dominated culture. She also turned the argument around: men may feel womb envy, envy of women's capacity for pregnancy, birth and motherhood, and may compensate through achievement. For Horney, culture, not anatomy, shapes the psychology of women. Her wider theory rests on basic anxiety, a child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a hostile world, which the person meets by moving toward, against or away from people. She also contrasted the real self with an idealised self that imposes a \"tyranny of the shoulds\". Example: a girl who wishes she were a boy because her brothers may study, travel and choose careers while she may not is, on Horney's view, envying their privileges, not their anatomy. Option a is right because Horney replaced penis envy with envy of men's social power and proposed womb envy in return. Exam trap: Carl Jung also broke with Freud, but his link to this topic is the term Electra complex, not a feminist critique.", "topic": "Horney's critique of penis envy"}, {"q": "Children talking aloud to themselves while working on a task was called ______ by Lev Vygotsky, who saw it as a tool for guiding their own behaviour, and ______ by Jean Piaget, who saw it as a sign of cognitive immaturity.", "options": {"a": "holophrastic speech; private speech", "b": "egocentric speech; private speech", "c": "private speech; telegraphic speech", "d": "private speech; egocentric speech"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Lev Vygotsky called it private speech and Jean Piaget called it egocentric speech. Preschool children often talk aloud to themselves while drawing, building or solving puzzles. The two great developmental theorists, both born in 1896, read this habit in opposite ways. Jean Piaget, in The Language and Thought of the Child (1923), called it egocentric speech: talk not adapted to a listener, seen in repetition, monologue and collective monologue (children \"talking together\" without really answering each other). For him it reflected the child's inability to take another person's viewpoint, and it simply faded as the child decentred. Lev Vygotsky, in Thinking and Speech (1934; translated as Thought and Language), called it private speech. He argued that speech begins as social communication, then turns back on the self to guide one's own actions, and finally goes underground as silent inner speech, a tool of thought. Laura Berk's research supports Vygotsky: children use more private speech when a task is harder, and it tends to go with better performance. Example: a five-year-old doing a jigsaw mutters, \"No, the corner piece goes here, then the blue one\"; she is steering her own behaviour, as Vygotsky claimed. Option d is right because it puts private speech with Vygotsky and egocentric speech with Piaget, in the order the stem asks. Exam trap: option b names the same two terms in reverse order, so check which theorist each blank refers to before choosing.", "topic": "Private speech (Vygotsky) vs egocentric speech (Piaget)"}, {"q": "An adult has no memory at all of a frightening childhood event; the memory was pushed out of awareness without any conscious effort. Which defence mechanism is this?", "options": {"a": "Suppression", "b": "Repression", "c": "Denial", "d": "Displacement"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: this is repression. Repression is the unconscious, motivated forgetting of threatening thoughts, memories or wishes: the ego pushes them out of awareness without the person knowing it is doing so. Sigmund Freud, who gave it a paper of its own, \"Repression\" (1915), called it the cornerstone on which the whole structure of psychoanalysis rests, and saw it as the primary defence on which the others build. His daughter Anna Freud set out the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), repression among them. A repressed memory does not vanish. It stays active in the unconscious and may return in disguised form through dreams, slips of the tongue or symptoms, which is why keeping it down costs psychic energy. Defences differ in whether they are conscious and in what they do with the threat. Repression blocks an inner memory or impulse unconsciously. Suppression blocks it consciously. Denial refuses to accept an outer fact, and displacement shifts an impulse onto a safer target. Example: a woman who nearly drowned at the age of five cannot recall the event at all, yet feels unexplained dread near deep water. Option b is right because the memory was removed from awareness without conscious effort, which is the defining feature of repression. Exam trap: suppression and repression both keep material out of mind, but only suppression is deliberate; George Vaillant even ranks suppression among the mature, healthy defences.", "topic": "Repression vs suppression"}, {"q": "Aaron Beck's cognitive triad of depression consists of negative views of:", "options": {"a": "the body, the mind and the spirit", "b": "the past, the present and the future", "c": "the self, the world and the future", "d": "parents, peers and teachers"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad consists of negative views of the self, the world and the future. Aaron T. Beck, an American psychiatrist, set out his cognitive theory of depression in Depression (1967) and built a treatment on it in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). He argued that depression is driven less by mood or unconscious anger than by how the person thinks. Early experiences lay down negative schemas, core beliefs such as \"I am unlovable\", which stress later activates. These produce automatic negative thoughts and cognitive distortions, including arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. Their content falls into three areas, the cognitive triad: the self (\"I am worthless\"), the world or present experience (\"nothing goes right for me\") and the future (\"it will never get better\"). Example: a student who fails one test thinks \"I am stupid\" (self), \"my teachers are against me\" (world) and \"I will never get a seat anywhere\" (future). Related accounts are Martin Seligman's learned helplessness and the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989). Option c is right because self, world and future are the three targets of negative thinking in Beck's model. Exam trap: the future is in the triad, but the past and present are not separate parts; \"world\" is the person's present experience. Key note: the recall list writes the triad as \"hopelessness, helplessness and worthlessness\". That is the clinical way of saying the same thing: worthless (self), helpless (world) and hopeless (future), so if those three words appear as an option, pick it.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad of depression"}, {"q": "A detective who decided early that the butler is guilty notices only the clues that point to the butler and ignores the rest. This is:", "options": {"a": "the fundamental attribution error", "b": "hindsight bias", "c": "confirmation bias", "d": "the self-serving bias"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: this is confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice, interpret and remember evidence that supports what we already believe, while overlooking or explaining away evidence against it. The British psychologist Peter Wason showed it with his 2-4-6 task (1960). Participants were told that the numbers 2, 4, 6 fitted a rule and had to find the rule by proposing new sets. Most guessed \"rising by two\" and tested only sets that fitted their guess, such as 8, 10, 12, so they never discovered that the real rule was simply any increasing numbers. Wason's four-card selection task (1966) showed the same failure to look for disconfirming cases. Once a hypothesis is formed, people treat agreeing facts as proof and ignore the rest, which makes beliefs, first impressions and stereotypes very resistant to change. Example: a clinician who decides in the first five minutes that a client has depression may ask only about low mood and miss signs of a thyroid problem. In the stem, the detective's early belief that the butler is guilty steers what he notices. Option c is right because he attends only to clues that confirm his existing belief and ignores those that do not. Exam trap: hindsight bias also distorts judgement, but it works after the outcome is known (\"I knew it all along\"), while confirmation bias works while evidence is still being gathered.", "topic": "Confirmation bias"}, {"q": "Checking WhatsApp for a reply that may arrive at any unpredictable moment is reinforced on which schedule?", "options": {"a": "Fixed ratio", "b": "Variable interval", "c": "Fixed interval", "d": "Variable ratio"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: checking WhatsApp for an unpredictable reply is reinforced on a variable interval schedule. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses are rewarded. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner described them in detail in Schedules of Reinforcement (1957). Ratio schedules reward a number of responses; interval schedules reward the first response after a period of time has passed. Each can be fixed (the same every time) or variable (changing around an average). On a variable interval schedule, the first response after an unpredictable amount of time is reinforced. Because the reward could become available at any moment, the learner keeps responding at a slow, steady rate, without the pauses seen on fixed schedules, and the behaviour is highly resistant to extinction. A fixed interval schedule, by contrast, produces a scallop: little responding just after a reward and a burst as the expected time nears. Classic variable interval examples are pop quizzes, fishing and checking email. Example: a student who expects a friend to reply some time this evening glances at the phone every few minutes; checking more often does not make the message arrive sooner, but the first check after it arrives is rewarded. Option b is right because the reply depends on unpredictable time passing, not on how many times you check. Exam trap: variable ratio sounds right because the reward is unpredictable, but on a ratio schedule more responses bring the reward sooner, as with a slot machine; here they do not.", "topic": "Variable interval schedule of reinforcement"}, {"q": "A stereotype is best defined as:", "options": {"a": "unfair behaviour towards members of a group", "b": "a negative feeling towards members of a group", "c": "a generalised belief about the characteristics of members of a group", "d": "a personal opinion based on one direct experience"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: a stereotype is a generalised belief about the characteristics of members of a group. The word comes from printing, where a stereotype was a fixed metal plate; the American journalist Walter Lippmann gave it its modern meaning in Public Opinion (1922), calling stereotypes the \"pictures in our heads\". Gordon Allport, in The Nature of Prejudice (1954), showed how such beliefs grow out of the normal habit of sorting people into categories. A stereotype assigns the same traits to every member of a group, whatever their individual differences. It can be positive or negative and is often shared across a culture. Social psychologists separate three linked terms with the ABC model: the stereotype is the cognitive part (a belief), prejudice is the affective part (a feeling, usually negative) and discrimination is the behavioural part (unfair action). Daniel Katz and Kenneth Braly (1933) first measured stereotypes by asking Princeton students to pick adjectives from a checklist for different ethnic groups. Later, Claude Steele and Joshua Aronson (1995) showed stereotype threat: fear of confirming a negative stereotype can lower a person's test performance. Example: \"all accountants are boring\" is a stereotype; disliking accountants is prejudice; refusing to invite one to a party is discrimination. Option c is right because a stereotype is a belief, a cognitive generalisation about a group, not a feeling or an action. Exam trap: options a and b describe discrimination and prejudice, the behavioural and affective partners of the stereotype, so match each term to its part of the ABC model.", "topic": "Stereotype vs prejudice vs discrimination"}, {"q": "The book 'Caste, Prejudice and the Individual' (1970), a classic of Indian social psychology, was written by:", "options": {"a": "Durganand Sinha", "b": "Udai Pareek", "c": "Anand C. Paranjpe", "d": "Jai B. P. Sinha"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the book was written by Anand C. Paranjpe. Caste, Prejudice and the Individual was published by Lalvani in Bombay in 1970. Prejudice is a negative attitude towards people because of the group they belong to. Much classic Western work, such as Gordon Allport's The Nature of Prejudice (1954), studied it through race and religion. Paranjpe turned to caste, India's own deep-rooted system of social grouping, and studied caste attitudes among Indian students. As the title suggests, he looked at prejudice at the level of the individual person, not only at society as a whole. This made the book an early classic of Indian social psychology. Paranjpe, who later taught at Simon Fraser University in Canada, went on to bridge Western and Indian thought in Theoretical Psychology: The Meeting of East and West (1984) and Self and Identity in Modern Psychology and Indian Thought (1998). He also co-edited the Handbook of Indian Psychology (2008) with K. Ramakrishna Rao and Ajit K. Dalal. Example of caste prejudice: a student who feels uneasy sharing a room or a meal with a classmate from another caste, though the two have never quarrelled, holds an attitude based on group membership, not on personal experience. Option c is right because Paranjpe is the author of this 1970 study. Exam trap: all four options are leading Indian psychologists, so link each name to one landmark: Durganand Sinha with indigenisation, Udai Pareek with organisational roles and OCTAPACE, and J. B. P. Sinha with the nurturant-task leader.", "topic": "Paranjpe: Caste, Prejudice and the Individual"}, {"q": "Caffeine is classified as a ______ and works mainly by ______.", "options": {"a": "opioid; activating mu receptors", "b": "depressant; enhancing GABA activity", "c": "hallucinogen; stimulating serotonin 5-HT2A receptors", "d": "stimulant; blocking adenosine receptors"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: caffeine is a stimulant that works mainly by blocking adenosine receptors. Caffeine, found in coffee, tea, cola and chocolate, is the most widely used psychoactive drug in the world; chemically it is a methylxanthine. To understand it, start with adenosine. Adenosine is a chemical messenger that builds up in the brain the longer we stay awake. When it binds to its receptors, mainly the A1 and A2A types, it dampens neural activity and creates the pressure to sleep. Caffeine has a similar shape, so it fits those receptors without activating them. It is an antagonist: it blocks adenosine and removes the brake, so alertness rises and fatigue falls. Because adenosine normally restrains dopamine and other transmitters, these become more active too, which explains caffeine's mild lift in mood. Regular users develop tolerance, and stopping suddenly brings withdrawal. DSM-5 (2013) made caffeine withdrawal an official diagnosis, and DSM-5-TR lists both caffeine intoxication (restlessness, insomnia, racing heart after large doses) and caffeine withdrawal, whose hallmark is headache, with tiredness and poor concentration. Example: a student who drinks three cups of coffee a day during exam season feels alert while studying but gets a pounding headache on the first coffee-free Sunday. Option d is right because caffeine is a central nervous system stimulant whose main action is adenosine receptor blockade. Exam trap: caffeine does raise dopamine activity, but only indirectly; blocking dopamine reuptake is cocaine's mechanism, and releasing dopamine is amphetamine's.", "topic": "Caffeine: a stimulant that blocks adenosine"}, {"q": "In schizophrenia, loosening of associations (derailment) is classified as a:", "options": {"a": "formal thought disorder, a disorder of the form of thought", "b": "Schneiderian first-rank symptom", "c": "disorder of the content of thought, like a delusion", "d": "perceptual disturbance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: loosening of associations is a formal thought disorder, a disorder of the form of thought. Psychopathology divides thinking problems into form and content. Form is how thoughts are linked and expressed; content is what is believed. Eugen Bleuler, the Swiss psychiatrist who coined the word schizophrenia, treated loosening of associations as the central fundamental symptom in Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias (1911); it heads the later \"four A's\": associations, affect, ambivalence and autism. In loosening, the logical links between ideas break down, so speech slides from one idea to another with no clear connection. Other formal thought disorders include derailment, tangentiality, incoherence or word salad, neologisms and poverty of content, which Nancy Andreasen's Thought, Language and Communication (TLC) scale (1979) rates. Content disorders, by contrast, are mainly delusions. Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms (1959) are a different list: audible thoughts, voices arguing or commenting, somatic passivity, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, made feelings, impulses and acts, and delusional perception. Example: asked about breakfast, a patient says, \"I had tea, tea grows in Assam, Assam is a state of mind, minds are watched by satellites\"; each step links loosely to the last, but the whole has no goal. Option a is right because loosening is a disturbance in how thoughts are connected, which defines formal thought disorder. Exam trap: thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting sound like thinking problems, but they are Schneiderian passivity experiences; loosening of associations is not a first-rank symptom.", "topic": "Loosening of associations: formal thought disorder"}, {"q": "A new depression scale is given to patients on the same day that a psychiatrist diagnoses them, and the scale scores agree closely with the diagnoses. This is evidence of the scale's:", "options": {"a": "content validity", "b": "predictive validity", "c": "concurrent validity", "d": "test-retest reliability"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: this is evidence of the scale's concurrent validity. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. One way to check is criterion-related validity: compare test scores with an outside standard, the criterion, that already reflects the trait. The American Psychological Association's Technical Recommendations for Psychological Tests and Diagnostic Techniques (1954) listed concurrent and predictive validity as separate types; later standards grouped them together as criterion-related validity. The difference is timing. In concurrent validity, the criterion is measured at about the same time as the test, so the question is whether the test can stand in for, or diagnose, the present state. In predictive validity, the criterion is measured later, so the question is whether the test forecasts the future, as when entrance scores are checked against later grades. Both are usually expressed as a validity coefficient, the correlation between test and criterion. Example: a new 15-minute anxiety checklist is given to clinic patients on the same day that clinicians rate their anxiety in full interviews; if scores and ratings correlate highly, the quick checklist has concurrent validity and could save clinical time. In the stem, the depression scale is checked against a psychiatrist's diagnosis made on the same day. Option c is right because the criterion, the psychiatrist's diagnosis, was obtained at the same time as the test scores. Exam trap: predictive validity also uses a criterion, so the deciding clue is timing: same day means concurrent, later means predictive.", "topic": "Concurrent validity"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a determinant in Rorschach scoring?", "options": {"a": "Human movement", "b": "Form", "c": "Animal movement", "d": "Space"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: space is not a determinant in Rorschach scoring. The Rorschach test, published by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in Psychodiagnostik (1921), uses ten inkblot cards; the person says what each blot might be, and an inquiry then finds out where and why. Each response is scored in several separate categories. Location records which part of the blot was used: W for the whole blot, D for a common detail, Dd for an unusual detail and S for the white space. Determinants record which feature of the blot shaped the response: F for form, M for human movement, FM for animal movement, m for inanimate movement, colour (FC, CF, C), achromatic colour (C'), shading (texture T, vista V, diffuse Y), form dimension (FD) and pairs or reflections. Content records what was seen (human, animal, anatomy), and further codes cover popular responses and form quality. John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) brought these codes together into the standard scoring method. Example: \"two people dancing\" seen in the whole card is scored W for location and M for determinant; \"a lake\" seen in the white centre is scored S for location, with F if its outline decided the answer. Option d is right because space (S) tells us where on the card the person looked, which makes it a location code, not a determinant. Exam trap: S looks like just another scoring letter next to F, M and FM, but it belongs to location; keep location, determinants and content as three separate questions.", "topic": "Rorschach scoring: location vs determinants"}, {"q": "Which method is used for memorising a poem?", "options": {"a": "Distributed practice", "b": "Serial memorising", "c": "Syntactic memorising", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: distributed practice is the method used for memorising a poem. Distributed (spaced) practice means spreading study over several shorter sessions with breaks in between; massed practice means doing it all in one long sitting, as in cramming. Hermann Ebbinghaus, the German pioneer who tested his own memory with nonsense syllables, reported in Memory (1885) that learning spread over days needed fewer total repetitions than learning crammed into one session. This is the spacing effect, one of the most reliable findings in memory research. Spacing works partly because each new session makes the learner retrieve material that is starting to fade, which strengthens it, and partly because fatigue and boredom are reduced. Textbooks also discuss whole versus part learning. In the whole method the learner reads the entire poem through each time; in the part method it is learnt verse by verse; in the progressive part method each new verse is added to those already learnt. The whole method suits a short, meaningful poem and the part method a long one. Example: a student who reads a poem for 15 minutes a day for four days will usually remember it longer than one who reads it for an hour the night before. Option a is right because distributed practice is the evidence-based method among the four choices. Exam trap: \"none of the above\" tempts students who expect whole or part learning, but distributed practice is a genuine method. Key note: the options are as the recall list remembers them; \"serial memorising\" and \"syntactic memorising\" are not standard method names, so distributed practice is the safe pick.", "topic": "Distributed practice and memorising a poem"}, {"q": "Watching a disturbing film alone, Japanese and American students showed the same facial expressions of disgust; when an experimenter sat with them, the Japanese students masked their expressions with polite smiles. This finding illustrates:", "options": {"a": "the James-Lange theory of emotion", "b": "that facial expressions of emotion are entirely learned", "c": "the facial feedback hypothesis", "d": "cultural display rules"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the finding illustrates cultural display rules. Display rules are learned social norms that tell members of a culture when, where and how strongly an emotion may be shown. Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen introduced the term in 1969. Their neurocultural theory holds that a set of basic facial expressions is universal and biologically wired, while display rules, which are cultural, govern how those expressions are managed in public. Ekman's six basic emotions are happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust. The study in the stem was run by Wallace Friesen (1972) and reported by Paul Ekman (1972). Japanese and American students watched stressful film clips. When they watched alone, both groups made the same expressions of disgust and fear. When an experimenter sat with them, the Japanese students more often masked negative expressions with polite smiles. Japan is a collectivist culture, and as Hazel Markus and Shinobu Kitayama (1991) argued, such cultures stress group harmony, so showing negative feelings in front of others, especially a higher-status person, is discouraged. Example: an Indian bride may be expected to look sad when leaving her parents' home, even if she feels happy; the display rule shapes the face, not the feeling. Option d is right because the same inner emotion produced different public expressions depending on the social setting, which is exactly what display rules predict. Exam trap: the study shows both universality and cultural shaping, so do not pick \"entirely learned\" just because a cultural difference appeared.", "topic": "Cultural display rules (Ekman and Friesen)"}, {"q": "A man vanishes from his home town and is found weeks later working in another city under a new name, with no memory of his past life. In DSM-5-TR this is diagnosed as:", "options": {"a": "depersonalisation-derealisation disorder", "b": "dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue", "c": "dissociative identity disorder", "d": "conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: in DSM-5-TR this is diagnosed as dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue. Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information, usually about traumatic or stressful events, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not caused by a drug or brain disease. A fugue (from the Latin for \"flight\") adds apparently purposeful travel or bewildered wandering linked to the amnesia; sometimes the person takes on a new identity. DSM-IV (1994) listed dissociative fugue as a separate disorder, but DSM-5 (2013) folded it into dissociative amnesia as a specifier, and DSM-5-TR (2022) keeps that. ICD-11 likewise codes dissociative amnesia (6B61) with a \"with dissociative fugue\" form. A fugue usually follows severe stress such as war, disaster, debt or marital crisis. It may last hours to months; recovery is often sudden, and the person then remembers the past life but has amnesia for the fugue period. The classic case is Ansel Bourne, an American preacher who in 1887 vanished from Rhode Island and lived for about two months as a shopkeeper called A. J. Brown in Pennsylvania; William James later studied him. Option b is right because the man has travelled away, lost his personal past and taken a new name, which is dissociative amnesia with fugue. Exam trap: students who learnt DSM-IV look for \"dissociative fugue\" as its own disorder, and others pick dissociative identity disorder because of the new name; in a fugue the new identity replaces the old one rather than alternating with it.", "topic": "Dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue"}, {"q": "Social identity theory was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Henri Tajfel and John Turner", "b": "Leon Festinger", "c": "Muzafer Sherif", "d": "Fritz Heider"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: social identity theory was proposed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner. Henri Tajfel, a Polish-born British social psychologist, and his former student John Turner set out the theory in \"An integrative theory of intergroup conflict\" (1979). Its central idea is that the self-concept has two parts: a personal identity, built from individual traits, and a social identity, built from the groups we belong to, such as nation, religion, caste, college or team. Because people want to feel good about themselves, they want their groups to look good too. They therefore seek positive distinctiveness: they compare their in-group with out-groups on dimensions where the in-group comes out ahead. This produces in-group favouritism and at times prejudice, even when the groups are not competing for anything real. The theory grew out of Tajfel's minimal group experiments (1971), which showed that simply being placed in a group was enough to make people favour it. Turner and colleagues later extended it into self-categorisation theory (1987). Example: after India wins a cricket match, a fan says \"we won\", feels proud and may mock the rival team's supporters; part of his self-esteem rides on the group. Option a is right because Tajfel and Turner jointly authored social identity theory. Exam trap: Muzafer Sherif also explained intergroup conflict, but through competition for scarce resources (realistic conflict theory); Tajfel and Turner showed that group membership alone can produce bias.", "topic": "Social identity theory: Tajfel and Turner"}, {"q": "In Henri Tajfel's 'minimal group' experiments, schoolboys split into groups on a trivial basis (preferring paintings by Klee or Kandinsky) then, when sharing out rewards:", "options": {"a": "favoured the other group to appear generous", "b": "shared the rewards equally between the groups", "c": "favoured their own group, even giving up a larger total so their group got more than the other", "d": "refused to allocate rewards to anyone they did not know"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the boys favoured their own group, even giving up a larger total so that their group got more than the other. Henri Tajfel, Michael Billig, R. P. Bundy and Claude Flament (1971) devised the minimal group paradigm to find the least that is needed to produce in-group bias. Bristol schoolboys aged 14 and 15 were split into groups on trivial grounds; in one version, by whether they preferred paintings by Paul Klee or Wassily Kandinsky. The groups were \"minimal\": members never met, did not know who else was in their group, and had no history of conflict and nothing to compete for. Each boy then shared out rewards between two other boys, known only by code number and group, using reward matrices. He never allocated to himself, so self-interest was ruled out. Fairness pulled choices towards the middle, but there was a clear, consistent tilt towards the in-group. Most striking was the maximum difference strategy: boys often accepted less for their own group as long as it beat the out-group. Example: offered 13 points for an in-group boy and 13 for an out-group boy, or 9 and 5, a boy using maximum difference takes 9 and 5. Mere categorisation was enough for discrimination, so real conflict over resources, as in Muzafer Sherif's theory, is not necessary. Option c is right because the boys valued beating the out-group above maximising their own group's reward. Exam trap: option b tempts because fairness played a part, but the key result was in-group favouritism.", "topic": "Tajfel's minimal group experiments"}, {"q": "According to social identity theory, the three mental processes through which a person comes to see himself or herself as a group member occur in which order?", "options": {"a": "Social comparison, social categorisation, social identification", "b": "Social categorisation, social identification, social comparison", "c": "Social identification, social comparison, social categorisation", "d": "Social identification, social categorisation, social comparison"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the order is social categorisation, social identification, social comparison. Social identity theory, proposed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979), explains how belonging to groups becomes part of who we are. It describes three mental processes that follow one another. First comes social categorisation: we sort people, including ourselves, into social categories such as Hindu or Muslim, student or teacher, Mumbai Indians fan or Chennai Super Kings fan. Categories simplify the social world and tell us which group we belong to. Second comes social identification: we take on the identity of the group we have placed ourselves in, adopt its norms and feel emotionally tied to it, so our self-esteem becomes bound up with the group's standing. Third comes social comparison: to keep that self-esteem high, we compare our in-group with out-groups in ways that favour our own, seeking positive distinctiveness. This last step is where in-group favouritism, rivalry and prejudice can arise. Turner and colleagues later developed the categorisation step into self-categorisation theory (1987). Example: a new student at a medical college notices that classmates are either hostel residents or day scholars (categorisation), joins the hostel and starts saying \"we hostellers\" (identification), and finally claims hostellers work harder than day scholars (comparison). Option b is right because a person must first categorise, then identify with a group, and only then compare it with others. Exam trap: social comparison is a familiar term from Leon Festinger (1954), so students may put it first, but in this theory it comes last.", "topic": "Social identity theory: the three processes"}, {"q": "In environmental psychology, anthropocentrism refers to:", "options": {"a": "the scientific study of human cultures and societies", "b": "the view that human beings are the most important beings, with nature valued mainly for its use to humans", "c": "giving animals or objects human-like thoughts and feelings", "d": "the belief that one's own culture is superior to others"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: anthropocentrism is the view that human beings are the most important beings, with nature valued mainly for its use to humans. The word comes from the Greek anthropos, \"human being\", and means human-centred. Environmental psychology studies how people and their physical surroundings affect each other, including why people do or do not protect nature. Suzanne Thompson and Michelle Barton (1994) distinguished two reasons for caring about the environment. Anthropocentric people support conservation because nature serves human interests: clean air and water, food, medicines, comfort and enjoyment. Ecocentric people value nature for its own sake, believing that plants, animals and ecosystems deserve protection whether or not humans benefit. Both groups may back the same green action, but for different reasons. Thompson and Barton also measured a third outlook, environmental apathy, a lack of interest in environmental issues. Example: two villagers oppose cutting down a forest. One says, \"We need it for firewood, rain and tourism\", which is anthropocentric. The other says, \"The forest and its tigers have a right to exist\", which is ecocentric. Option b is right because anthropocentrism places humans at the centre and treats nature as valuable mainly for what it gives people. Exam trap: anthropocentrism and anthropomorphism share the Greek root anthropos, but anthropomorphism means giving human traits to animals or objects, which is a different idea.", "topic": "Anthropocentrism vs ecocentrism"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"cip-2023": [{"q": "How many items does the MMPI-2-RF (Restructured Form, 2008) contain?", "concept": "The answer is b: the MMPI-2-RF contains 338 items. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory began with Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), who built its scales by empirical keying: an item was kept if patients in a diagnostic group answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The revision by James Butcher and colleagues (1989), the MMPI-2, has 567 true-false items. Its old clinical scales overlap heavily because they share a general distress factor, which Auke Tellegen and colleagues (2003) called demoralisation. They removed it and built the Restructured Clinical (RC) scales, such as RC2 (low positive emotions) and RC4 (antisocial behaviour). Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen (2008) then chose the 338 MMPI-2 items needed to score the new scales and published them as the MMPI-2-RF (Restructured Form). It is shorter and faster, yet keeps validity scales that check how the person answered: random or fixed responding, over-reporting and under-reporting. Example: a client who marks \"true\" to almost every item raises TRIN-r, warning that the clinical profile cannot be trusted. Option b is right because 338 is the published item count of the MMPI-2-RF. Exam trap: all four numbers belong to the same family; 567 is the MMPI-2, 478 the MMPI-A for adolescents (1992) and 335 the MMPI-3 (2020). Key note: the recall list remembered this item as \"338 items and 4 validity scales\". The 338 is right, but the MMPI-2-RF has nine validity scales: eight in 2008 (VRIN-r, TRIN-r, F-r, Fp-r, Fs, FBS-r, L-r, K-r) plus RBS, added in 2011.", "traps": {"a": "567 is the item count of the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989), the full adult revision from which the 338 MMPI-2-RF items were drawn, so it is the parent test, not the RF.", "c": "478 is the item count of the MMPI-A (1992), the adolescent version for ages 14 to 18, not the restructured adult form published by Ben-Porath and Tellegen in 2008.", "d": "335 is the item count of the MMPI-3 (2020), the newest adult form by Ben-Porath and Tellegen, which grew out of the MMPI-2-RF with new norms and some new scales; it is a close-number distractor."}}, {"q": "In a one-way ANOVA, the null hypothesis that all group means are equal is rejected only when the F ratio is:", "concept": "The answer is c: in a one-way ANOVA the null hypothesis is rejected only when F is large, in the right tail of the F distribution. Analysis of variance was developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, and George Snedecor named the F ratio after him in 1934. ANOVA asks whether three or more group means differ by splitting the total variability into two parts. The between-groups mean square measures how far the group means spread around the grand mean; the within-groups mean square measures ordinary error inside each group. F is their ratio: MS between divided by MS within. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance, so F hovers around 1. If the means really differ, the numerator grows and F rises well above 1. Because both mean squares are variances, F can never be negative. Example: three teaching methods give mean scores of 50, 60 and 70 with little spread inside each class of ten. MS between is large, F might be 9.0, and with 2 and 27 degrees of freedom that far exceeds the 0.05 critical value of about 3.35, so H0 is rejected. Option c is right because only a big F shows that group means differ more than chance allows. Exam trap: an F far below 1 is not evidence of a difference; it only means the group means are unusually close together. Key note: the recall list remembers this item as \"the F test is essentially a two tailed test\". ANOVA's alternative hypothesis is non-directional, which is why some books loosely call it two-tailed in spirit, but any difference among means makes F bigger, so the rejection region lies only in the right tail. See also the next F question in this set.", "traps": {"a": "An F far below 1 means the group means are closer together than chance would predict; it is never grounds for rejecting the null hypothesis, so the left tail is not a rejection region in ANOVA.", "b": "F is a ratio of two variances, each a sum of squares divided by its degrees of freedom, so it can never be negative; negative values are possible for t and z, not for F.", "d": "Rejecting in both tails describes a two-tailed t or z test, or the F test comparing two variances; in ANOVA only a large F, in the upper tail, counts against the null hypothesis."}}, {"q": "After both medial temporal lobes were removed in 1953, patient H.M. could no longer form new long-term declarative memories. The structure mainly responsible for this loss was the:", "concept": "The answer is a: the hippocampus was mainly responsible for H.M.'s loss of new long-term declarative memory. Henry Molaison (1926 to 2008), known in the literature as H.M., had severe epilepsy. In 1953 the neurosurgeon William Scoville removed the medial temporal lobes on both sides, including most of the hippocampus, the amygdala and nearby cortex. The seizures came under control, but H.M. was left with profound anterograde amnesia: he could not form new conscious memories of facts or events. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) described the case, and Milner went on to study him for years. His short-term memory was normal, so he could hold a number in mind by rehearsing it, but once distracted the information was gone. Many older memories survived, showing that the hippocampus is needed to consolidate new declarative memories rather than to store all old ones. Example: H.M. could meet the same researcher many times and still treat each visit as a first meeting. Yet Milner taught him mirror drawing, tracing a star seen only in a mirror, and he improved day after day while saying he had never done the task before. That spared procedural learning depends on other circuits. Option a is right because the hippocampus consolidates new facts and events into long-term memory, and its loss caused his amnesia. Exam trap: the amygdala was removed too, but it handles emotional memory and fear learning, not the general laying down of new facts and events.", "traps": {"b": "The amygdala was also removed in H.M.'s operation, but it serves emotional memory and fear conditioning; damage to it alone does not cause a global loss of new declarative memories.", "c": "The cerebellum supports motor learning and timing, such as eyeblink conditioning and skill learning; H.M.'s steady gains in mirror drawing show that his procedural systems, including the cerebellum, still worked.", "d": "The basal ganglia support procedural learning and habits; they were not removed in H.M.'s surgery, and their sparing helps explain why he could still learn new motor skills."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a subtest of the Performance Scale of the classic Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS and WAIS-R)?", "concept": "The answer is a: Digit Span is not a Performance subtest of the classic WAIS and WAIS-R; it belongs to the Verbal Scale. David Wechsler (1896 to 1981), chief psychologist at Bellevue Hospital in New York, published the Wechsler-Bellevue scale in 1939, the WAIS in 1955 and the WAIS-R in 1981. Unlike the early Stanford-Binet, his scales gave separate Verbal and Performance IQs as well as a Full Scale IQ, and used the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15). The Verbal Scale had six subtests: Information, Comprehension, Arithmetic, Similarities, Digit Span and Vocabulary. The Performance Scale had five: Digit Symbol, Picture Completion, Block Design, Picture Arrangement and Object Assembly. Verbal subtests are given and answered in words; Performance subtests need the person to arrange, assemble or copy materials, often against the clock. Example: in Digit Span the examiner reads 7-2-9-4 and the person repeats it forwards or backwards. Nothing is handled, so it is a verbal task of attention and working memory. In Block Design, by contrast, the person copies a red and white pattern with cubes. Option a is right because Digit Span sat on the Verbal side, while the other three were Performance subtests. Exam trap: later editions changed the structure. The WAIS-III (1997) kept the Verbal and Performance IQs but added four index scores, and the WAIS-IV (2008) dropped the Verbal and Performance IQs altogether. Digit Span now counts towards the Working Memory Index.", "traps": {"b": "Picture Arrangement, putting a set of cartoon panels into a sensible story order, was a classic Performance subtest of the WAIS and WAIS-R; it was dropped from the WAIS-IV.", "c": "Block Design, copying red and white patterns with coloured cubes against the clock, is a core Performance subtest and still survives in the WAIS-IV Perceptual Reasoning Index.", "d": "Object Assembly, putting jigsaw-like pieces together to form familiar objects, was a classic Performance subtest; it became optional in the WAIS-III and was dropped from the WAIS-IV."}}, {"q": "Which of these theories of intelligence was built on factor analysis?", "concept": "The answer is d: Louis Thurstone's primary mental abilities theory was built on factor analysis. Factor analysis is a statistical method that examines how many test scores correlate with one another and finds a smaller number of underlying factors that explain those correlations. Charles Spearman (1904) used an early form of it to argue for a general factor, g, plus specific factors, s. Louis Thurstone (1938) developed multiple factor analysis, gave a large battery of tests to college students, and found about seven separate abilities rather than one dominant g. His primary mental abilities are verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning. Later factor-analytic models followed the same path: J. P. Guilford's structure of intellect, Raymond Cattell and John Horn's fluid and crystallised intelligence, and John Carroll's three-stratum theory (1993). Example: if vocabulary and reading tests correlate highly with each other but only weakly with mental rotation tests, factor analysis separates a verbal factor from a spatial factor. Option d is right because Thurstone's seven abilities came directly out of factor analysing test scores. Exam trap: Gardner and Sternberg also describe several kinds of intelligence, so they sound similar, but neither derived their list from factor analysis. Gardner used criteria such as the effects of brain damage, savants and cultural evidence, and Sternberg used an information-processing view.", "traps": {"a": "Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences (Frames of Mind, 1983) rests on criteria such as isolation by brain damage, savants and prodigies, and cultural evidence, not on factor analysis; Gardner rejects a single g.", "b": "Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory (1985) divides intelligence into analytical, creative and practical parts using an information-processing approach, rather than factor analysis of test scores.", "c": "Jean Piaget's theory describes how thinking changes through four stages from sensorimotor to formal operations; it is a developmental stage theory built on clinical interviews and observation, not on factor analysis."}}, {"q": "Solomon Asch's line-judgement studies and Stanley Milgram's 'shock' studies are the classic demonstrations of, respectively:", "concept": "The answer is c: Asch's line studies demonstrate conformity and Milgram's shock studies demonstrate obedience. Social influence comes in three classic kinds. Conformity means changing behaviour or belief to match a group, even though no one asks you to. Compliance means agreeing to a direct request from someone without special authority. Obedience means following an order from an authority figure. Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) showed groups a standard line and three comparison lines and asked which one matched. Only one member was a real participant; the rest were confederates who all gave the same wrong answer on critical trials. Real participants went along with the wrong majority on about 37% of critical trials, and about 75% conformed at least once, even though the right answer was obvious. Stanley Milgram (1963) at Yale told participants, acting as \"teachers\", to give a \"learner\" shocks rising in 15-volt steps for each mistake. The shocks were fake, but the participants did not know that. When they hesitated, the experimenter said things like \"Please continue\". 65% went all the way to 450 volts. Example: copying classmates who all stand up for a song is conformity; standing because the principal orders it is obedience. Option c is right because Asch's pressure came from peers and Milgram's from an authority, in that order. Exam trap: the question says \"respectively\", so the order matters; option a names the right two ideas in reverse order.", "traps": {"a": "Obedience and conformity names the correct two processes but in reverse order; Asch studied yielding to peers (conformity) and Milgram studied following an authority's orders (obedience).", "b": "Compliance is agreeing to a direct request, studied through tactics such as foot-in-the-door (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966); Asch's confederates never asked anyone to agree, and Milgram's study is obedience, not conformity.", "d": "Group polarisation is the tendency of group discussion to push members towards more extreme views (Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni, 1969); Asch's participants held no discussion, so only the second half, obedience, is right."}}, {"q": "Indian textbooks credit the 'unifactor' (monarchic) theory, which treats intelligence as one single, undivided capacity, to:", "concept": "The answer is a: Indian textbooks credit the unifactor (monarchic) theory to Alfred Binet. Many Indian psychology and education books sort the early theories of intelligence by how many factors they allow. The unifactor or monarchic theory treats intelligence as one single, undivided power that rules all mental activity, as a monarch rules a kingdom. It is credited to Alfred Binet, who with Théodore Simon built the first practical intelligence test (1905), and is often linked with Lewis Terman and William Stern. On this view each person has a fixed total amount of this one capacity, used equally in every task. The two-factor theory belongs to Charles Spearman (1904): a general factor g plus a specific factor s for each test. The multifactor or anarchic theory belongs to Edward Thorndike: intelligence is many separate abilities with no single ruler. The group factor or oligarchic theory belongs to Louis Thurstone: a few broad primary abilities share power, like a small ruling group. Example: on the monarchic view, a child who does well in arithmetic should do equally well in language and drawing, because one power runs all three. Option a is right because this scheme gives the monarchic label to Binet's view of intelligence as a single, unified capacity. Exam trap: Spearman's g sounds like a \"single factor\", but his theory is the two-factor theory, g plus s, so in this classification it is not the unifactor theory.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Spearman (1904) proposed the two-factor theory: a general factor g shared by all tasks plus specific s factors; the g makes it sound single-factor, but it has two kinds of factor.", "c": "Edward Thorndike held the multifactor or anarchic theory: intelligence is a collection of many separate, specific abilities with no single ruling power, the opposite of the monarchic view.", "d": "Louis Thurstone (1938) gave the group factor or oligarchic theory: about seven primary mental abilities, such as verbal comprehension, number and space, share control like a small ruling group."}}, {"q": "The first woman to be officially awarded a PhD in psychology (Cornell University, 1894) was:", "concept": "The answer is b: Margaret Floy Washburn was the first woman officially awarded a PhD in psychology, by Cornell University in 1894. In the late nineteenth century many universities let women attend classes but would not grant them doctorates, so several women did doctoral-level work without receiving the degree. Washburn (1871 to 1939) first hoped to study with James McKeen Cattell at Columbia, which would admit her only as an unofficial \"hearer\". She moved to Cornell, where Edward Bradford Titchener, the leading structuralist in America, supervised her. Her doctoral thesis was on tactual perception, and Wilhelm Wundt published it in his journal Philosophische Studien. She then taught at Vassar College for decades, wrote The Animal Mind (1908), an influential textbook of comparative psychology, and in 1921 became the second woman president of the American Psychological Association (APA). Example of the contrast: Mary Whiton Calkins finished every requirement for a Harvard PhD in 1895, yet Harvard refused to award the degree to a woman, so the first official psychology doctorate for a woman remains Washburn's. Option b is right because Washburn's 1894 Cornell degree was the first doctorate in psychology actually awarded to a woman. Exam trap: Calkins is famous as the first woman APA president (1905), so students often pick her here; earning a degree in practice and being officially awarded it are different things, and the question asks about the official award.", "traps": {"a": "Mary Whiton Calkins completed all the requirements for a Harvard PhD in 1895, but Harvard refused to grant it to a woman; she is known as the first woman APA president (1905).", "c": "Christine Ladd-Franklin completed her doctoral work at Johns Hopkins in 1882 but received the degree only in 1926; she is best known for an evolutionary theory of colour vision.", "d": "Leta Stetter Hollingworth earned her PhD in 1916, more than twenty years later; she is known for research challenging myths about the psychology of women and for her work on gifted children."}}, {"q": "The term 'meme', for a unit of cultural information (an idea, tune or habit) that spreads from person to person by imitation, was coined by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the term \"meme\" was coined by Richard Dawkins. Dawkins, a British evolutionary biologist, introduced the word in The Selfish Gene (1976). The book argued that the gene is the real unit of natural selection, and in a late chapter he suggested that culture has a replicator of its own. He took the Greek mimeme, \"something imitated\", and shortened it to rhyme with gene. A meme is any unit of cultural information that is copied from mind to mind: a tune, a catchphrase, a fashion, a recipe or a ritual. Like genes, memes vary, are copied (mostly by imitation) and are selected: the catchier or more useful ones spread, and the rest die out. This view treats culture as an evolutionary process in its own right, running far faster than genetic change. Example: a dance step from a film song is copied by thousands of people at weddings within months, often changing slightly as it spreads; on Dawkins's view it is a meme competing with other dance steps for attention and imitation. Internet memes borrowed the word much later. Option a is right because Dawkins coined the word in 1976 by analogy with gene. Exam trap: Bandura's observational learning explains how a habit is copied, and Vygotsky stressed cultural tools, so both sound related, but neither coined the term.", "traps": {"b": "Edward O. Wilson wrote Sociobiology: The New Synthesis (1975), applying evolution and kin selection to social behaviour; he later wrote on links between genes and culture but did not coin \"meme\".", "c": "Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory holds that children learn through social interaction and cultural tools such as language; he described how culture is passed on but never coined the word \"meme\".", "d": "Albert Bandura's observational learning, shown in the Bobo doll studies (1961), describes imitation of models, the mechanism by which memes spread, but he did not coin the term."}}, {"q": "A test gives nearly the same scores when the same people take it again two weeks later. This shows that the test has high:", "concept": "The answer is d: nearly identical scores two weeks apart show high test-retest reliability. Reliability is the consistency of a measurement. In classical test theory, which grew from the work of Charles Spearman (1904), every observed score is a true score plus random error, and reliability is the proportion of score variance that is true variance. Test-retest reliability is estimated by giving the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores. The correlation is called the coefficient of stability, because it shows how stable scores are over time. The time gap matters. If it is too short, people remember their answers (carry-over) or improve with practice, which inflates the coefficient. If it is too long, the trait itself may truly change, which lowers it. So a test manual should always report the interval. Other forms answer different questions: parallel (alternate) forms reliability correlates two equivalent versions; internal consistency (split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach's alpha) checks whether the items in one sitting hang together; inter-rater reliability checks agreement between scorers. Example: 50 students take a numerical reasoning test in June and again two weeks later, and the two sets of scores correlate 0.88, so the test is stable. Option d is right because consistency of scores across two occasions is exactly what test-retest reliability measures. Exam trap: consistency is reliability, not validity; high stability says nothing about whether the test measures what it claims to.", "traps": {"a": "Predictive validity is how well test scores forecast a later criterion, such as entrance test scores predicting first-year marks; it concerns accuracy of prediction, not consistency of scores over time.", "b": "Content validity is how well the items sample the whole domain the test is meant to cover, usually judged by experts; repeating the test says nothing about content coverage.", "c": "Construct validity is evidence that a test measures the theoretical trait it claims to (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955), shown by convergent, discriminant and factor-analytic evidence; stable scores alone do not show it."}}, {"q": "Which statement about reliability and validity is correct?", "concept": "The answer is c: reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same result each time? Validity is accuracy of meaning: does the test measure what it claims to measure? The two are linked in one direction only. A test full of random error cannot correlate well with anything, so an unreliable test cannot be valid. But a test can be perfectly consistent and still measure the wrong thing. Classical test theory makes this exact, building on the correction for attenuation of Charles Spearman (1904). The highest validity coefficient a test can reach is the square root of its reliability coefficient, called the index of reliability. If reliability is 0.64, validity cannot exceed 0.80; if reliability is 0.25, validity cannot exceed 0.50. Raising reliability raises the ceiling on validity, but it does not guarantee that validity reaches that ceiling. Example: a bathroom scale that always shows you 5 kg heavier than you are is reliable, because it gives the same reading every time, but it is not valid, because the reading is wrong. A scale whose reading jumps around by several kilos is neither reliable nor valid. Option c is right because reliability sets the upper limit on validity without ensuring it. Exam trap: option b reverses the logic; \"a valid test must be reliable\" is true, but \"a reliable test must be valid\" is false.", "traps": {"a": "A test cannot be valid without being reliable; random error limits how well it can correlate with anything, so its validity can never exceed the square root of its reliability.", "b": "A reliable test is not always valid: the bathroom scale that always reads 5 kg too heavy is consistent but wrong, so reliability alone does not guarantee validity.", "d": "Whether a test measures what it claims to measure is the definition of validity, not reliability; reliability only tells us whether scores are consistent and free of random error."}}, {"q": "A new anxiety scale correlates highly with an established anxiety scale and only weakly with a vocabulary test. This pattern is evidence of its:", "concept": "The answer is d: high correlation with another anxiety scale and low correlation with a vocabulary test is evidence of convergent and discriminant (construct) validity. Construct validity is the degree to which a test measures the theoretical trait, or construct, it claims to measure. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) set out the idea: because a construct such as anxiety cannot be seen directly, we gather evidence that test scores behave as the theory predicts. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) added two key kinds of evidence in their multitrait-multimethod matrix. Convergent validity means the test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct. Discriminant (or divergent) validity means it correlates weakly with measures of different constructs. Both are needed: a new anxiety scale that correlated highly with everything, including vocabulary, might really be measuring reading ability or general test-taking skill. In the matrix, several traits are each measured by several methods, such as self-report, peer rating and interview, and same-trait correlations should be higher than different-trait ones. Example: a new depression inventory should correlate strongly with the Beck Depression Inventory but only weakly with a spelling test. Option d is right because high correlation with a similar measure plus low correlation with an unrelated one is exactly convergent and discriminant evidence. Exam trap: students notice only the high correlation, but the low correlation with vocabulary is the discriminant half and matters just as much.", "traps": {"a": "Face validity is whether a test looks, on the surface, as if it measures what it claims to, judged by test-takers; it involves no correlations with other measures at all.", "b": "Predictive validity is how well scores forecast a future criterion, such as a later diagnosis or job performance; here the scale was compared with other tests taken now, not with a later outcome.", "c": "Split-half reliability correlates two halves of the same test, corrected with the Spearman-Brown formula; it measures internal consistency, not how a test relates to other tests."}}, {"q": "A 60-year-old man insists that his intestines have rotted and that he is already dead. He wakes at 3 a.m. every day and cannot fall asleep again, and he has lost a lot of weight. The most likely diagnosis is:", "concept": "The answer is c: severe major depressive disorder with psychotic features is the most likely diagnosis. Major depression is marked by low mood or loss of interest for at least two weeks, plus symptoms such as sleep change, appetite or weight change, guilt and suicidal thoughts. In severe cases delusions appear. When their content matches the depressed mood, such as guilt, poverty, disease, punishment or death, they are called mood-congruent. The most extreme is the nihilistic delusion: the belief that one's organs have rotted, the world has ended, or one is already dead. Jules Cotard (1880) described this picture, now called Cotard's syndrome, and it is seen mostly in older patients with severe depression. Waking around 3 a.m. and being unable to sleep again is early morning awakening (terminal insomnia). Together with marked weight loss it points to melancholic features, the biological pattern of depression that is often worse in the morning. Older textbooks called this kind of late-life depression involutional melancholia. Applying it: this man has a depressive syndrome (insomnia, weight loss) and a delusion that fits it (decay and death), so the delusion is part of the mood disorder. Option c is right because nihilistic, mood-congruent delusions plus melancholic biological symptoms define psychotic depression. Exam trap: any delusion tempts students towards schizophrenia, but when the delusions match a dominant depressive syndrome and arise within it, the diagnosis is a mood disorder with psychotic features.", "traps": {"a": "Schizophrenia involves delusions and hallucinations that occur largely outside, or outlast, any mood episode; here a full depressive syndrome dominates and the delusion fits the mood, which points away from it.", "b": "Delusional disorder, somatic type, involves a fixed bodily delusion, such as infestation, with fairly intact functioning; any mood episodes must be brief compared with the delusions, but here severe depression dominates the picture.", "d": "Generalised anxiety disorder is chronic, excessive worry about many everyday matters for at least six months; it does not involve delusions, and worry is not a fixed false belief."}}, {"q": "Which of these is a first-line, evidence-based psychological treatment for post-traumatic stress disorder?", "concept": "The answer is b: prolonged exposure therapy is a first-line, evidence-based psychological treatment for PTSD. Post-traumatic stress disorder follows a terrifying event and brings intrusive memories and nightmares, avoidance of reminders, negative changes in mood and beliefs, and hyperarousal. Avoidance keeps the disorder going: the person never stays with the memory long enough to learn that it is a memory, not a present danger. Prolonged exposure (PE) was developed by Edna Foa, based on the emotional processing theory of Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986). It has two parts. In imaginal exposure the client repeatedly retells the trauma aloud in the present tense, and the session is recorded so they can listen again at home. In in vivo exposure the client gradually approaches safe situations they have been avoiding. Fear rises, then falls with repetition, and the fear memory is corrected. Other first-line treatments include cognitive processing therapy (Patricia Resick), trauma-focused CBT, and eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR, Francine Shapiro, 1987). NICE and American Psychological Association guidelines recommend these trauma-focused therapies. Example: a woman assaulted in a car park recounts the attack in every session and then practises parking in busy, safe car parks until her fear drops. Option b is right because PE has strong support from randomised trials in PTSD. Exam trap: aversion therapy also makes the client face something unpleasant, but it pairs an unwanted behaviour with discomfort to create a new aversion, while PE removes an existing fear.", "traps": {"a": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic technique of saying whatever comes to mind to reveal unconscious conflicts; it is not a trauma-focused treatment and lacks strong evidence for PTSD.", "c": "A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) rewards target behaviours with tokens exchangeable for privileges, used in wards and classrooms; it builds behaviours rather than treating trauma memories and fear.", "d": "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking, with an unpleasant stimulus, such as a nausea-producing drug, to create an aversion; PTSD needs existing fear reduced, not new fear added."}}, {"q": "In a moderately skewed distribution the mean is 62 and the median is 65. Using Karl Pearson's empirical formula, the mode is approximately:", "concept": "The answer is d: the mode is approximately 71. Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936), the British statistician who gave us the product-moment correlation, noticed that in a moderately skewed, single-peaked distribution the three averages keep a steady spacing. The median lies about two-thirds of the way from the mode to the mean, so the distance from mean to mode is about three times the distance from mean to median. As a formula: Mean minus Mode = 3 (Mean minus Median). Rearranged: Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean. This empirical relation is only approximate, but it lets you estimate the mode when the raw data are not given. Working: 3 x 65 = 195; 2 x 62 = 124; 195 minus 124 = 71. Check the order: mean 62, median 65, mode 71. The mean is lowest because it is pulled towards a long tail of low scores, so the distribution is negatively (left) skewed, like marks on a very easy test where most students score high and a few score low. Pearson's coefficient of skewness, 3 (Mean minus Median) divided by SD, is also negative here. Option d is right because 3 x 65 minus 2 x 62 gives 71. Exam trap: students often swap the multipliers and compute 3 Mean minus 2 Median, which gives 56, or forget that in a negative skew the mode must lie above the median, not between the mean and median.", "traps": {"a": "63.5 is simply the average of the mean and the median ((62 + 65) / 2); no rule puts the mode between them, and in a skewed curve the mode lies beyond the median.", "b": "68 adds the mean-median gap once to the median (65 + 3); the empirical rule puts the mode twice that gap beyond the median, 65 + 6 = 71.", "c": "59 subtracts the gap from the mean (62 minus 3), which would put the mode below the mean as in a positive skew; here the mean is below the median, so the mode must be higher still."}}, {"q": "A between-subjects 3 x 3 x 2 factorial experiment has 10 participants in each group. How many participants are needed in total?", "concept": "The answer is b: 180 participants are needed in total. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at the same time, crossing every level of each factor with every level of the others. Ronald Fisher made the method popular in agricultural research and described it in The Design of Experiments (1935). The notation tells you the structure: each number is one factor, and its value is that factor's number of levels. So 3 x 3 x 2 means three factors, with 3, 3 and 2 levels. The number of conditions, or cells, is found by multiplying the levels: 3 x 3 x 2 = 18 cells. In a between-subjects design each participant serves in only one cell, so the total sample is cells times participants per cell: 18 x 10 = 180. Example: a study crosses therapy type (CBT, interpersonal therapy, waiting list), session length (30, 45 or 60 minutes) and homework (given or not given). One cell is \"CBT in 45-minute sessions with homework\", and it needs its own ten people. Factorial designs let you test each main effect and also interactions, for instance whether homework helps only when sessions are short. Option b is right because 18 cells with 10 participants each gives 180. Exam trap: 18 is the number of cells, not people. In a fully within-subjects design, where the same people pass through all 18 conditions, only 10 would be needed.", "traps": {"a": "80 comes from adding the levels (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) and multiplying by 10; the levels of crossed factors must be multiplied, not added, to count the cells.", "c": "18 is the number of cells (3 x 3 x 2), not the number of people; with 10 different participants in each cell, the total must be 18 times 10.", "d": "90 comes from multiplying only two factors (3 x 3 x 10) and ignoring the third factor with 2 levels, which doubles the number of cells from 9 to 18."}}, {"q": "The rhombencephalon (hindbrain) develops into:", "concept": "The answer is d: the rhombencephalon (hindbrain) develops into the medulla, pons and cerebellum. Early in embryonic life the front end of the neural tube swells into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), the mesencephalon (midbrain) and the rhombencephalon (hindbrain). By about the fifth week two of these divide, giving five secondary vesicles. The prosencephalon splits into the telencephalon, which becomes the cerebral cortex, basal ganglia and limbic structures, and the diencephalon, which becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays single and becomes the midbrain, with its tectum (roof, holding the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum (floor). The rhombencephalon splits into the metencephalon, which becomes the pons and cerebellum, and the myelencephalon, which becomes the medulla oblongata. The name comes from the diamond (rhomboid) shape of the floor of the fourth ventricle in this region. The medulla and pons, together with the midbrain, form the brainstem, which controls breathing, heart rate and arousal. Example: a stroke in the medulla can stop breathing, and damage to the cerebellum, also a hindbrain structure, makes walking unsteady and movements clumsy. Option d is right because the metencephalon and myelencephalon, the two parts of the rhombencephalon, form exactly these three structures. Exam trap: \"mes-\" means middle and \"met-\" means after; the mesencephalon is the midbrain, while the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) is part of the hindbrain.", "traps": {"a": "The thalamus and hypothalamus develop from the diencephalon, the rear division of the prosencephalon (forebrain); they sit above the brainstem and are not hindbrain structures.", "b": "The tectum and tegmentum are the roof and floor of the mesencephalon (midbrain), which stays undivided during development; it is a separate primary vesicle lying in front of the hindbrain.", "c": "The cerebral hemispheres and basal ganglia develop from the telencephalon, the front division of the prosencephalon (forebrain), at the opposite end of the brain from the hindbrain."}}, {"q": "The classic dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia holds that the positive symptoms result from:", "concept": "The answer is b: the classic dopamine hypothesis holds that positive symptoms come from too much dopamine activity, especially at D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway. Positive symptoms are experiences added to normal life: delusions, hallucinations and disorganised thinking. The hypothesis grew from drug evidence. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) argued that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, blocks dopamine receptors. In 1976 Philip Seeman and colleagues, and separately Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, showed that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks how strongly they bind D2 receptors: the tighter the binding, the smaller the dose needed. Drugs that raise dopamine point the same way: high doses of amphetamine can cause a paranoid psychosis, and L-dopa given for Parkinson's disease can cause hallucinations. The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and limbic areas, and its overactivity is blamed for positive symptoms. The revised view (Kenneth Davis and colleagues, 1991; Oliver Howes and Shitij Kapur, 2009) adds that dopamine is too low in the mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex, which helps explain negative and cognitive symptoms. Example: haloperidol, a strong D2 blocker, often reduces hallucinations but does little for apathy and social withdrawal. Option b is right because positive symptoms are tied to dopamine excess, not deficit. Exam trap: low dopamine in the mesocortical pathway belongs to negative symptoms, and loss of dopamine cells in the substantia nigra belongs to Parkinson's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Too little mesolimbic dopamine is the reverse of the hypothesis; reduced dopamine is proposed only in the mesocortical (prefrontal) pathway and is linked to negative and cognitive symptoms, not positive ones.", "c": "Serotonin, made in the raphe nuclei, enters later views because LSD-type hallucinogens act at 5-HT2A receptors and atypical antipsychotics block them, but the classic hypothesis is about dopamine.", "d": "Loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra, which feeds the nigrostriatal pathway, causes Parkinson's disease; it lowers dopamine and produces tremor, rigidity and slowed movement, not psychosis."}}, {"q": "A mother tells her son that he can have dessert only after he finishes his vegetables. She is using:", "concept": "The answer is d: the mother is using the Premack principle. David Premack (1959) proposed that reinforcers are best thought of as behaviours, not things. Any behaviour a person does often, when free to choose, can reinforce a behaviour they do less often. The rule is: a more probable behaviour, allowed only after a less probable one, will strengthen the less probable one. To find what will work, you watch what the person does when free to choose and rank the activities by how much time they get. Premack showed this with children: those who preferred eating sweets to playing a pinball machine played more pinball to earn sweets, while those who preferred pinball ate more sweets to earn a turn at the machine. Because parents use it naturally, it is called Grandma's rule: \"first you work, then you play\". It is widely used in behaviour therapy, classrooms and parent training. Example: a student who loves cricket but avoids homework must study for an hour before going out to play; the cricket, a high-probability activity, reinforces studying. Here the son eats dessert gladly but avoids vegetables, so making dessert depend on vegetables strengthens vegetable eating. Option d is right because a preferred activity is being used to reinforce a less preferred one. Exam trap: the Crespi effect also involves rewards, but it is about a sudden change in the size of a reward, not about using one behaviour to reinforce another.", "traps": {"a": "\"Decay conditioning\" is not a standard learning term; decay is a theory of forgetting, close to Edward Thorndike's law of disuse, which says memory traces fade with time if unused.", "b": "The Crespi effect (Leo Crespi, 1942) is a contrast effect: rats shifted to a larger reward overshoot, and those shifted to a smaller reward undershoot, the performance of rats always given that amount.", "c": "Higher-order conditioning is classical conditioning in which a well-established conditioned stimulus acts as the unconditioned stimulus for a new neutral stimulus, as Ivan Pavlov showed; dessert rules use operant reinforcement instead."}}, {"q": "A trainer teaches a pigeon to turn in a full circle by reinforcing first a slight head turn, then a quarter turn, then a half turn, and so on. This procedure is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: reinforcing a slight head turn, then a quarter turn, then a half turn, is shaping. Shaping is the operant technique of reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour. B. F. Skinner (1904 to 1990) developed it in the 1940s while training rats and pigeons. It solves a basic problem: you cannot reinforce a behaviour the animal never performs, and a pigeon will not spontaneously turn a full circle. So the trainer starts with something the bird already does, such as any slight turn of the head, and reinforces it with food. Then the criterion is raised: only bigger turns earn food, while smaller ones are no longer reinforced. This is differential reinforcement: closer approximations are reinforced and earlier ones extinguished. Step by step, the behaviour moves towards the goal. A click or a light paired with food (a conditioned reinforcer) is often used so that reinforcement arrives instantly, at the exact moment of the right movement. Example: a speech therapist working with a child who does not speak first reinforces any sound, then a \"b\" sound, then \"ba\", and finally the word \"ball\". Option b is right because the trainer reinforced closer and closer approximations of one response until the full circle appeared. Exam trap: chaining also builds complex behaviour step by step, but it links different responses into a sequence, while shaping gradually refines a single response.", "traps": {"a": "Chaining links a sequence of different responses, each step producing the cue for the next and the final step being reinforced, as in teaching a child to dress; here one response is gradually refined instead.", "c": "Fading gradually removes prompts or cues, such as a teacher's hint, so that the behaviour comes under the control of natural cues; it changes the stimulus, not the response being reinforced.", "d": "Stimulus generalisation is responding in the same way to stimuli similar to the original one, such as a pigeon pecking a key of a slightly different colour; it is a learning effect, not a training procedure."}}, {"q": "People choose a surgery described as having a '90% survival rate' more often than the same surgery described as having a '10% death rate'. This is the:", "concept": "The answer is c: choosing the surgery more often when it is described by a 90% survival rate than by a 10% death rate is the framing effect. The framing effect is a shift in choice when the same facts are worded differently, usually as gains or as losses. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1981) showed it with the Asian disease problem. Told that a disease will kill 600 people, most people preferred a programme that saves 200 for sure to a gamble with a one-third chance of saving all 600. When the same options were worded as 400 dying for sure versus a one-third chance that nobody dies, most chose the gamble. Their prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky, 1979) explains why: people judge outcomes as gains or losses from a reference point, and losses loom larger than equal gains (loss aversion). So people avoid risk when options are framed as gains and seek risk when they are framed as losses. Barbara McNeil and colleagues (1982) found the same in medicine: patients, students and doctors chose surgery over radiation therapy more often when results were given as survival rather than death rates. Example: shoppers rate minced beef labelled \"75% lean\" more highly than the same beef labelled \"25% fat\". Option c is right because 90% surviving and 10% dying are the same fact, and only the positive versus negative wording moved the choice. Exam trap: the percentages look like an anchor, but anchoring needs a first number that biases a later estimate, and nobody estimates anything here.", "traps": {"a": "The availability heuristic (Tversky and Kahneman, 1973) is judging how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind, such as fearing flying after news of a crash; no recall is involved here.", "b": "The anchoring effect is when a first number pulls a later numerical estimate towards it, as when a high asking price inflates your guess of value; here the facts are equal and only the wording differs.", "d": "The sunk cost effect (Hal Arkes and Catherine Blumer, 1985) is continuing with something because of what has already been spent, such as sitting through a bad film because the ticket was paid for."}}, {"q": "The MMPI-2 (1989) has how many items and how many basic clinical scales?", "concept": "The answer is d: the MMPI-2 has 567 items and 10 basic clinical scales. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) was built by psychologist Starke Hathaway and psychiatrist J. C. McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota. They used empirical criterion keying: an item was kept for a scale only if patients with a particular diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The original test had 550 different true-false statements (566 counting repeated items). In 1989 James Butcher and colleagues published the MMPI-2, which rewrote dated items, removed objectionable ones and collected new norms from about 2,600 American adults; it has 567 items. The 10 clinical scales kept their old numbers and names: 1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania and 0 Social Introversion. Validity scales such as L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness) check how the person approached the test, and the MMPI-2 added VRIN and TRIN to detect inconsistent or fixed responding. Scores are T-scores, and 65 or above counts as clinically raised. Example: a person whose two highest scales are 2 and 7 (a 2-7 code type) usually presents as anxious, tense and depressed. Option d is right because 567 items and ten clinical scales are the defining numbers of the MMPI-2. Exam trap: 550 belongs to the 1943 MMPI, 478 to the adolescent MMPI-A and 338 to the MMPI-2-RF, so learn each number with its version.", "traps": {"a": "478 items is the MMPI-A (1992), the adolescent version for ages 14 to 18. It also keeps the 10 clinical scales, which makes it tempting, but the item count does not match the MMPI-2.", "b": "338 items and 9 scales describe the MMPI-2-RF (2008, Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen), whose nine Restructured Clinical (RC) scales replace the original clinical scales with shorter, less overlapping measures.", "c": "550 is the number of unique items in the original 1943 MMPI of Hathaway and McKinley; the 1989 revision kept the 10 clinical scales but changed the item pool to 567."}}, {"q": "The WAIS-IV yields a Full Scale IQ and four index scores. Which of the following is NOT one of the WAIS-IV indexes?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Fluid Reasoning Index is not one of the WAIS-IV indexes. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale was created by David Wechsler (1955), building on his Wechsler-Bellevue scale of 1939, and its fourth edition, the WAIS-IV, appeared in 2008 for ages 16 to 90. The WAIS-IV dropped the old Verbal IQ and Performance IQ and reports a Full Scale IQ plus four index scores. The Verbal Comprehension Index (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information) measures word knowledge and verbal reasoning. The Perceptual Reasoning Index (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles) measures non-verbal reasoning and spatial problem solving. The Working Memory Index (Digit Span, Arithmetic) measures holding and working on information in mind. The Processing Speed Index (Symbol Search, Coding) measures quick, accurate visual scanning. These ten core subtests give the Full Scale IQ. A General Ability Index, built from the verbal and perceptual subtests only, gives an estimate less affected by memory and speed. Fluid Reasoning became a separate index in the children's WISC-V (2014), which split perceptual reasoning into Visual Spatial and Fluid Reasoning to give five primary indexes; the WAIS-5 (2024) later did the same. Example: an adult with a Verbal Comprehension Index of 115 and a Processing Speed Index of 85 has a 30-point gap that the Full Scale IQ hides. Option a is right because Fluid Reasoning is a WISC-V index, not a WAIS-IV one. Exam trap: Matrix Reasoning measures fluid ability, yet in the WAIS-IV it sits under the Perceptual Reasoning Index.", "traps": {"b": "The Perceptual Reasoning Index is one of the four WAIS-IV indexes, built from Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles; the WISC-V later split it into Visual Spatial and Fluid Reasoning.", "c": "The Verbal Comprehension Index is a WAIS-IV index, built from Similarities, Vocabulary and Information, and measures word knowledge and verbal reasoning, largely crystallised ability.", "d": "The Processing Speed Index is a WAIS-IV index, built from Symbol Search and Coding, and measures how quickly and accurately a person scans and handles simple visual information."}}, {"q": "A 72-year-old man is asked the date and where he is, to repeat three words, to count back from 100 in sevens, to recall the three words, to name a pencil and a watch, to follow a three-step command and to copy two intersecting pentagons. The test being given is the:", "concept": "The answer is c: the test being given is the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE). The MMSE was published by Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh (1975) as a quick bedside screen for cognitive impairment that takes about ten minutes. It is scored out of 30 across five areas. Orientation gives 10 points: five for time, such as the date, and five for place. Registration gives 3 points for repeating three words. Attention and calculation give 5 points, for counting back from 100 in sevens or spelling WORLD backwards. Recall gives 3 points for the same three words a few minutes later. Language and praxis give 9 points: naming two objects, repeating a phrase, following a three-step command, reading and obeying \"Close your eyes\", writing a sentence and copying two intersecting pentagons. The total is 10 + 3 + 5 + 3 + 9 = 30. A common cut-off is 23/24: 24 or above is usually taken as normal, and 23 or below calls for full assessment. Scores are affected by education, and the MMSE misses much mild cognitive impairment, which the Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) detects better. Example: a retired professor with early dementia may still score 27, while a man with little schooling may score 22 with no disease. Option c is right because every task in the vignette is a standard MMSE item. Exam trap: the pentagon copy may suggest the Bender test, but one copying item inside a broad screen does not make it the Bender.", "traps": {"a": "The Wechsler Memory Scale, first published by David Wechsler in 1945 and now in its fourth edition, is a long, detailed battery of auditory and visual memory subtests, not a ten-minute screen of orientation and language.", "b": "The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Lauretta Bender, 1938) asks the person to copy nine geometric designs; it has no orientation, word recall, naming or commands, so one copying item does not make this the Bender.", "d": "The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) is a projective personality test in which the person says what ten inkblots look like; it does not test orientation, memory or language."}}, {"q": "A woman has had six major depressive episodes and one hypomanic episode lasting five days, but never a full manic episode. The diagnosis is:", "concept": "The answer is a: six major depressive episodes plus one hypomanic episode, with no history of mania, is bipolar II disorder. The current rules come from the DSM-5-TR of the American Psychiatric Association (2022); bipolar II first entered the manual in DSM-IV (1994). A major depressive episode needs at least five of nine symptoms, including depressed mood or loss of interest, for at least two weeks. A manic episode needs abnormally elevated or irritable mood with increased energy for at least seven days (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causing marked impairment or with psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same kind of symptoms for at least four consecutive days, clear enough for others to notice, but not severe enough to cause marked impairment or hospitalisation, and without psychosis. Bipolar I needs only one manic episode, with or without depression. Bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, and never a manic one. Example, applying the rules step by step to her: her high lasted 5 days; 5 is at least 4, so it meets hypomania; 5 is less than 7 and she was not hospitalised, so it is not mania. One hypomanic episode plus six major depressive episodes, with no mania, gives bipolar II. Option a is right because her history matches the bipolar II rule exactly. Exam trap: bipolar II is not simply a milder bipolar I; its depressions are often long and disabling.", "traps": {"b": "Bipolar I disorder requires at least one full manic episode, lasting a week or needing hospitalisation, with marked impairment or psychosis; she has never had mania, so bipolar I cannot be given.", "c": "Cyclothymic disorder needs at least two years (one in children) of fluctuating hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria; her six full major depressive episodes rule it out.", "d": "Recurrent major depressive disorder fits the six depressions, but DSM-5-TR excludes it once a person has ever had a manic or hypomanic episode not caused by a substance or medical condition."}}, {"q": "A factory pays its tailors 50 rupees for every 10 shirts they finish. This is an example of which schedule of reinforcement?", "concept": "The answer is a: paying tailors 50 rupees for every 10 shirts they finish is a fixed ratio schedule. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses earn a reward. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) mapped these schedules in their book Schedules of Reinforcement, using pigeons and rats. Continuous reinforcement rewards every response; partial (intermittent) schedules reward only some, and they differ on two questions. First, does the reward depend on the number of responses (ratio) or on time passing (interval)? Second, is the requirement fixed or variable? Working out this case: the pay depends on output, so it is a ratio schedule; the number never changes, so it is fixed. One payment per 10 shirts is FR-10, worth 50 / 10 = 5 rupees a shirt. Fixed ratio schedules produce a high, steady rate of work with a short post-reinforcement pause just after each reward. Fixed interval schedules give a scalloped pattern: slow after a reward, fast just before the next is due. Variable ratio schedules give the highest rates and the most resistance to extinction, and variable interval schedules give slow, steady responding. Example: a tailor works quickly through ten shirts, collects the 50 rupees, takes a tea break, then speeds up again. Option a is right because payment is tied to a set count of finished shirts, not to the clock. Exam trap: the word \"every\" can suggest a regular time interval, but here it means every tenth piece of work, so it is a ratio schedule.", "traps": {"b": "Fixed interval reinforces the first response after a set time has passed, such as a monthly salary or a weekly test; it produces a scalloped pattern in which effort rises just before the reward is due.", "c": "Variable ratio reinforces after an unpredictable number of responses around an average, as with slot machines or lottery tickets; it gives the highest response rates and the greatest resistance to extinction.", "d": "Variable interval reinforces the first response after unpredictable stretches of time, as when checking a phone for a reply; it produces slow, steady responding rather than reward for each batch of work."}}, {"q": "The F ratio can be used to test hypotheses that are:", "concept": "The answer is b: the F ratio can test both one-tailed and two-tailed hypotheses, depending on how the hypothesis is stated. The F ratio is one variance divided by another. Ronald A. Fisher developed analysis of variance in the 1920s (Statistical Methods for Research Workers, 1925), and George Snedecor named the statistic F in his honour. F can never be negative, and its distribution is skewed to the right. In one-way ANOVA, F = mean square between groups / mean square within groups. If the null hypothesis of equal population means is true, F is close to 1, and only a large F counts against the null, so the ANOVA test uses the right tail alone. F is also used to compare two variances directly. With a non-directional hypothesis (the two population variances differ), the test is two-tailed: put the larger variance on top and read the critical value at alpha / 2. With a directional hypothesis (group 1 is more variable), it is one-tailed at alpha. Example: 21 patients have an anxiety variance of 36 and 21 controls a variance of 16, so F = 36 / 16 = 2.25 with 20 and 20 degrees of freedom. One-tailed at .05, the critical F is 2.12, so 2.25 is significant; two-tailed at .05 (.025 in each tail), the critical F is 2.46, so it is not. Option b is right because the tails follow the hypothesis, not the statistic. Exam trap: ANOVA's right-tail-only rejection region does not make every F test one-tailed. Key note: the recall list (the topics students remembered from the CIP 2023 exam) does not say which option CIP keyed, and one earlier recall line remembers the answer as 'essentially two tailed'; statistically, 'both' is the defensible answer.", "traps": {"a": "One-tailed only describes how the ANOVA F test is read, since only a large F rejects the null; but an F test of two variances is two-tailed when the hypothesis is non-directional, so 'only' is wrong.", "c": "Two-tailed only fits the common non-directional comparison of two variances, and some students recall it, but a directional variance hypothesis and the right-tail ANOVA test are both one-tailed, so 'only' fails.", "d": "F is an inferential test statistic, compared with a critical value from the F distribution to decide about a null hypothesis; descriptive statistics such as the mean and standard deviation only summarise data."}}, {"q": "A researcher reports the average anxiety score of her 50 participants, then uses a t-test to conclude that anxiety differs between men and women in the whole college. These two steps use, in order:", "concept": "The answer is b: reporting the average anxiety score of the 50 participants is descriptive statistics, and using a t-test to conclude something about the whole college is inferential statistics. Descriptive statistics summarise the data actually collected. They include measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode), measures of variability (range, standard deviation), tables and graphs, and they make no claim beyond the people measured. Inferential statistics use probability to reason from a sample to the population it came from. They include significance tests such as the t-test, F test and chi-square, and confidence intervals. The t-test itself was published by William Sealy Gosset (1908) under the pen name \"Student\". A number that describes a sample is a statistic, written with Roman letters such as M or s; the matching value for the whole population is a parameter, written with Greek letters such as μ (mu) or σ (sigma). Example: suppose the 25 men average 45 and the 25 women average 39. Saying the gap is 45 - 39 = 6 points is description. Asking whether a 6-point gap in these 50 people means that men and women in the whole college truly differ, and getting a p value from the t-test, is inference. Option b is right because the first step summarises the sample and the second generalises to the population. Exam trap: a step that contains a mean is not automatically descriptive; ask whether the conclusion goes beyond the people actually measured.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the order: the average of the 50 participants only describes the sample in hand, while the t-test is what generalises to the college population, so descriptive comes first and inferential second.", "c": "Descriptive both times is wrong because the t-test does more than summarise: it uses probability, in the form of a p value, to reach a conclusion about the whole college, which is inference.", "d": "Inferential both times is wrong because the average of 50 participants, reported on its own, makes no claim beyond those 50 people; it is a descriptive summary of the sample."}}, {"q": "'Children are born with a clean slate, and everything they become is written on it by experience.' Which perspective does this describe?", "concept": "The answer is b: the view that children are born with a clean slate on which experience writes everything is empiricism, the tabula rasa position. Empiricism holds that all knowledge comes from experience through the senses, not from ideas present at birth. Its classic statement is by the English philosopher John Locke (1690) in An Essay Concerning Human Understanding. Locke compared the newborn mind to \"white paper, void of all characters\"; the Latin label tabula rasa means a scraped or blank writing tablet. Simple ideas enter through sensation and reflection, and complex ideas are built by combining them. Later British empiricists included George Berkeley, David Hume, James Mill and John Stuart Mill, with Thomas Hobbes as an earlier forerunner. The opposing position, nativism, says some knowledge or capacities are inborn; Plato, René Descartes with his innate ideas, and Immanuel Kant are its classic voices. This contrast is the philosophical root of the nature versus nurture debate. In psychology, John B. Watson's behaviourism is the modern heir of empiricism: his boast \"Give me a dozen healthy infants\" claimed that training alone could turn any child into any type of adult. Example: on Locke's view, a child who grows up fearing dogs and speaking Bengali acquired both entirely from what he saw, heard and was taught. Option b is right because a blank slate filled only by experience is the defining claim of empiricism. Exam trap: if an exam offers 'behavioural perspective' and not empiricism, choose behavioural, since it carries the same blank-slate assumption.", "traps": {"a": "Nativism is the opposite view: some knowledge or abilities are inborn. Plato, René Descartes with his innate ideas, and Immanuel Kant held versions of it, and Noam Chomsky's innate language faculty is a modern example.", "c": "The psychoanalytic perspective of Sigmund Freud stresses inborn instinctual drives in the id and unconscious conflict, so it does not treat the newborn as a blank slate, even though early experience matters.", "d": "The humanistic perspective of Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow assumes an inborn tendency to grow towards self-actualisation, so the person is not a blank slate written on only by experience."}}, {"q": "A study uses a significance level of .05, and the statistical test gives p = .01. The researcher should:", "concept": "The answer is a: with a significance level of .05 and p = .01, the researcher should reject the null hypothesis. A significance test starts with the null hypothesis (H0), the claim that there is no effect or no difference in the population. The p-value is the probability of getting data at least as extreme as those observed if H0 were true. Ronald A. Fisher (1925), in Statistical Methods for Research Workers, set out this logic and suggested .05 as a convenient cut-off. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) added the alternative hypothesis (H1), a significance level (alpha) fixed before the study, and two kinds of error. The decision rule is simple: if p is less than or equal to alpha, reject H0 and call the result statistically significant; if p is greater than alpha, fail to reject H0. Here, step by step: alpha = .05 and p = .01; .01 is smaller than .05; so H0 is rejected. Rejecting H0 risks a Type I error (rejecting a true null), and alpha is the rate of that error accepted in advance. Example: a relaxation programme is compared with a control group on exam anxiety; the t-test gives p = .01, so the researcher rejects the null of no difference and concludes that the programme group differs. Option a is right because p falls below alpha. Exam trap: p is not the probability that H0 is true, and a large p never lets you \"accept\" H0, only fail to reject it.", "traps": {"b": "Accepting the null would only arise if p were above .05, and even then the correct wording is 'fail to reject', because a non-significant result does not prove there is no effect.", "c": "Rejecting the alternative hypothesis gets the direction backwards: a p of .01 is below alpha, so the evidence counts against the null and supports, rather than rules out, the alternative.", "d": "Concluding the result is due to chance is the opposite of what p = .01 shows: data this extreme would arise only about 1 time in 100 if chance alone, the null hypothesis, were at work."}}, {"q": "After a stroke, a man understands speech and speaks fluently, but he cannot find the names of everyday objects such as a comb or a key. The lobe most likely damaged is the:", "concept": "The answer is d: a man who understands speech and speaks fluently but cannot name everyday objects most likely has damage to the left temporal lobe. His problem is anomia, and when it is the main deficit it is called anomic aphasia: comprehension, fluency and repetition are good, but word finding fails, especially for nouns. Patients fill the gaps with circumlocution (talking around the word) and vague words such as \"thing\". Language is usually based in the left hemisphere. Paul Broca (1861) linked non-fluent, effortful speech to the left frontal lobe, and Carl Wernicke (1874) linked fluent speech with poor comprehension to the left posterior temporal lobe. Naming itself depends heavily on left temporal cortex. Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1996) showed that damage to the left temporal pole and inferior temporal regions impaired retrieval of names for people, animals and tools, with different parts linked to different categories. Example: shown a comb, the man says \"it's the thing you, you know, for your hair\" and can show how to use it, which proves he recognises it and lacks only the word. Option d is right because fluent speech, intact comprehension and an isolated naming failure point to the left temporal lobe. Exam trap: \"cannot name\" is not \"cannot recognise\"; failing to recognise a seen object would be visual agnosia, a problem of the occipital and temporal visual areas. Key note: anomia is the least localising aphasia and can also follow damage to the left angular gyrus in the parietal lobe, but the left temporal lobe is the textbook answer.", "traps": {"a": "Occipital lobe damage disturbs vision and can cause visual agnosia, where a seen object is not recognised although it can be named by touch; this man knows the objects but cannot find the words.", "b": "Damage to the left frontal lobe (Broca's area), not the right, gives non-fluent, effortful speech; the right frontal lobe is not the language side in most people, and his speech is fluent anyway.", "c": "Right parietal damage typically causes hemispatial neglect, ignoring the left side of space, along with dressing and constructional difficulties; it does not produce a word-finding problem like this."}}, {"q": "In Joseph LeDoux's 'low road' for fear, a sensory signal about a threat travels from the thalamus directly to the:", "concept": "The answer is c: in Joseph LeDoux's low road for fear, a threat signal travels from the thalamus directly to the amygdala. Joseph LeDoux (1996) set out this two-route model in The Emotional Brain, based on fear conditioning studies in rats pairing a tone with shock. Sensory signals pass first through the thalamus, the brain's relay station, and from there take two routes. The low road runs straight from the thalamus to the amygdala. It is fast but crude, carrying only a rough sketch of the stimulus, enough to start a defensive reaction before you know what you are reacting to. The high road runs from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and then to the amygdala. It is slower but detailed, so it can identify what the stimulus really is. Both roads end in the amygdala, an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei in the medial temporal lobe. Its central nucleus then triggers the fear response through the hypothalamus (racing heart, stress hormones) and the brainstem (freezing, startle). Example: in a garden you jump back from a curved shape on the path; a moment later the visual cortex tells you it is a hose, and you calm down. The jump came from the low road, the correction from the high road. Option c is right because the amygdala is the direct target of the thalamic low road. Exam trap: the prefrontal cortex regulates and extinguishes fear afterwards; it does not receive the first signal. Key note: the recall list (topics students remembered from the CIP 2023 exam) records only 'emotional info goes to which area at the end', with options including prefrontal cortex, thalamus and amygdala; both roads end in the amygdala, so it is the answer either way.", "traps": {"a": "The prefrontal cortex, especially its ventromedial part, works on fear after the alarm has fired, regulating it and supporting extinction; it is not on the fast thalamus-to-amygdala low road at all.", "b": "The hippocampus adds context, such as where and when a threat was met, and passes this to the amygdala; it is not the direct destination of the thalamic low road.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates movement and supports simple motor conditioning such as the conditioned eyeblink; it is not part of LeDoux's fear pathways from the thalamus."}}, {"q": "According to the actor-observer bias, when we explain another person's behaviour we tend to attribute it to:", "concept": "The answer is d: according to the actor-observer bias, we tend to attribute another person's behaviour to dispositional (internal) factors. Attribution is the process of explaining why people act as they do. Fritz Heider (1958) first separated internal (dispositional) causes, such as personality, ability or effort, from external (situational) causes, such as luck, pressure or task difficulty. Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971) then described the actor-observer bias: as actors we explain our own behaviour by the situation, but as observers we explain other people's identical behaviour by their dispositions. Two reasons are usually given. The first is perceptual: the actor looks out at the situation, while the observer looks at the actor, so whatever is most visible seems to be the cause. Michael Storms (1973) reversed the bias by showing people videotapes of a conversation from the other person's viewpoint. The second is information: actors know how they behave across many situations, while observers usually see a single moment. The observer's half of this bias overlaps with the fundamental attribution error, named by Lee Ross (1977): the general tendency to overrate dispositional and underrate situational causes of others' behaviour. Example: when you trip on the stairs, you blame the wet step; when a classmate trips on the same stairs, you decide she is clumsy. Option d is right because observers explaining someone else's behaviour lean towards disposition. Exam trap: do not confuse this with the self-serving bias, which depends on the outcome: we credit our successes to ourselves and blame our failures on the situation.", "traps": {"a": "Situational factors are how we explain our own behaviour, the actor's side of the bias; the question asks about another person's behaviour, which observers tend to pin on disposition instead.", "b": "Luck is one external, unstable cause in Bernard Weiner's attribution model of success and failure, alongside ability, effort and task difficulty; it is not what observers typically use to explain others' actions.", "c": "Weighing situational and dispositional causes equally is what a careful, unbiased judge would do; the point of the bias is that observers give too much weight to disposition."}}, {"q": "Ravi learned Spanish in college and is now learning French. When he tries to recall his Spanish vocabulary, French words keep coming to mind instead. This is:", "concept": "The answer is a: when newly learned French words block Ravi's recall of his older Spanish vocabulary, that is retroactive interference. Interference theory says we forget mainly because memories compete with one another, not because they simply fade. John McGeoch (1932) argued this in his attack on the old law of disuse, and interference became the leading account of forgetting from long-term memory. It works in two directions. In retroactive interference, new learning disrupts recall of something learned earlier; retro means backwards, so the new memory reaches back and spoils the old. In proactive interference, earlier learning disrupts recall of something learned later; pro means forwards, so the old memory pushes forward into the new. Benton Underwood (1957) showed how strong proactive interference is: the more word lists people had learned before, the faster they forgot a new one. Interference is greatest when the two sets of material are similar, which is why two closely related languages clash so badly. John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924) supported interference over decay: people who slept after learning nonsense syllables recalled more than those who stayed awake. Example: after changing your phone number, you struggle to remember the old one (retroactive); when asked for the new one, the old one keeps coming out (proactive). Option a is right because the memory being tested is the old one (Spanish) and the intruder is the new one (French). Exam trap: first ask which memory is being recalled, then remember that PRO means the old works forward and RETRO means the new works backward.", "traps": {"b": "Proactive interference is old learning disrupting new: it would apply if Ravi, trying to speak French, kept producing Spanish words. Benton Underwood (1957) showed its power in list learning.", "c": "Decay theory says memory traces simply fade with time and disuse, the idea behind Edward Thorndike's law of disuse; it cannot explain why specific French words intrude, which points to competing memories.", "d": "Repression is Sigmund Freud's defence mechanism of pushing threatening, anxiety-laden memories out of awareness; Spanish vocabulary is emotionally neutral, so there is no motive to repress it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT common to the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ) and the NEO Personality Inventory?", "concept": "The answer is c: psychoticism is not common to the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ) and the NEO Personality Inventory. The EPQ was published by Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975). It measures Hans Eysenck's three superfactors, extraversion (E), neuroticism (N) and psychoticism (P), plus a Lie scale that checks for faking good. It added P to the earlier Eysenck Personality Inventory (1964), which measured only E and N. Eysenck saw psychoticism as tough-mindedness: aggressive, cold, impulsive, egocentric and low in empathy. The NEO Personality Inventory was developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985), and its revised form, the NEO-PI-R (1992), measures the Big Five: neuroticism, extraversion, openness to experience, agreeableness and conscientiousness, each with six facets. Setting the lists side by side: the EPQ has E, N and P; the NEO has N, E, O, A and C. The overlap is only N and E. Psychoticism appears only in the EPQ, while openness, agreeableness and conscientiousness appear only in the NEO. Research links high P with low agreeableness and low conscientiousness, which is partly why Eysenck argued that three factors were enough. Example: a person who is impulsive, careless of rules and unconcerned about others would score high on EPQ psychoticism and low on NEO agreeableness and conscientiousness. Option c is right because the NEO has no psychoticism scale. Exam trap: if a version of this question lists agreeableness instead, it too is not shared, because the EPQ has no agreeableness scale; only neuroticism and extraversion appear in both.", "traps": {"a": "Neuroticism, or emotional instability, is in both: it is one of Eysenck's three superfactors and one of the Big Five domains in the NEO, with facets such as anxiety and depression.", "b": "Extraversion is in both: Eysenck linked it to low cortical arousal, and the NEO includes it as a Big Five domain with facets such as warmth, gregariousness and assertiveness.", "d": "This is wrong because psychoticism is not in the NEO: Costa and McCrae's five domains are neuroticism, extraversion, openness, agreeableness and conscientiousness, with no psychoticism scale."}}, {"q": "Order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation are facets of which NEO-PI-R domain?", "concept": "The answer is d: order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation are facets of the conscientiousness domain of the NEO-PI-R. The NEO-PI-R was published by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992) to measure the Big Five, the five broad trait dimensions that factor analyses of personality keep finding. Its structure is simple arithmetic: 5 domains × 6 facets = 30 facets, and 30 facets × 8 items = 240 items, each rated on a five-point scale. Conscientiousness is the tendency to be organised, reliable, hard-working and self-controlled. Its six facets are competence (feeling capable), order (being neat and organised), dutifulness (keeping rules and promises), achievement striving (working hard towards goals), self-discipline (finishing tasks despite boredom) and deliberation (thinking before acting). Only competence is missing from the question's list. Each other domain has its own six. Neuroticism: anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness, vulnerability. Extraversion: warmth, gregariousness, assertiveness, activity, excitement seeking, positive emotions. Openness: fantasy, aesthetics, feelings, actions, ideas, values. Agreeableness: trust, straightforwardness, altruism, compliance, modesty, tender-mindedness. Murray Barrick and Michael Mount (1991) found conscientiousness to be the most consistent Big Five predictor of job performance across occupations. Example: a student who keeps a planner, submits work before deadlines and checks answers before handing in a paper is high in conscientiousness. Option d is right because all five listed facets belong to conscientiousness. Exam trap: impulsiveness sounds like the opposite of deliberation, but in the NEO-PI-R it is a neuroticism facet, not a conscientiousness one.", "traps": {"a": "Agreeableness has the facets trust, straightforwardness, altruism, compliance, modesty and tender-mindedness; it concerns how a person treats others, not order, duty or self-control.", "b": "Openness to experience has the facets fantasy, aesthetics, feelings, actions, ideas and values; it concerns curiosity and imagination, not dutifulness or self-discipline.", "c": "Extraversion has the facets warmth, gregariousness, assertiveness, activity, excitement seeking and positive emotions; activity and assertiveness can sound like achievement striving but belong here."}}, {"q": "The Bonferroni correction is used to:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Bonferroni correction is used to control the familywise Type I error rate when several comparisons are made. A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis; alpha (often .05) is its chance in one test. When a researcher runs many tests, these chances build up. The familywise error rate is the probability of at least one Type I error anywhere in the family of tests. With five independent tests at .05, the chance of no error in all five is 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 = 0.774, so the chance of at least one false positive is 1 - 0.774 = 0.226, about 23%. The correction is named after the Italian mathematician Carlo Emilio Bonferroni, whose probability inequality underlies it; Olive Jean Dunn (1961) applied it to multiple comparisons of means. The rule is to divide alpha by the number of comparisons. For five tests, .05 / 5 = .01, so each test must reach p ≤ .01, and the familywise rate stays at or below 5 × .01 = .05. Example: comparing four therapy groups in every pair gives 4 × 3 / 2 = 6 t-tests, so the per-test alpha becomes .05 / 6 = .0083. The price is that Bonferroni is conservative: stricter thresholds lower power and raise Type II errors. Option b is right because the correction keeps the overall false-positive rate at the chosen alpha. Exam trap: Bonferroni never increases power; it trades power for protection against false positives.", "traps": {"a": "Bonferroni does the opposite of increasing power: by shrinking the alpha for each test it makes significance harder to reach, so power falls; power rises with larger samples or bigger effects.", "c": "Unequal variances are checked with Levene's test and handled with Welch's t-test, which adjusts the degrees of freedom; Bonferroni deals with the number of tests, not with variances.", "d": "Skewed distributions are normalised with transformations such as the log or square root, or analysed with non-parametric tests; Bonferroni changes only the significance threshold for each test."}}, {"q": "The most effective psychological treatment for a specific phobia, such as an intense fear of dogs, is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the most effective psychological treatment for a specific phobia such as an intense fear of dogs is exposure-based therapy, such as systematic desensitisation or in vivo exposure. A specific phobia is a marked, lasting fear of a particular object or situation that the person avoids. Avoidance brings instant relief, which negatively reinforces it, so the fear never fades. Exposure breaks this cycle through repeated, prolonged contact with the feared thing until fear declines and new safety learning takes hold. Mary Cover Jones (1924) removed a toddler called Peter's fear of a rabbit by bringing it closer bit by bit while he ate. Joseph Wolpe (1958) formalised it as systematic desensitisation, based on reciprocal inhibition: relaxation and anxiety cannot exist together. The client learns deep muscle relaxation, builds a fear hierarchy rated in Subjective Units of Distress (SUDS, 0 to 100), then imagines each step while relaxed, starting at the bottom. In vivo exposure uses real situations, and flooding starts with the most feared situation at once. Lars-Göran Öst (1989) showed that a single guided exposure session of up to three hours can treat many specific phobias. Example: a dog-phobic client climbs a hierarchy: a photo of a dog (SUDS 20), a video (35), a leashed dog across the road (50), standing two metres away (70), and patting a calm dog (90). Option c is right because exposure has the strongest evidence for specific phobia. Exam trap: flooding is also exposure; it differs only in being intense rather than graded.", "traps": {"a": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) offers empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness to support growth; it does not directly extinguish a conditioned fear and lacks exposure's evidence for phobia.", "b": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic technique of saying whatever comes to mind to uncover unconscious conflict; it has little evidence for treating specific phobias.", "d": "Electroconvulsive therapy (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938) induces a brief seizure under anaesthesia; it is used for severe or treatment-resistant depression and catatonia, not for phobias."}}, {"q": "Which therapy was developed specifically for, and has the strongest evidence in, borderline personality disorder?", "concept": "The answer is d: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed specifically for borderline personality disorder and has the strongest evidence in it. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a lasting pattern of unstable emotions, relationships and self-image, with impulsivity, fear of abandonment, and often self-harm and suicidal behaviour. DBT was developed by the American psychologist Marsha Linehan (1993), originally for chronically suicidal women with BPD. Her biosocial theory says BPD grows from a biological vulnerability to intense emotion combined with an invalidating environment, one that dismisses or punishes the child's feelings. Dialectical refers to balancing two opposites: acceptance (validation and mindfulness, drawn from Zen practice) and change (behavioural problem solving). Standard DBT combines weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills group, phone coaching in a crisis and a consultation team for therapists. The skills group teaches four modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Randomised trials have shown less self-harm and fewer suicide attempts and hospital days than usual treatment. Other therapies with evidence in BPD include mentalisation-based treatment (Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy), schema therapy (Jeffrey Young) and transference-focused psychotherapy (Otto Kernberg). Example: a young woman who cuts herself after arguments learns to ride out the urge with distress tolerance skills, such as holding ice, then uses interpersonal effectiveness skills to ask her partner for what she needs. Option d is right because DBT is the only option designed for BPD. Exam trap: DBT is a third-wave cognitive-behavioural therapy, so do not rule it out just because the other options are behavioural too.", "traps": {"a": "Exposure and response prevention, introduced by Victor Meyer (1966), exposes a person to obsessional triggers while blocking compulsions; it is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD, not BPD.", "b": "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus such as a nausea-inducing drug; it targets habits and addictions and has no role in BPD.", "c": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) method of pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy; it is designed for phobias and anxiety, not for the emotional instability of BPD."}}, {"q": "Charles Spearman proposed a general intelligence factor, 'g', after noticing that:", "concept": "The answer is a: Charles Spearman proposed a general intelligence factor, g, after noticing that scores on very different mental tests all correlate positively with one another. Charles Spearman (1904), a British psychologist, set this out in his paper \"'General intelligence', objectively determined and measured\". He studied schoolchildren's marks in subjects such as classics, French, English and mathematics, along with tests of sensory discrimination, and found that a child who did well on one tended to do well on the others. This pattern of all-positive correlations is now called the positive manifold. To explain it, Spearman developed an early form of factor analysis, a method that finds the hidden factors behind a set of correlations. He concluded that every test draws on one general factor, g, shared by all intellectual tasks, plus a specific factor, s, unique to that test: his two-factor theory. Tests differ in how heavily they load on g; abstract reasoning tasks load highly, simple sensory tasks less. Spearman linked g to a kind of general mental energy. Example: a student who scores high on vocabulary will usually score above average on arithmetic and block design too, although each test also needs its own specific skill. Louis Thurstone (1938) later challenged a single g with seven primary mental abilities, but even his abilities correlated with one another, pointing back to g. Option a is right because the positive correlations among diverse tests were the evidence g was built on. Exam trap: \"two-factor theory\" does not mean two kinds of intelligence; it means g plus s.", "traps": {"b": "Eight separate, independent intelligences is Howard Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences (Frames of Mind, 1983), later expanded to eight with the naturalist intelligence; Gardner argued against a single g.", "c": "Spearman never claimed intelligence is entirely inherited, and modern twin and adoption studies put the heritability of IQ at a moderate level, roughly half, varying with age and environment.", "d": "Rising average IQ scores across generations is the Flynn effect, documented by James Flynn in the 1980s, long after Spearman; it is not the observation that led him to propose g."}}, {"q": "In the method of paired comparisons, each stimulus is compared with every other stimulus. With 8 stimuli, how many pairs must a judge rate?", "concept": "The answer is d: with 8 stimuli a judge must rate 28 pairs. In the method of paired comparisons, every stimulus is presented with every other stimulus, one pair at a time, and the judge simply says which member of the pair is preferred, heavier, brighter or more favourable. Louis Thurstone (1927) gave the method its theory in his law of comparative judgement. He assumed that each stimulus produces a spread of reactions on a hidden psychological scale, so the proportion of times A is chosen over B shows how far apart A and B lie. Turning those proportions into z scores yields an interval scale, which Thurstone used in psychophysics and in attitude scaling. The number of pairs follows a simple rule: each stimulus is paired with the n minus 1 others, and each pair is counted once, so pairs = n(n - 1) / 2. Step by step for this item: n = 8; n - 1 = 7; 8 x 7 = 56; 56 / 2 = 28. Example: a researcher ranking 8 brands of tea by taste would serve 28 pairs of cups, and a brand that wins most of its 7 contests ends up at the top of the scale. The count grows fast (20 stimuli need 190 pairs), so the method suits small sets. Option d is right because 8 x 7 / 2 = 28. Exam trap: forgetting to divide by 2 gives 56, which counts A versus B and B versus A as two different pairs.", "traps": {"a": "16 is simply 2 x 8, doubling the number of stimuli; it has no basis in the pairing rule and badly undercounts the 28 distinct pairs a judge must rate.", "b": "64 is 8 x 8 (n squared), which would pair each stimulus with itself and count both orders of every pair; neither happens in the method of paired comparisons.", "c": "56 is n(n - 1) = 8 x 7, the number of ordered pairs; it counts A-B and B-A separately, so it must be halved to give the 28 distinct pairs."}}, {"q": "In Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, which stages come immediately before and immediately after 'autonomy vs shame and doubt'?", "concept": "The answer is b: trust vs mistrust comes before autonomy vs shame and doubt, and initiative vs guilt comes after it. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, proposed eight psychosocial stages that span the whole life. Each stage is a crisis, a turning point between a healthy and an unhealthy pole, and resolving it well leaves a lasting virtue or basic strength. The order is fixed by what Erikson called the epigenetic principle: each stage has its own time of special importance and builds on those before it. The stages are: trust vs mistrust (birth to about 1 year, hope); autonomy vs shame and doubt (about 1 to 3, will); initiative vs guilt (about 3 to 6, purpose); industry vs inferiority (about 6 to 12, competence); identity vs role confusion (adolescence, fidelity); intimacy vs isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity vs stagnation (middle adulthood, care); integrity vs despair (old age, wisdom). Example: a two-year-old who insists on feeding herself and choosing her own clothes is working on autonomy; if her parents mock every spill, she may learn shame and doubt. A year earlier the issue was whether her caregivers were reliable (trust); a few years later it will be whether she can plan and start activities without guilt (initiative). Option b is right because it gives the true neighbours of stage 2: stage 1 before and stage 3 after. Exam trap: industry vs inferiority is stage 4, the school years, so it comes two steps after autonomy, not next.", "traps": {"a": "Initiative vs guilt is stage 3 and industry vs inferiority is stage 4; both come after autonomy vs shame and doubt, so this option wrongly places stage 3 before it.", "c": "Trust vs mistrust is correctly placed before, but industry vs inferiority (about 6 to 12 years, competence) is stage 4; initiative vs guilt, stage 3, comes directly after autonomy.", "d": "Intimacy vs isolation (stage 6, young adulthood) and identity vs role confusion (stage 5, adolescence) are adult and teenage crises far from toddlerhood, and here even their order is reversed."}}, {"q": "During psychoanalysis, a client keeps 'forgetting' appointments and changes the subject whenever painful memories come close. Sigmund Freud called this:", "concept": "The answer is d: Freud called this resistance. Resistance is the client's unconscious opposition to the work of therapy, especially to bringing repressed, painful material into awareness. Sigmund Freud described it in his chapter of Studies on Hysteria (1895), written with Josef Breuer, when he noticed that patients pushed back against the very memories whose recall would relieve their symptoms. The same forces that repressed a memory in the first place keep it out of consciousness during treatment, so the closer the analysis comes to a sensitive area, the stronger the resistance becomes. It takes many forms: missing or forgetting sessions, arriving late, long silences, changing the subject, joking, intellectualising, or suddenly feeling cured. Freud came to see resistance not as a nuisance but as a signpost. The analyst notices it, interprets it and helps the client understand what it protects, and this slow process of working through is central to psychoanalysis. Example: a woman in analysis talks freely about her job, but each time her father's drinking comes up she says she has nothing to say, and then she forgets her next appointment. The analyst might gently point out the pattern and ask what makes that topic so hard to approach. Option d is right because forgetting appointments and dodging painful memories are classic signs of opposing the uncovering process. Exam trap: transference can also slow therapy, but it means directing old feelings onto the analyst; behaviour that steers away from the painful material itself is resistance.", "traps": {"a": "Transference is the client redirecting feelings from past figures, such as parents, onto the analyst, for example treating the analyst like a strict father; it is not avoidance of painful memories.", "b": "Countertransference is the analyst's own emotional reaction to the client, often stirred by the analyst's unresolved conflicts; it concerns the therapist's feelings, not the client's avoidance.", "c": "Catharsis is the release of pent-up emotion when a traumatic memory is recalled, the basis of Josef Breuer's cathartic method with Anna O in Studies on Hysteria (1895); it is the opposite of avoidance."}}, {"q": "A 10-year-old helps around the house mainly so that his parents will see him as a 'good boy'. In Lawrence Kohlberg's theory he is reasoning at:", "concept": "The answer is b: the boy is reasoning at Stage 3, the good boy-good girl (interpersonal conformity) orientation. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), in his doctoral dissertation at the University of Chicago, studied moral reasoning by giving boys dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasons behind a choice, not the choice itself. He proposed three levels with two stages each. Preconventional: Stage 1, punishment and obedience (right is whatever avoids punishment); Stage 2, instrumental exchange (right is what serves my interests, 'you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours'). Conventional: Stage 3, good boy-good girl (right is what pleases others and earns their approval); Stage 4, law and order (right is doing one's duty and keeping the social system going). Postconventional: Stage 5, social contract (laws are agreements that can be changed for the common good); Stage 6, universal ethical principles (justice and human dignity). Example: asked why Heinz should steal, a Stage 3 child says, 'His family would think he is a bad husband if he let her die.' Carol Gilligan (1982) later argued that this justice-based scheme undervalued an ethic of care. Option b is right because the boy acts mainly to be seen as a 'good boy' by his parents, which is approval-seeking. Exam trap: Stage 2 also involves doing things for others, but for a concrete return; Stage 3 helps to win approval and be thought of as nice.", "traps": {"a": "Stage 1, punishment and obedience, judges right and wrong by whether an act is punished; the boy would help only to avoid a scolding, not to be seen as good.", "c": "Stage 2, instrumental exchange (naive hedonism), is a deal-making view: he would help to get pocket money or a favour in return, not to win his parents' approval.", "d": "Stage 4, law and order, centres on duty, rules and keeping society running; the boy would help because a son is obliged to and rules must be kept, not to look good."}}, {"q": "Match each theme with its theorist. (i) Attachment theory (ii) Parenting styles (iii) Ecological systems theory (iv) Zone of proximal development. A: Diana Baumrind, B: John Bowlby, C: Lev Vygotsky, D: Urie Bronfenbrenner", "concept": "The answer is a: attachment theory goes with John Bowlby, parenting styles with Diana Baumrind, ecological systems theory with Urie Bronfenbrenner and the zone of proximal development with Lev Vygotsky. Bowlby, a British psychiatrist, set out attachment theory in his trilogy Attachment (1969), Separation (1973) and Loss (1980). He argued that infants are biologically prepared to bond with a caregiver, that one main figure matters most (monotropy) and that early bonds become an internal working model for later relationships. Baumrind (1966, 1971), by crossing warmth with control, described authoritative (warm and firm), authoritarian (strict and cold) and permissive (warm but lax) styles; Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) added the neglectful style. Bronfenbrenner (1979) placed the child inside nested systems: the microsystem (family, school), the mesosystem (links between them), the exosystem (settings that affect the child indirectly, such as a parent's job) and the macrosystem (culture); he later added the chronosystem (change over time). Vygotsky, the Soviet psychologist whose ideas reached English readers in Mind in Society (1978), defined the zone of proximal development as the gap between what a child can do alone and what she can do with skilled help. Example: a child whose father loses his job (exosystem) may meet harsher, authoritarian parenting at home, which can strain a once secure attachment. Option a is right because it is the only option with all four pairs correct. Exam trap: Bowlby and Baumrind both start with B, and Bronfenbrenner and Vygotsky both stress social context, so check every pair.", "traps": {"b": "This swaps the first two: Diana Baumrind did not develop attachment theory and John Bowlby did not classify parenting styles; the last two pairs are right, but one swap makes the option wrong.", "c": "This swaps the last two: Lev Vygotsky did not propose ecological systems theory, and Urie Bronfenbrenner did not coin the zone of proximal development.", "d": "This gives attachment to Bronfenbrenner and ecological systems to Bowlby, reversing two pioneers: Bowlby is attachment (1969 to 1980) and Bronfenbrenner is ecological systems (1979)."}}, {"q": "A girl with a ratio IQ of 125 has a chronological age of 8 years. What is her mental age?", "concept": "The answer is c: her mental age is 10 years. Mental age (MA) is the age level at which a child performs on an intelligence test; the idea grew out of the age-graded scales of Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905, 1908). William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age (CA) to get a mental quotient, and Lewis Terman multiplied it by 100 in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, giving the ratio IQ: IQ = MA / CA x 100. To find MA, rearrange the formula: MA = IQ x CA / 100. Step by step: IQ x CA = 125 x 8 = 1000; 1000 / 100 = 10 years. Check: 10 / 8 = 1.25, and 1.25 x 100 = 125. To find CA instead, use CA = MA x 100 / IQ; for example, a boy with MA 9 and IQ 75 has CA = 9 x 100 / 75 = 900 / 75 = 12 years. Ratio IQ fails for adults, because mental age stops rising in the late teens while chronological age keeps growing. David Wechsler (1939) therefore introduced the deviation IQ, which compares a person with others of the same age on a scale with mean 100 and standard deviation 15. Option c is right because 125 x 8 / 100 = 10. Exam trap: dividing the wrong way (8 x 100 / 125 = 6.4) gives an MA below the CA, which is impossible when the IQ is above 100.", "traps": {"a": "9 years would give IQ = 9 / 8 x 100 = 112.5, not 125; it looks like a guess that she is one year ahead rather than the result of the formula.", "b": "12.5 is just 125 divided by 10; it ignores chronological age entirely and would give IQ = 12.5 / 8 x 100 = 156.25.", "d": "6.4 comes from 8 x 100 / 125, the CA formula misapplied; a mental age below the chronological age would mean an IQ under 100, contradicting her IQ of 125."}}, {"q": "Conformity and obedience differ in the kind of social pressure involved. Which pairing is correct?", "concept": "The answer is a: in conformity the pressure is implicit and comes from peers or the group, whereas in obedience it is an explicit, direct command from an authority. Social influence can be arranged as a ladder of increasing directness. Conformity means changing behaviour or belief to match a group when nobody has asked you to; the pressure comes from the group's unspoken norms. Solomon Asch (1951) showed it in his line-judgement studies, where students gave clearly wrong answers to match a unanimous group of confederates. Compliance is going along with an explicit request, usually from an equal, as in the foot-in-the-door and door-in-the-face tactics. Obedience is following a direct order from someone seen as a legitimate authority; Stanley Milgram (1963) found that 65% of his participants obeyed an experimenter's commands up to what they believed was a 450-volt shock. So conformity is horizontal (between equals) and implicit, while obedience is vertical (from above) and explicit. Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955) explained why we conform: normative influence (the wish to be liked and accepted) and informational influence (the belief that the group knows better). Example: a new employee starts wearing formal clothes because everyone else does (conformity); when the manager says 'Wear a tie tomorrow' and he does, that is obedience. Option a is right because it matches each process with its true source of pressure. Exam trap: compliance also involves an explicit request, but from a peer rather than an authority, so 'explicit' alone does not mean obedience.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the two: an explicit command from an authority defines obedience, as in Stanley Milgram's studies, and implicit peer pressure defines conformity, as in Solomon Asch's studies.", "c": "Only obedience involves explicit commands from an authority; in conformity nobody gives an order, as in Asch's studies, where the confederates simply stated their wrong answers aloud.", "d": "Only conformity rests on implicit group pressure; obedience, as in Milgram's studies, involves an authority who explicitly says 'Please continue' or 'You have no other choice, you must go on'."}}, {"q": "Which of the following does NOT describe Raymond Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test (CFIT)?", "concept": "The answer is a: the CFIT is not a performance test in which objects are handled and arranged. Raymond Cattell (1940) published an early 'culture-free' intelligence test, later revised as the Culture Fair Intelligence Test and issued by his Institute for Personality and Ability Testing (IPAT, founded 1949). It aims to measure fluid intelligence (Gf), the ability to reason with new problems, while keeping the effects of language, schooling and culture as low as possible. It comes in three scales for different ages. Scales 2 and 3 are paper-and-pencil booklets that can be given to groups, each with four subtests: series (choose the figure that completes a sequence), classification (find the odd figure out), matrices (complete a pattern) and conditions or topology (find the figure where a dot can be placed in the same relation to the shapes). All items use figures, so no reading is needed, and every subtest has a strict time limit, so the test is speeded. A performance test, by contrast, requires the person to manipulate concrete material, as in Kohs Block Design, Alexander's Pass-Along Test or the Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests. Example: a child who cannot read English can still take the CFIT by marking which drawing completes a row of shapes. Option a is right because the CFIT is a figural paper-and-pencil test, not a test of handling objects. Exam trap: 'non-verbal' does not mean 'performance'; a test can avoid words and still be done with pencil and paper. Key note: the recall list says the CIP 2023 options were speed test, power test, non-verbal test and performance test, and that the first three were true but 'performance test' was not. That matches this item: the CFIT is timed, non-verbal and measures reasoning power, but it is not a performance test.", "traps": {"b": "This does describe the CFIT: Scales 2 and 3 are printed booklets answered with pencil and paper, and they can be given to whole groups at once.", "c": "This does describe the CFIT: every item uses figures, shapes and patterns (series, classification, matrices, conditions), so no reading or vocabulary is required.", "d": "This is the very purpose of the CFIT: Cattell designed it to reduce the influence of language, schooling and cultural background on the measurement of fluid intelligence."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a feature of a major depressive episode 'with atypical features' in DSM-5-TR?", "concept": "The answer is b: mood reactivity, in which mood brightens in response to positive events, is a feature of depression with atypical features. In DSM-5-TR (2022), 'with atypical features' is a specifier added to a major depressive episode. Mood reactivity is required, plus at least two of four other features: significant weight gain or increased appetite; hypersomnia (sleeping too much); leaden paralysis (heavy, leaden feelings in the arms or legs); and a long-standing pattern of sensitivity to interpersonal rejection that causes real social or work problems. The specifier cannot be given if the same episode meets criteria for melancholic or catatonic features. It entered the manual in DSM-IV (1994), building on work by Frederic Quitkin and colleagues at Columbia University in the 1980s, who found that these patients responded better to monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) than to tricyclic antidepressants. Its features are almost the mirror image of melancholia, the classic 'typical' depression, whose signs include loss of pleasure, lack of mood reactivity, early morning awakening, depression regularly worse in the morning, marked weight loss, psychomotor change and excessive guilt. Example: a 25-year-old woman has felt low for weeks, yet she cheers up when friends visit; she sleeps eleven hours a night, craves sweets, has gained weight, says her limbs feel like lead and is crushed by any hint of criticism. Option b is right because mood reactivity is the one required atypical feature. Exam trap: the other three options are classic melancholic features, so think 'reverse of melancholia' when you see atypical.", "traps": {"a": "Early morning awakening, at least two hours before the usual time, is a melancholic feature; atypical depression shows the opposite sleep pattern, hypersomnia.", "c": "Marked loss of appetite and weight is a melancholic feature; atypical depression is marked instead by increased appetite or significant weight gain.", "d": "Depression that is regularly worse in the morning (diurnal variation) is listed in DSM-5-TR as a melancholic feature, not an atypical one."}}, {"q": "A chemical that a neuron releases into the synaptic cleft and that acts on a nearby target cell across that small gap is a:", "concept": "The answer is c: a chemical released into the synaptic cleft that acts on a nearby target cell is a neurotransmitter. Neurons communicate across synapses, tiny gaps between the end of one neuron and the next cell. Otto Loewi (1921) proved that this communication is chemical. He stimulated the vagus nerve of a frog's heart, which slowed it, then transferred the fluid bathing that heart to a second heart, which also slowed. He called the substance Vagusstoff; it was later identified as acetylcholine, and Loewi shared the 1936 Nobel Prize with Henry Dale. A substance counts as a neurotransmitter when it is made in the neuron, stored in synaptic vesicles in the terminal button, released when an action potential arrives (calcium enters and the vesicles fuse with the membrane), binds to receptors on the postsynaptic cell, and is then quickly removed by reuptake or broken down by an enzyme. Neurohormones, hormones and pheromones also carry chemical messages, but they travel farther: through the blood, or even outside the body. Example: at the neuromuscular junction, a motor neuron releases acetylcholine, which binds to receptors on the muscle fibre and makes it contract; the enzyme acetylcholinesterase then breaks it down so the muscle can relax. Option c is right because release into the synaptic cleft onto a nearby cell is the defining feature of a neurotransmitter. Exam trap: a neurohormone is also released by a neuron, so the deciding clue is where it goes: into the cleft (neurotransmitter) or into the blood (neurohormone).", "traps": {"a": "A hormone is secreted by an endocrine gland, such as the thyroid or adrenal gland, into the bloodstream and acts on distant target organs, often slowly; it is not released into a synaptic cleft.", "b": "A neurohormone is released by a neuron into the blood rather than into a synapse, for example oxytocin and vasopressin from the posterior pituitary, so it acts on distant targets.", "d": "A pheromone is a chemical released outside the body that signals to other members of the same species, such as the sex attractants of moths; it does not act across a synapse."}}, {"q": "The smallest unit of language that carries meaning is the:", "concept": "The answer is a: the morpheme is the smallest unit of language that carries meaning. Language is built in layers: sounds combine into meaningful units, these form words, words form phrases and sentences, and rules govern each step. The term 'morpheme' came from the Polish linguist Jan Baudouin de Courtenay in the late nineteenth century, and Leonard Bloomfield's book Language (1933) made it a basic idea of linguistics. A morpheme cannot be split into smaller parts that still mean something. Free morphemes can stand alone as words (dog, happy, run); bound morphemes must attach to another morpheme (the plural -s, the past tense -ed, prefixes such as un- and suffixes such as -ness). So 'dog' is one morpheme, 'dogs' is two, and 'unhappiness' is three: un + happy + ness. A morpheme differs from a phoneme, the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning without having any meaning itself. The /b/ and /p/ in 'bat' and 'pat' are phonemes; English has about 40 to 44 of them. Above these units sit syntax (rules for ordering words into sentences), semantics (meaning) and pragmatics (how language is used in social settings). Example: a child who says 'two foots' has learned that the bound morpheme -s marks the plural, even though she applies it to the wrong word. Option a is right because the morpheme is defined as the minimal unit of meaning. Exam trap: if a question asks for the smallest unit of sound, or the basic unit of speech, the answer switches to phoneme; the word 'meaning' points to morpheme.", "traps": {"b": "A phoneme is the smallest unit of speech sound, such as /b/ or /p/; it can change a word's meaning (bat versus pat) but carries no meaning of its own.", "c": "Syntax is the set of rules for combining words into phrases and sentences, such as word order; it is a system of rules, not a unit that carries meaning.", "d": "Pragmatics is the social use of language, such as taking turns, politeness and knowing that 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request; it is not a unit at all."}}, {"q": "A tutor gives a child a lot of help with a new puzzle and then gradually withdraws that help as the child becomes able to solve it alone. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: giving a lot of help at first and then gradually withdrawing it as the child becomes able to manage alone is called scaffolding. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) coined the term in a study in which a tutor helped 3- to 5-year-olds build a pyramid from interlocking wooden blocks. Like the temporary scaffolding around a building, the support holds the learner up while a skill is being built and is taken away once the structure can stand on its own. Wood and his colleagues listed what a good tutor does: recruits the child's interest, simplifies the task, keeps the child on track, marks the important features, controls frustration and demonstrates solutions. The key rule is contingency: help increases when the child struggles and decreases when the child succeeds. Scaffolding is closely tied to the zone of proximal development of Lev Vygotsky, the gap between what a child can do alone and what she can do with a more skilled partner. Scaffolding is the practical way an adult works inside that zone, although Vygotsky himself never used the word. Example: when teaching a child to ride a bicycle, a parent first holds the seat firmly, then holds it loosely, then just runs alongside, and finally lets go completely. Option c is right because the gradual withdrawal of support as competence grows is the defining feature of scaffolding. Exam trap: shaping also builds skills in small steps, but by reinforcing closer and closer approximations to a target, not by fading an adult's help.", "traps": {"a": "Conservation is Jean Piaget's term for understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in appearance, such as water poured into a taller glass; it is a concrete operational achievement, not a tutoring method.", "b": "Assimilation is Jean Piaget's term for fitting new experiences into existing schemes, as when a toddler calls every four-legged animal 'doggie'; it describes the child's thinking, not adult support.", "d": "Shaping is B. F. Skinner's operant technique of reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour; it builds skills step by step through rewards, with no idea of withdrawing support."}}, {"q": "In the prisoner's dilemma, each player does better individually by ______, yet both end up worse off than if both had ______.", "concept": "The answer is b: each player does better individually by defecting (confessing), yet both end up worse off than if both had cooperated (stayed silent). The game was devised by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher (1950) at the RAND Corporation, and the mathematician Albert Tucker gave it the prisoners' story and its name. Two suspects are questioned separately. In a typical version, if both stay silent each gets 1 year; if one confesses and the other stays silent, the confessor goes free and the silent one gets 10 years; if both confess, each gets 5 years. From one prisoner's view: if my partner stays silent, confessing gives me 0 years instead of 1; if my partner confesses, confessing gives me 5 years instead of 10. Either way confessing is better for me, so it is the dominant strategy, and both confessing is the Nash equilibrium. Yet 5 years each is worse than the 1 year each they would get by both staying silent. This makes it a mixed-motive game and a model of social dilemmas, where individual rationality leads to collective loss. When the game is repeated, cooperation can win: in Robert Axelrod's computer tournaments around 1980, the simple tit-for-tat strategy (cooperate first, then copy the partner's last move) scored best. Example: two rival shops each cut prices to steal customers and both earn less than if neither had. Option b is right because defection is individually best but mutually harmful. Exam trap: option a reverses the logic; cooperation pays only when both players cooperate.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the dilemma: staying silent is individually risky because the partner may confess and leave you with the longest sentence; mutual cooperation, not defection, gives the better joint outcome.", "c": "Withdrawing is not a move in the classic prisoner's dilemma; each player can only cooperate or defect, and competing (defecting) is the individually tempting choice, not the better joint one.", "d": "This reverses the logic in the language of trust: trusting the partner (staying silent) is individually risky, and mutual distrust (both confessing) is the bad outcome, not the better one."}}, {"q": "In research practice, the questionable practice called HARKing stands for:", "concept": "The answer is a: HARKing stands for Hypothesizing After the Results are Known. Norbert Kerr (1998) coined the term for a common questionable research practice: looking at the data, finding a pattern, and then writing the report as if that pattern had been predicted from the start. The problem is not exploring data; exploration is valuable. The problem is disguising an after-the-fact (post hoc) idea as a before-the-fact (a priori) hypothesis. When many patterns are possible, some will appear by chance alone, so a hypothesis built to fit one of them will look strongly confirmed even if it is just noise. HARKing therefore inflates false positives, hides the hypotheses that failed and gives readers a false picture of how strong a theory is. It became a central concern in the replication crisis of the 2010s, alongside p-hacking (trying many analyses until one gives p below .05). The main remedies are pre-registration, in which hypotheses and analyses are filed publicly before data collection, and Registered Reports, in which a journal reviews and accepts the study plan before the results exist. Example: a researcher predicts that caffeine will improve memory, finds no overall effect, notices that it helped only students tested in the afternoon, and publishes a paper whose introduction 'predicts' an afternoon effect. Option a is right because Kerr's acronym spells out exactly this practice. Exam trap: HARKing is not simply forming ideas after seeing data; it is presenting such an idea as if it had been made in advance.", "traps": {"b": "'Hypothesis And Research Keying' is an invented expansion; no such term exists in research methods, and 'keying' refers to scoring test items, not to forming hypotheses.", "c": "'Holding Analyses Rigid until Known' is invented; fixing analyses in advance actually describes pre-registration, which is the cure for HARKing, not HARKing itself.", "d": "'Hierarchical Analysis of Research Knowledge' is invented; it borrows the sound of real terms such as hierarchical regression but has nothing to do with Kerr's 1998 concept."}}, {"q": "In the Atkinson-Shiffrin multi-store model (1968), information passes through the memory stores in which order?", "concept": "The answer is d: information passes from the sensory register to the short-term store and then to the long-term store. Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) proposed this multi-store model, often called the modal model, which treats memory as a flow of information through three stores linked by control processes. First, the sensory register holds a brief, raw copy of what the senses take in. George Sperling (1960) showed that the visual (iconic) store lasts only about a quarter to half a second; the auditory (echoic) store lasts about 2 to 4 seconds. Only what we attend to moves on. Second, the short-term store holds a small amount, about 7 plus or minus 2 items according to George Miller (1956), for roughly 18 to 30 seconds unless it is rehearsed; Lloyd and Margaret Peterson (1959) showed rapid forgetting when rehearsal was blocked. Third, rehearsal transfers information to the long-term store, which seems unlimited in capacity and can last a lifetime. Example: you glance at a phone number (sensory register), repeat it while dialling (short-term store), and after using it many times you know it by heart (long-term store). Later, Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) replaced the single short-term store with a multi-part working memory, and Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) argued that depth of processing matters more than rehearsal. Option d is right because it gives the sensory, short-term, long-term order. Exam trap: the retrieval route from long-term back to short-term does not change the order in which new information enters.", "traps": {"a": "This puts the sensory register second, but sensory input must be registered first; only what is attended to in the sensory register reaches the short-term store.", "b": "This is the full reverse of encoding; only retrieval moves information from the long-term store back to the short-term store, and nothing flows back into the sensory register.", "c": "This skips the short-term store; in the model, information must be attended to and rehearsed in the short-term store before it can reach the long-term store."}}, {"q": "Using interviews, observation and questionnaires to study the same research question, so that each set of findings can cross-check the others, is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: using interviews, observation and questionnaires on the same question so that each set of findings cross-checks the others is called triangulation. The word comes from navigation and surveying, where the position of a point is fixed by taking bearings from two or more known points. In research it means approaching the same question from several angles, so that the weaknesses of one approach are balanced by the strengths of another. Norman Denzin (1970), in The Research Act, described four types: data triangulation (different times, places or people), investigator triangulation (several researchers), theory triangulation (several theoretical lenses) and methodological triangulation (several methods), which he further split into within-method and between-method forms. When the different sources agree, confidence in the conclusion grows; when they disagree, the gap itself becomes something to explain. Triangulation is a key way to build credibility, one of the trustworthiness criteria of Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985), and it is central to mixed-methods research. Example: a researcher studying stress among nurses interviews nurses about their experiences, observes ward routines during night shifts and gives a standard stress questionnaire. If all three show that understaffing drives stress, the finding is far more convincing than any one source alone. Option c is right because combining methods so that each set of results checks the others is exactly what triangulation means. Exam trap: despite the 'tri', triangulation does not require exactly three methods; two or more sources or methods are enough.", "traps": {"a": "Randomisation is the random assignment of participants to conditions, championed by Ronald Fisher, so that groups are equal on average; it controls bias but does not cross-check findings from different methods.", "b": "Counterbalancing varies the order of conditions across participants (for example AB for half and BA for the other half) to control order and practice effects in within-subjects designs.", "d": "Matching equates groups on a relevant variable, such as pairing participants of similar IQ across conditions; it is a technique of experimental control, not the use of several data sources."}}, {"q": "Which is the correct order of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, from the base of the pyramid to the top?", "concept": "The answer is c: from base to top, Maslow's hierarchy runs physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, self-actualisation. Abraham Maslow (1943) set out the hierarchy in 'A Theory of Human Motivation' and developed it in Motivation and Personality (1954). His central idea is prepotency: lower needs are more urgent, and higher needs become strong motivators only when the ones below are reasonably, though not completely, satisfied. Physiological needs (food, water, sleep, warmth) come first. Next are safety needs (security, order, freedom from fear), then love and belongingness needs (friends, family, acceptance in a group), then esteem needs (self-respect, competence and recognition from others). At the top is self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming. Maslow called the lower four deficiency needs (D-needs), because their absence creates a tension we try to reduce, and called the growth motives at the top being needs (B-needs). Later he also wrote of cognitive and aesthetic needs and, near the end of his life, of self-transcendence. Example: a man who has just lost his home in a flood thinks first about food and water, then about a safe place to stay; only once these are settled does he miss his friends, then care about his standing at work, and later about painting again. Option c is right because it is the only option with the five needs in the correct order. Exam trap: students often swap esteem and belongingness; remember 'Please Stop Licking Every Sweet': physiological, safety, love, esteem, self-actualisation.", "traps": {"a": "This places love and belongingness second, below safety; in Maslow's hierarchy safety needs (security, freedom from fear) sit directly above physiological needs and below belongingness.", "b": "This puts safety at the base and esteem beneath love and belongingness; in fact physiological needs form the base, and love and belongingness come before esteem.", "d": "This swaps esteem and love and belongingness; Maslow placed the need for acceptance and affection below the need for respect and recognition."}}, {"q": "A toddler carries one particular soft blanket everywhere and cannot fall asleep without it. Donald Winnicott called such a thing a:", "concept": "The answer is b: Winnicott called such a thing a transitional object. Donald Winnicott (1953), a British paediatrician and psychoanalyst, described it in his paper 'Transitional Objects and Transitional Phenomena'. A transitional object is the infant's first 'not-me' possession: a blanket, a soft toy or the corner of a sheet, chosen by the child and treated as uniquely precious. It belongs to an intermediate area of experience between inner reality (the baby's sense that the mother is part of himself) and outer reality (the discovery that she is a separate person who comes and goes). The object stands in for the comfort of the mother, so it helps the child tolerate separation, cope with anxiety, especially at bedtime, and move gradually towards independence. The child insists that it must not be changed, may both cuddle and maul it, and in time it simply loses its meaning rather than being mourned. Winnicott saw this as a normal, healthy step, made possible by a 'good-enough mother' who provides a reliable holding environment and then lets the infant face small, bearable frustrations. He traced the same intermediate space into later play, creativity and culture. Example: Linus in the Peanuts comic strip, who drags his blanket everywhere, is the best-known picture of a transitional object. Option b is right because a special soft item that soothes the toddler in the mother's absence is exactly Winnicott's concept. Exam trap: a secure base also gives comfort, but in attachment theory it is the caregiver, a person, not an object.", "traps": {"a": "Part object is Melanie Klein's term for relating to part of a person as if it were the whole, such as the 'good breast' and 'bad breast' of early infancy; it is not a comfort possession.", "c": "A secure base, in the work of John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, is the attachment figure from whom the child explores and to whom the child returns; it is a person, not a blanket.", "d": "A selfobject is Heinz Kohut's self psychology term for another person experienced as part of the self, who mirrors or is idealised and so supports self-esteem; it is not a toddler's blanket."}}, {"q": "Research carried out by practitioners to solve a problem in their own setting, in repeated cycles of planning, acting, observing and reflecting, is called ______ and was introduced by ______.", "concept": "The answer is a: this is action research, introduced by Kurt Lewin. Kurt Lewin (1946), the German-American founder of modern social psychology, set out the idea in 'Action Research and Minority Problems'. He wanted research that would not just describe social problems, such as prejudice or poor working conditions, but help solve them, and he described it as a spiral of steps: plan, take action, find out what happened, then plan again. Later writers in education, notably Stephen Kemmis, turned this into the familiar cycle of planning, acting, observing and reflecting. Its defining features are that the practitioners themselves (teachers, nurses, managers, community members) do the research in their own setting, the aim is practical change rather than general laws, and the process repeats until the problem improves. When the people affected are full partners at every stage, it is called participatory action research. The trade-off is that findings are tied to one local setting and are hard to generalise, and the researcher is not a neutral outsider. Example: a school counsellor notices that many students skip the first class of the day, plans a peer-mentoring scheme, runs it for a month, tracks attendance, reflects with the teachers on what worked, and then revises the scheme for the next cycle. Option a is right because practitioner-led cycles of planning, acting, observing and reflecting are what Lewin named action research. Exam trap: evaluation research also judges whether a programme works, but it is usually done by outside evaluators to deliver a verdict, not by practitioners in repeated improvement cycles.", "traps": {"b": "Basic research seeks knowledge for its own sake, not solutions to a local problem; Wilhelm Wundt, who founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, is linked with laboratory science, not action research.", "c": "Ex post facto research, defined by Fred Kerlinger, examines possible causes after the effect has already occurred, without manipulating any variable; it involves no cycles of action by practitioners.", "d": "Evaluation research judges whether a programme meets its goals, and Donald Campbell argued in 'Reforms as Experiments' (1969) for testing social reforms this way; it is not Lewin's practitioner cycle."}}, {"q": "In Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant is extremely upset when the mother leaves; when she returns, the infant clings to her but also pushes her away angrily and cannot be comforted. This attachment pattern is:", "concept": "The answer is d: this is the insecure-resistant (ambivalent) attachment pattern. Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978) reported in Patterns of Attachment the results of the Strange Situation, a laboratory procedure for infants of about 12 to 18 months. It has eight short episodes in which the mother and a stranger come and go, and observers note exploration, distress at separation and, above all, behaviour at reunion. In the original Baltimore samples, about 65 to 70% of infants were secure (Type B): they used the mother as a secure base, were upset when she left and were quickly comforted when she returned. About 20% were insecure-avoidant (Type A): little distress, and they ignored or turned away from the mother at reunion. About 10 to 15% were insecure-resistant or ambivalent (Type C): clingy and reluctant to explore even with the mother present, extremely distressed at separation, and at reunion they sought contact but also resisted it, arching away, hitting or pushing, and could not be settled. Ainsworth linked this pattern to inconsistent caregiving, warm at some times and unavailable at others. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986, 1990) later added a disorganised pattern (Type D). Example: an infant cries hard when the mother leaves, reaches for her on return, then pushes her away angrily and keeps crying. Option d is right because clinging combined with angry resistance and failure to be soothed defines resistant attachment. Exam trap: disorganised infants also behave oddly, but they lack any coherent strategy, freezing or approaching with the face turned away.", "traps": {"a": "Securely attached infants (Type B) are upset when the mother leaves but greet her warmly and are soon comforted on her return; they do not push her away angrily.", "b": "Insecure-avoidant infants (Type A) show little distress when the mother leaves and ignore or turn away from her at reunion; they neither cling nor protest.", "c": "Disorganised attachment (Type D), added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon, shows contradictory, confused behaviour such as freezing or approaching with the face averted, often linked to frightened or frightening caregiving."}}, {"q": "Which psychoanalyst rejected Sigmund Freud's concept of penis envy, arguing that women envy men's social power and that men may feel 'womb envy'?", "concept": "The answer is a: Karen Horney rejected Sigmund Freud's concept of penis envy. Freud held that a girl who discovers she lacks a penis feels inferior and envious, and that this envy shapes female personality. Karen Horney (1885 to 1952), a German-born psychoanalyst who later worked in the United States, challenged this in essays written between 1922 and 1937, such as \"The Flight from Womanhood\" (1926); they were collected after her death as Feminine Psychology (1967). She argued that what women envy is not the organ but the social power, freedom and status that men enjoy in a male-dominated culture. She also turned the argument around: men may feel womb envy, envy of women's capacity for pregnancy, birth and motherhood, and may compensate through achievement. For Horney, culture, not anatomy, shapes the psychology of women. Her wider theory rests on basic anxiety, a child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a hostile world, which the person meets by moving toward, against or away from people. She also contrasted the real self with an idealised self that imposes a \"tyranny of the shoulds\". Example: a girl who wishes she were a boy because her brothers may study, travel and choose careers while she may not is, on Horney's view, envying their privileges, not their anatomy. Option a is right because Horney replaced penis envy with envy of men's social power and proposed womb envy in return. Exam trap: Carl Jung also broke with Freud, but his link to this topic is the term Electra complex, not a feminist critique.", "traps": {"b": "Melanie Klein founded the object-relations tradition, with play technique and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; she stressed the infant's envy of the breast, not men's social power as the real object of envy.", "c": "Anna Freud, Sigmund Freud's daughter, set out the ego's defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936) and pioneered child analysis; she stayed loyal to her father's theory rather than attacking penis envy.", "d": "Carl Jung founded analytical psychology and coined the term Electra complex, which Freud himself rejected; Jung broke with Freud over the sexual nature of libido, not over penis envy."}}, {"q": "Children talking aloud to themselves while working on a task was called ______ by Lev Vygotsky, who saw it as a tool for guiding their own behaviour, and ______ by Jean Piaget, who saw it as a sign of cognitive immaturity.", "concept": "The answer is d: Lev Vygotsky called it private speech and Jean Piaget called it egocentric speech. Preschool children often talk aloud to themselves while drawing, building or solving puzzles. The two great developmental theorists, both born in 1896, read this habit in opposite ways. Jean Piaget, in The Language and Thought of the Child (1923), called it egocentric speech: talk not adapted to a listener, seen in repetition, monologue and collective monologue (children \"talking together\" without really answering each other). For him it reflected the child's inability to take another person's viewpoint, and it simply faded as the child decentred. Lev Vygotsky, in Thinking and Speech (1934; translated as Thought and Language), called it private speech. He argued that speech begins as social communication, then turns back on the self to guide one's own actions, and finally goes underground as silent inner speech, a tool of thought. Laura Berk's research supports Vygotsky: children use more private speech when a task is harder, and it tends to go with better performance. Example: a five-year-old doing a jigsaw mutters, \"No, the corner piece goes here, then the blue one\"; she is steering her own behaviour, as Vygotsky claimed. Option d is right because it puts private speech with Vygotsky and egocentric speech with Piaget, in the order the stem asks. Exam trap: option b names the same two terms in reverse order, so check which theorist each blank refers to before choosing.", "traps": {"a": "Holophrastic speech is the one-word stage, about 12 to 18 months, when a single word such as \"milk\" stands for a whole sentence; it is a stage of language, not self-talk, and private speech is Vygotsky's term, not Piaget's.", "b": "Egocentric speech then private speech reverses the pairing: egocentric speech is Piaget's term and private speech is Vygotsky's, so this order gives each theorist the other's label.", "c": "Private speech for Vygotsky is correct, but telegraphic speech is the two-word stage of about 18 to 24 months (\"want ball\"), a stage of language development, not Piaget's label for children's self-talk."}}, {"q": "An adult has no memory at all of a frightening childhood event; the memory was pushed out of awareness without any conscious effort. Which defence mechanism is this?", "concept": "The answer is b: this is repression. Repression is the unconscious, motivated forgetting of threatening thoughts, memories or wishes: the ego pushes them out of awareness without the person knowing it is doing so. Sigmund Freud, who gave it a paper of its own, \"Repression\" (1915), called it the cornerstone on which the whole structure of psychoanalysis rests, and saw it as the primary defence on which the others build. His daughter Anna Freud set out the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), repression among them. A repressed memory does not vanish. It stays active in the unconscious and may return in disguised form through dreams, slips of the tongue or symptoms, which is why keeping it down costs psychic energy. Defences differ in whether they are conscious and in what they do with the threat. Repression blocks an inner memory or impulse unconsciously. Suppression blocks it consciously. Denial refuses to accept an outer fact, and displacement shifts an impulse onto a safer target. Example: a woman who nearly drowned at the age of five cannot recall the event at all, yet feels unexplained dread near deep water. Option b is right because the memory was removed from awareness without conscious effort, which is the defining feature of repression. Exam trap: suppression and repression both keep material out of mind, but only suppression is deliberate; George Vaillant even ranks suppression among the mature, healthy defences.", "traps": {"a": "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate decision to push a worry out of mind, such as choosing not to think about exam results until tomorrow; here the forgetting happened without any conscious effort.", "c": "Denial is refusing to accept an external reality that is too painful, such as insisting a dead relative is still alive; it concerns present outer facts, not an inner memory pushed out of awareness.", "d": "Displacement redirects an impulse from a threatening target to a safer one, such as shouting at a younger brother after being scolded by the boss; nothing is forgotten in displacement."}}, {"q": "Aaron Beck's cognitive triad of depression consists of negative views of:", "concept": "The answer is c: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad consists of negative views of the self, the world and the future. Aaron T. Beck, an American psychiatrist, set out his cognitive theory of depression in Depression (1967) and built a treatment on it in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). He argued that depression is driven less by mood or unconscious anger than by how the person thinks. Early experiences lay down negative schemas, core beliefs such as \"I am unlovable\", which stress later activates. These produce automatic negative thoughts and cognitive distortions, including arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. Their content falls into three areas, the cognitive triad: the self (\"I am worthless\"), the world or present experience (\"nothing goes right for me\") and the future (\"it will never get better\"). Example: a student who fails one test thinks \"I am stupid\" (self), \"my teachers are against me\" (world) and \"I will never get a seat anywhere\" (future). Related accounts are Martin Seligman's learned helplessness and the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989). Option c is right because self, world and future are the three targets of negative thinking in Beck's model. Exam trap: the future is in the triad, but the past and present are not separate parts; \"world\" is the person's present experience. Key note: the recall list writes the triad as \"hopelessness, helplessness and worthlessness\". That is the clinical way of saying the same thing: worthless (self), helpless (world) and hopeless (future), so if those three words appear as an option, pick it.", "traps": {"a": "The body, the mind and the spirit is a holistic or spiritual triad, not Beck's; his triad is about the content of negative thinking, that is how the person sees the self, the world and the future.", "b": "The past, the present and the future is a tempting time-based triad, but Beck's triad has only one time element, the future; the other two parts are the self and the world.", "d": "Parents, peers and teachers are sources of social influence on a child, not parts of a cognitive model; Beck's triad concerns the depressed person's views of self, world and future."}}, {"q": "A detective who decided early that the butler is guilty notices only the clues that point to the butler and ignores the rest. This is:", "concept": "The answer is c: this is confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice, interpret and remember evidence that supports what we already believe, while overlooking or explaining away evidence against it. The British psychologist Peter Wason showed it with his 2-4-6 task (1960). Participants were told that the numbers 2, 4, 6 fitted a rule and had to find the rule by proposing new sets. Most guessed \"rising by two\" and tested only sets that fitted their guess, such as 8, 10, 12, so they never discovered that the real rule was simply any increasing numbers. Wason's four-card selection task (1966) showed the same failure to look for disconfirming cases. Once a hypothesis is formed, people treat agreeing facts as proof and ignore the rest, which makes beliefs, first impressions and stereotypes very resistant to change. Example: a clinician who decides in the first five minutes that a client has depression may ask only about low mood and miss signs of a thyroid problem. In the stem, the detective's early belief that the butler is guilty steers what he notices. Option c is right because he attends only to clues that confirm his existing belief and ignores those that do not. Exam trap: hindsight bias also distorts judgement, but it works after the outcome is known (\"I knew it all along\"), while confirmation bias works while evidence is still being gathered.", "traps": {"a": "The fundamental attribution error, named by Lee Ross (1977), is overestimating personality and underestimating the situation when explaining other people's behaviour; the detective's problem is selective attention to evidence, not how he explains behaviour.", "b": "Hindsight bias, studied by Baruch Fischhoff (1975), is the \"I knew it all along\" feeling after an outcome is known; the detective is still collecting clues, so there is no known outcome yet.", "d": "The self-serving bias is crediting our successes to ourselves and blaming our failures on outside causes; it protects self-esteem and is not about selecting evidence that supports a belief."}}, {"q": "Checking WhatsApp for a reply that may arrive at any unpredictable moment is reinforced on which schedule?", "concept": "The answer is b: checking WhatsApp for an unpredictable reply is reinforced on a variable interval schedule. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses are rewarded. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner described them in detail in Schedules of Reinforcement (1957). Ratio schedules reward a number of responses; interval schedules reward the first response after a period of time has passed. Each can be fixed (the same every time) or variable (changing around an average). On a variable interval schedule, the first response after an unpredictable amount of time is reinforced. Because the reward could become available at any moment, the learner keeps responding at a slow, steady rate, without the pauses seen on fixed schedules, and the behaviour is highly resistant to extinction. A fixed interval schedule, by contrast, produces a scallop: little responding just after a reward and a burst as the expected time nears. Classic variable interval examples are pop quizzes, fishing and checking email. Example: a student who expects a friend to reply some time this evening glances at the phone every few minutes; checking more often does not make the message arrive sooner, but the first check after it arrives is rewarded. Option b is right because the reply depends on unpredictable time passing, not on how many times you check. Exam trap: variable ratio sounds right because the reward is unpredictable, but on a ratio schedule more responses bring the reward sooner, as with a slot machine; here they do not.", "traps": {"a": "Fixed ratio rewards a set number of responses, such as being paid for every ten shirts sewn; it gives high rates with a pause after each reward, and checking more does not produce the reply.", "c": "Fixed interval rewards the first response after a set, predictable time, such as a weekly test; it produces a scallop of responding, and the timing of a WhatsApp reply is not predictable.", "d": "Variable ratio rewards an unpredictable number of responses, as with slot machines and lotteries; it gives the highest response rates, but checking more often does not make a reply arrive sooner."}}, {"q": "A stereotype is best defined as:", "concept": "The answer is c: a stereotype is a generalised belief about the characteristics of members of a group. The word comes from printing, where a stereotype was a fixed metal plate; the American journalist Walter Lippmann gave it its modern meaning in Public Opinion (1922), calling stereotypes the \"pictures in our heads\". Gordon Allport, in The Nature of Prejudice (1954), showed how such beliefs grow out of the normal habit of sorting people into categories. A stereotype assigns the same traits to every member of a group, whatever their individual differences. It can be positive or negative and is often shared across a culture. Social psychologists separate three linked terms with the ABC model: the stereotype is the cognitive part (a belief), prejudice is the affective part (a feeling, usually negative) and discrimination is the behavioural part (unfair action). Daniel Katz and Kenneth Braly (1933) first measured stereotypes by asking Princeton students to pick adjectives from a checklist for different ethnic groups. Later, Claude Steele and Joshua Aronson (1995) showed stereotype threat: fear of confirming a negative stereotype can lower a person's test performance. Example: \"all accountants are boring\" is a stereotype; disliking accountants is prejudice; refusing to invite one to a party is discrimination. Option c is right because a stereotype is a belief, a cognitive generalisation about a group, not a feeling or an action. Exam trap: options a and b describe discrimination and prejudice, the behavioural and affective partners of the stereotype, so match each term to its part of the ABC model.", "traps": {"a": "Unfair behaviour towards members of a group is discrimination, the behavioural part of the ABC model; a person can hold a stereotype without ever acting on it.", "b": "A negative feeling towards members of a group is prejudice, the affective part; Allport defined prejudice as an antipathy based on a faulty and inflexible generalisation, and that generalisation is the stereotype.", "d": "A personal opinion based on one direct experience is an individual impression; a stereotype is a generalised, usually socially shared belief about a whole group, often held with no direct contact at all."}}, {"q": "The book 'Caste, Prejudice and the Individual' (1970), a classic of Indian social psychology, was written by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the book was written by Anand C. Paranjpe. Caste, Prejudice and the Individual was published by Lalvani in Bombay in 1970. Prejudice is a negative attitude towards people because of the group they belong to. Much classic Western work, such as Gordon Allport's The Nature of Prejudice (1954), studied it through race and religion. Paranjpe turned to caste, India's own deep-rooted system of social grouping, and studied caste attitudes among Indian students. As the title suggests, he looked at prejudice at the level of the individual person, not only at society as a whole. This made the book an early classic of Indian social psychology. Paranjpe, who later taught at Simon Fraser University in Canada, went on to bridge Western and Indian thought in Theoretical Psychology: The Meeting of East and West (1984) and Self and Identity in Modern Psychology and Indian Thought (1998). He also co-edited the Handbook of Indian Psychology (2008) with K. Ramakrishna Rao and Ajit K. Dalal. Example of caste prejudice: a student who feels uneasy sharing a room or a meal with a classmate from another caste, though the two have never quarrelled, holds an attitude based on group membership, not on personal experience. Option c is right because Paranjpe is the author of this 1970 study. Exam trap: all four options are leading Indian psychologists, so link each name to one landmark: Durganand Sinha with indigenisation, Udai Pareek with organisational roles and OCTAPACE, and J. B. P. Sinha with the nurturant-task leader.", "traps": {"a": "Durganand Sinha led the call to indigenise psychology in India, as in Psychology in a Third World Country: The Indian Experience (1986); he did not write this book on caste prejudice.", "b": "Udai Pareek was a leading Indian organisational psychologist, known for role theory, role stress and the OCTAPACE profile of organisational culture; his work was on organisations, not on caste prejudice.", "d": "Jai B. P. Sinha is known for the nurturant-task leader, a leadership style suited to Indian work culture that combines care for subordinates with a firm focus on the task; he did not write this book."}}, {"q": "Caffeine is classified as a ______ and works mainly by ______.", "concept": "The answer is d: caffeine is a stimulant that works mainly by blocking adenosine receptors. Caffeine, found in coffee, tea, cola and chocolate, is the most widely used psychoactive drug in the world; chemically it is a methylxanthine. To understand it, start with adenosine. Adenosine is a chemical messenger that builds up in the brain the longer we stay awake. When it binds to its receptors, mainly the A1 and A2A types, it dampens neural activity and creates the pressure to sleep. Caffeine has a similar shape, so it fits those receptors without activating them. It is an antagonist: it blocks adenosine and removes the brake, so alertness rises and fatigue falls. Because adenosine normally restrains dopamine and other transmitters, these become more active too, which explains caffeine's mild lift in mood. Regular users develop tolerance, and stopping suddenly brings withdrawal. DSM-5 (2013) made caffeine withdrawal an official diagnosis, and DSM-5-TR lists both caffeine intoxication (restlessness, insomnia, racing heart after large doses) and caffeine withdrawal, whose hallmark is headache, with tiredness and poor concentration. Example: a student who drinks three cups of coffee a day during exam season feels alert while studying but gets a pounding headache on the first coffee-free Sunday. Option d is right because caffeine is a central nervous system stimulant whose main action is adenosine receptor blockade. Exam trap: caffeine does raise dopamine activity, but only indirectly; blocking dopamine reuptake is cocaine's mechanism, and releasing dopamine is amphetamine's.", "traps": {"a": "Opioids such as morphine and heroin act on mu opioid receptors to relieve pain and produce euphoria and drowsiness; caffeine has no opioid action and causes alertness, not sedation.", "b": "Depressants such as alcohol, benzodiazepines and barbiturates enhance GABA-A activity to calm and sedate; caffeine does the opposite, raising arousal by blocking adenosine, the brain's own brake.", "c": "Hallucinogens such as LSD and psilocybin stimulate serotonin 5-HT2A receptors and alter perception; caffeine does not cause hallucinations at ordinary doses and works through adenosine receptors."}}, {"q": "In schizophrenia, loosening of associations (derailment) is classified as a:", "concept": "The answer is a: loosening of associations is a formal thought disorder, a disorder of the form of thought. Psychopathology divides thinking problems into form and content. Form is how thoughts are linked and expressed; content is what is believed. Eugen Bleuler, the Swiss psychiatrist who coined the word schizophrenia, treated loosening of associations as the central fundamental symptom in Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias (1911); it heads the later \"four A's\": associations, affect, ambivalence and autism. In loosening, the logical links between ideas break down, so speech slides from one idea to another with no clear connection. Other formal thought disorders include derailment, tangentiality, incoherence or word salad, neologisms and poverty of content, which Nancy Andreasen's Thought, Language and Communication (TLC) scale (1979) rates. Content disorders, by contrast, are mainly delusions. Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms (1959) are a different list: audible thoughts, voices arguing or commenting, somatic passivity, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, made feelings, impulses and acts, and delusional perception. Example: asked about breakfast, a patient says, \"I had tea, tea grows in Assam, Assam is a state of mind, minds are watched by satellites\"; each step links loosely to the last, but the whole has no goal. Option a is right because loosening is a disturbance in how thoughts are connected, which defines formal thought disorder. Exam trap: thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting sound like thinking problems, but they are Schneiderian passivity experiences; loosening of associations is not a first-rank symptom.", "traps": {"b": "Schneider's first-rank symptoms are passivity experiences, special hallucinations and delusional perception, such as thought insertion or voices commenting; loosening of associations is not on Schneider's list.", "c": "Disorders of the content of thought concern what is believed, mainly delusions such as persecution or grandeur; loosening concerns how ideas are linked, not what they say.", "d": "Perceptual disturbances are hallucinations, illusions and similar changes in sensing, such as hearing voices; loosening of associations is a problem of thinking and speech, not of perception."}}, {"q": "A new depression scale is given to patients on the same day that a psychiatrist diagnoses them, and the scale scores agree closely with the diagnoses. This is evidence of the scale's:", "concept": "The answer is c: this is evidence of the scale's concurrent validity. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. One way to check is criterion-related validity: compare test scores with an outside standard, the criterion, that already reflects the trait. The American Psychological Association's Technical Recommendations for Psychological Tests and Diagnostic Techniques (1954) listed concurrent and predictive validity as separate types; later standards grouped them together as criterion-related validity. The difference is timing. In concurrent validity, the criterion is measured at about the same time as the test, so the question is whether the test can stand in for, or diagnose, the present state. In predictive validity, the criterion is measured later, so the question is whether the test forecasts the future, as when entrance scores are checked against later grades. Both are usually expressed as a validity coefficient, the correlation between test and criterion. Example: a new 15-minute anxiety checklist is given to clinic patients on the same day that clinicians rate their anxiety in full interviews; if scores and ratings correlate highly, the quick checklist has concurrent validity and could save clinical time. In the stem, the depression scale is checked against a psychiatrist's diagnosis made on the same day. Option c is right because the criterion, the psychiatrist's diagnosis, was obtained at the same time as the test scores. Exam trap: predictive validity also uses a criterion, so the deciding clue is timing: same day means concurrent, later means predictive.", "traps": {"a": "Content validity is whether the items fairly sample the whole domain, judged by experts, such as checking that a depression scale covers mood, sleep, appetite and guilt; it does not compare scores with a diagnosis.", "b": "Predictive validity compares test scores with a criterion measured later, such as entrance test scores with first-year grades; here the diagnosis was made on the same day, not in the future.", "d": "Test-retest reliability is the consistency of scores when the same test is given twice to the same people; it concerns reliability, not validity, and needs no outside criterion."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a determinant in Rorschach scoring?", "concept": "The answer is d: space is not a determinant in Rorschach scoring. The Rorschach test, published by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in Psychodiagnostik (1921), uses ten inkblot cards; the person says what each blot might be, and an inquiry then finds out where and why. Each response is scored in several separate categories. Location records which part of the blot was used: W for the whole blot, D for a common detail, Dd for an unusual detail and S for the white space. Determinants record which feature of the blot shaped the response: F for form, M for human movement, FM for animal movement, m for inanimate movement, colour (FC, CF, C), achromatic colour (C'), shading (texture T, vista V, diffuse Y), form dimension (FD) and pairs or reflections. Content records what was seen (human, animal, anatomy), and further codes cover popular responses and form quality. John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) brought these codes together into the standard scoring method. Example: \"two people dancing\" seen in the whole card is scored W for location and M for determinant; \"a lake\" seen in the white centre is scored S for location, with F if its outline decided the answer. Option d is right because space (S) tells us where on the card the person looked, which makes it a location code, not a determinant. Exam trap: S looks like just another scoring letter next to F, M and FM, but it belongs to location; keep location, determinants and content as three separate questions.", "traps": {"a": "Human movement (M) is a core determinant: the person sees humans, or human-like figures, in action, such as \"two people dancing\"; it is often read as a sign of imagination and inner life.", "b": "Form (F) is the most basic determinant, used when the outline or shape alone decides the response, such as \"a bat\" because of the wing shape; it is usually read as a sign of control.", "c": "Animal movement (FM) is a determinant for animals seen in action, such as \"two bears climbing\"; it is usually linked to more basic needs and impulses than human movement."}}, {"q": "Which method is used for memorising a poem?", "concept": "The answer is a: distributed practice is the method used for memorising a poem. Distributed (spaced) practice means spreading study over several shorter sessions with breaks in between; massed practice means doing it all in one long sitting, as in cramming. Hermann Ebbinghaus, the German pioneer who tested his own memory with nonsense syllables, reported in Memory (1885) that learning spread over days needed fewer total repetitions than learning crammed into one session. This is the spacing effect, one of the most reliable findings in memory research. Spacing works partly because each new session makes the learner retrieve material that is starting to fade, which strengthens it, and partly because fatigue and boredom are reduced. Textbooks also discuss whole versus part learning. In the whole method the learner reads the entire poem through each time; in the part method it is learnt verse by verse; in the progressive part method each new verse is added to those already learnt. The whole method suits a short, meaningful poem and the part method a long one. Example: a student who reads a poem for 15 minutes a day for four days will usually remember it longer than one who reads it for an hour the night before. Option a is right because distributed practice is the evidence-based method among the four choices. Exam trap: \"none of the above\" tempts students who expect whole or part learning, but distributed practice is a genuine method. Key note: the options are as the recall list remembers them; \"serial memorising\" and \"syntactic memorising\" are not standard method names, so distributed practice is the safe pick.", "traps": {"b": "\"Serial memorising\" is not a standard method name; serial learning is a laboratory procedure in which items are learnt in a fixed order, used since Ebbinghaus to measure memory, not a recommended way to study.", "c": "\"Syntactic memorising\" is not a recognised method in memory research; syntax is the grammatical structure of sentences, a topic in language, not a study technique for poems.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because distributed practice is a genuine, well-supported method; spacing study sessions improves long-term memory for a poem compared with cramming it in one sitting."}}, {"q": "Watching a disturbing film alone, Japanese and American students showed the same facial expressions of disgust; when an experimenter sat with them, the Japanese students masked their expressions with polite smiles. This finding illustrates:", "concept": "The answer is d: the finding illustrates cultural display rules. Display rules are learned social norms that tell members of a culture when, where and how strongly an emotion may be shown. Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen introduced the term in 1969. Their neurocultural theory holds that a set of basic facial expressions is universal and biologically wired, while display rules, which are cultural, govern how those expressions are managed in public. Ekman's six basic emotions are happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust. The study in the stem was run by Wallace Friesen (1972) and reported by Paul Ekman (1972). Japanese and American students watched stressful film clips. When they watched alone, both groups made the same expressions of disgust and fear. When an experimenter sat with them, the Japanese students more often masked negative expressions with polite smiles. Japan is a collectivist culture, and as Hazel Markus and Shinobu Kitayama (1991) argued, such cultures stress group harmony, so showing negative feelings in front of others, especially a higher-status person, is discouraged. Example: an Indian bride may be expected to look sad when leaving her parents' home, even if she feels happy; the display rule shapes the face, not the feeling. Option d is right because the same inner emotion produced different public expressions depending on the social setting, which is exactly what display rules predict. Exam trap: the study shows both universality and cultural shaping, so do not pick \"entirely learned\" just because a cultural difference appeared.", "traps": {"a": "The James-Lange theory, from William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), holds that bodily reactions come first and our perception of them is the emotion; it says nothing about cultural rules for showing feelings.", "b": "Facial expressions being entirely learned is contradicted by the study itself: alone, Japanese and American students showed the same disgust expressions, supporting Ekman's view that basic expressions are universal.", "c": "The facial feedback hypothesis says that making an expression changes the felt emotion, as in Fritz Strack and colleagues' (1988) pen study; the stem is about hiding emotions in company, not faces changing feelings."}}, {"q": "A man vanishes from his home town and is found weeks later working in another city under a new name, with no memory of his past life. In DSM-5-TR this is diagnosed as:", "concept": "The answer is b: in DSM-5-TR this is diagnosed as dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue. Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information, usually about traumatic or stressful events, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not caused by a drug or brain disease. A fugue (from the Latin for \"flight\") adds apparently purposeful travel or bewildered wandering linked to the amnesia; sometimes the person takes on a new identity. DSM-IV (1994) listed dissociative fugue as a separate disorder, but DSM-5 (2013) folded it into dissociative amnesia as a specifier, and DSM-5-TR (2022) keeps that. ICD-11 likewise codes dissociative amnesia (6B61) with a \"with dissociative fugue\" form. A fugue usually follows severe stress such as war, disaster, debt or marital crisis. It may last hours to months; recovery is often sudden, and the person then remembers the past life but has amnesia for the fugue period. The classic case is Ansel Bourne, an American preacher who in 1887 vanished from Rhode Island and lived for about two months as a shopkeeper called A. J. Brown in Pennsylvania; William James later studied him. Option b is right because the man has travelled away, lost his personal past and taken a new name, which is dissociative amnesia with fugue. Exam trap: students who learnt DSM-IV look for \"dissociative fugue\" as its own disorder, and others pick dissociative identity disorder because of the new name; in a fugue the new identity replaces the old one rather than alternating with it.", "traps": {"a": "Depersonalisation-derealisation disorder involves feeling detached from oneself or one's surroundings, as if in a dream, while memory and reality testing stay intact; there is no travel or loss of identity.", "c": "Dissociative identity disorder, formerly multiple personality disorder, involves two or more distinct identities that alternate in control, with recurrent amnesic gaps; a fugue usually shows one new identity replacing the old.", "d": "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) presents neurological-type symptoms such as paralysis, blindness or non-epileptic seizures without a medical cause; it does not involve losing one's identity or travelling."}}, {"q": "Social identity theory was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: social identity theory was proposed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner. Henri Tajfel, a Polish-born British social psychologist, and his former student John Turner set out the theory in \"An integrative theory of intergroup conflict\" (1979). Its central idea is that the self-concept has two parts: a personal identity, built from individual traits, and a social identity, built from the groups we belong to, such as nation, religion, caste, college or team. Because people want to feel good about themselves, they want their groups to look good too. They therefore seek positive distinctiveness: they compare their in-group with out-groups on dimensions where the in-group comes out ahead. This produces in-group favouritism and at times prejudice, even when the groups are not competing for anything real. The theory grew out of Tajfel's minimal group experiments (1971), which showed that simply being placed in a group was enough to make people favour it. Turner and colleagues later extended it into self-categorisation theory (1987). Example: after India wins a cricket match, a fan says \"we won\", feels proud and may mock the rival team's supporters; part of his self-esteem rides on the group. Option a is right because Tajfel and Turner jointly authored social identity theory. Exam trap: Muzafer Sherif also explained intergroup conflict, but through competition for scarce resources (realistic conflict theory); Tajfel and Turner showed that group membership alone can produce bias.", "traps": {"b": "Leon Festinger proposed social comparison theory (1954) and cognitive dissonance theory (1957); social identity theory borrows the idea of comparison and applies it to groups, but it was Tajfel and Turner's.", "c": "Muzafer Sherif ran the Robbers Cave experiment (1954) and developed realistic conflict theory, which explains hostility through competition for scarce resources rather than through group membership alone.", "d": "Fritz Heider proposed balance theory and laid the foundations of attribution theory in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958); he did not propose social identity theory."}}, {"q": "In Henri Tajfel's 'minimal group' experiments, schoolboys split into groups on a trivial basis (preferring paintings by Klee or Kandinsky) then, when sharing out rewards:", "concept": "The answer is c: the boys favoured their own group, even giving up a larger total so that their group got more than the other. Henri Tajfel, Michael Billig, R. P. Bundy and Claude Flament (1971) devised the minimal group paradigm to find the least that is needed to produce in-group bias. Bristol schoolboys aged 14 and 15 were split into groups on trivial grounds; in one version, by whether they preferred paintings by Paul Klee or Wassily Kandinsky. The groups were \"minimal\": members never met, did not know who else was in their group, and had no history of conflict and nothing to compete for. Each boy then shared out rewards between two other boys, known only by code number and group, using reward matrices. He never allocated to himself, so self-interest was ruled out. Fairness pulled choices towards the middle, but there was a clear, consistent tilt towards the in-group. Most striking was the maximum difference strategy: boys often accepted less for their own group as long as it beat the out-group. Example: offered 13 points for an in-group boy and 13 for an out-group boy, or 9 and 5, a boy using maximum difference takes 9 and 5. Mere categorisation was enough for discrimination, so real conflict over resources, as in Muzafer Sherif's theory, is not necessary. Option c is right because the boys valued beating the out-group above maximising their own group's reward. Exam trap: option b tempts because fairness played a part, but the key result was in-group favouritism.", "traps": {"a": "Favouring the other group to appear generous is the opposite of what happened; despite having no contact or conflict, the boys consistently tilted rewards towards their own group, not the out-group.", "b": "Fairness did influence choices, but the boys did not simply split rewards equally; they reliably deviated towards their own group, and that deviation is the minimal group effect.", "d": "The boys did allocate rewards, even though every recipient was anonymous and known only by a code number and group label; anonymity made the in-group bias more striking, not absent."}}, {"q": "According to social identity theory, the three mental processes through which a person comes to see himself or herself as a group member occur in which order?", "concept": "The answer is b: the order is social categorisation, social identification, social comparison. Social identity theory, proposed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979), explains how belonging to groups becomes part of who we are. It describes three mental processes that follow one another. First comes social categorisation: we sort people, including ourselves, into social categories such as Hindu or Muslim, student or teacher, Mumbai Indians fan or Chennai Super Kings fan. Categories simplify the social world and tell us which group we belong to. Second comes social identification: we take on the identity of the group we have placed ourselves in, adopt its norms and feel emotionally tied to it, so our self-esteem becomes bound up with the group's standing. Third comes social comparison: to keep that self-esteem high, we compare our in-group with out-groups in ways that favour our own, seeking positive distinctiveness. This last step is where in-group favouritism, rivalry and prejudice can arise. Turner and colleagues later developed the categorisation step into self-categorisation theory (1987). Example: a new student at a medical college notices that classmates are either hostel residents or day scholars (categorisation), joins the hostel and starts saying \"we hostellers\" (identification), and finally claims hostellers work harder than day scholars (comparison). Option b is right because a person must first categorise, then identify with a group, and only then compare it with others. Exam trap: social comparison is a familiar term from Leon Festinger (1954), so students may put it first, but in this theory it comes last.", "traps": {"a": "Social comparison first cannot work: a person cannot compare \"us\" with \"them\" before sorting people into groups and identifying with one; comparison is the last of the three steps.", "c": "Social identification first is out of order: you cannot identify with a group until you have categorised people and placed yourself in a category, and comparison comes last, not in the middle.", "d": "Social identification before categorisation reverses the first two steps; categorisation must come first, so although comparison is correctly placed last, the sequence as a whole is wrong."}}, {"q": "In environmental psychology, anthropocentrism refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: anthropocentrism is the view that human beings are the most important beings, with nature valued mainly for its use to humans. The word comes from the Greek anthropos, \"human being\", and means human-centred. Environmental psychology studies how people and their physical surroundings affect each other, including why people do or do not protect nature. Suzanne Thompson and Michelle Barton (1994) distinguished two reasons for caring about the environment. Anthropocentric people support conservation because nature serves human interests: clean air and water, food, medicines, comfort and enjoyment. Ecocentric people value nature for its own sake, believing that plants, animals and ecosystems deserve protection whether or not humans benefit. Both groups may back the same green action, but for different reasons. Thompson and Barton also measured a third outlook, environmental apathy, a lack of interest in environmental issues. Example: two villagers oppose cutting down a forest. One says, \"We need it for firewood, rain and tourism\", which is anthropocentric. The other says, \"The forest and its tigers have a right to exist\", which is ecocentric. Option b is right because anthropocentrism places humans at the centre and treats nature as valuable mainly for what it gives people. Exam trap: anthropocentrism and anthropomorphism share the Greek root anthropos, but anthropomorphism means giving human traits to animals or objects, which is a different idea.", "traps": {"a": "The scientific study of human cultures and societies is anthropology; it shares the Greek root anthropos, \"human\", but it is an academic discipline, not an attitude about humans' place in nature.", "c": "Giving animals or objects human-like thoughts and feelings is anthropomorphism, such as saying a car is angry; it projects human traits outward rather than ranking humans above nature.", "d": "The belief that one's own culture is superior is ethnocentrism, a term popularised by William Graham Sumner in Folkways (1906); it is about cultural groups, not humans versus nature."}}]});
